Page 111 datum: -------- begin text --------
{{also|dátum|Datum}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*deh₃-}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|datum||a [[given]]}}. {{doublet|en|die}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdeɪtəm/|/ˈdætəm/|/ˈdɑːtəm/}} (''see {{l|en|data}} for regional distribution'')
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vininn126-datum.wav|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪtəm|ætəm|ɑːtəm|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{wikipedia|data|mul=datum reference}}
{{en-noun|data|s|pl1qual=senses 1–3|pl2qual=senses 1 and 4–5}}

# {{lb|en|dated}} {{singular of|en|data|cap=1}}; a single recorded [[observation]].
# {{lb|en|philosophy}} A fact known from direct observation.
# {{lb|en|philosophy}} A premise from which conclusions are drawn.
# {{lb|en|cartography|surveying|engineering}} A fixed [[reference point]] or set of reference points which precisely define a system of [[measurement]] or a [[coordinate system]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2000|publisher=International Boundaries Research Unit, University of Durham|author=Nuno Sergio Marques Antunes|journal=Maritime Briefing|volume=2|number=7|title=The Importance of the Tidal Datum in the Definition of Maritime Limits and Boundaries|page=5|text=In a strict sense, a ''tidal '''datum''''' can be understood as the reference plane (or surface) to which the height of a predicted tide is referred.{{...}}Sounding and chart '''datums''' are low water '''datums''', that is, they refer to the level of the water surface at low tide. Nonetheless, there are also '''datums''' based on high water levels.{{...}}These two different '''datums''' may be included in the broader category of ''vertical '''datums''''', which comprises any plane or surface used as a reference to measure vertical distances (such as depths, drying features, heights on shore, etc.).}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Roger F Tomlinson|title=Thinking about GIS: geographic information system planning for managers|passage='''Datums''' are another important map aspect related to projection. A '''datum''' provides a base reference for measuring locations on Earth's surface.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Yong-Qi Chen; Yuk-Cheung Lee|title=Geographical Data Acquisition|page=|chapter=2.3|text=For horizontal measurements [on the Earth], we fix a mathematical body of Earth in space using a Cartesian coordinate system. After that, a separate coordinate system is created over the surface of this body to generate horizontal coordinates. A mathematical earth body fixed in space makes up the ''horizontal '''datum'''''.}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} A floating reference point, or [[SLDMB]], used to evaluate surface currents in a body of water. Often employed by coastal search and rescue.

====Derived terms====
{{der3|en|datum circle|datum line|datum plane|datum point|datum surface|chart datum}}

====Translations====
{{trans-top|single piece of information}}
* Bulgarian: {{t|bg|данна|f}}, {{t|bg|изходна величина}}
* Catalan: {{t+|ca|dada|f}}
* Czech: {{t+|cs|údaj|m}}
* Finnish: {{t+|fi|tieto}}
* French: {{t+|fr|donnée}}
* German: {{t+|de|Fakt|m|n}}, {{t+|de|Faktum|n}}, {{t+|de|Informationseinheit|f}}
* Greek: {{t+|el|δεδομένο|n}}
* Ido: {{t+|io|datumo}}, {{t+|io|donajo}}, {{t+|io|donatajo}}
* Irish: {{t|ga|sonra|m}}
* Italian: {{t+|it|dato|m}}
* Japanese: {{t+|ja|データ|tr=dēta}}
* Polish: {{t+|pl|dana|f}}
* Portuguese: {{t+|pt|dado|m}}
