Page 488 Besiana: -------- begin text --------
==Albanian==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|sq|Besianë}}

===Etymology===
A feminized personal name based on the {{m|sq|besë}}.

It is considered an ancient Albanian name as it is documented in [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Procopius Procopius] list of restored cities that were rebuilt in Dardania, close to Petrizen, in his 560 A. D. work ''of the buildings of Justinian''.<ref>''Palestine Pilgrims' Text Society. – Of the buildings of Justinian. By Procopius (Circ. 560 A.D.). Translated by Aubrey Stewart and annotated by Col. Sir C. W. Wilson and Prof. Hayter Lewis.'' London, 1888, [https://archive.org/details/cu31924028534224/page/n129/mode/2up?q=Dardania p. 103].</ref>

===Proper noun===
{{sq-noun|g=f}}

# {{given name|sq|female}}
# {{rfv-sense|sq|)Definition sounds like a common name, maybe a literal translation of the meaning?)}} A woman of honour
# {{rfv-sense|sq|(Procopius wrote in Greek; linked above is an English translation)}} {{place|sq|city|c/Kosovo}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|sq|besë}}
* {{l|sq|Besa}}
* {{l|sq|Besart}}
* {{l|sq|Besarta}}

====References====
<references/>

==Latin==
{{rfv|la|Procopius was a Greek author.}}

===Etymology===
Placename near {{m|la|Dardapara}}, documented by Procopius in 560 A. D.<ref>''Palestine Pilgrims' Text Society. – Of the buildings of Justinian. By Procopius (Circ. 560 A.D.). Translated by Aubrey Stewart and annotated by Col. Sir C. W. Wilson and Prof. Hayter Lewis.'' London, 1888, [https://archive.org/details/cu31924028534224/page/n129/mode/2up?q=Dardania p. 103].</ref> Borrowed from archaic {{m|sq|Besiana}}, ultimatily a formation of {{bor|la|sqj-pro|*baitši}}.

===Proper noun===
{{la-proper noun|Besiana<2.loc>}}

# A city close to {{m|la|Petrizen}} and {{m|la|Dardapara}}, current [[Besiana]] or [[Podujeva]].

====Declension====
{{la-ndecl|Besiana<2.loc>}}

===References===
<references/>

