===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To fail to keep up (the pace), to fall behind.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|canto=I|passage=Behind her farre away a Dwarfe did '''lag''', / That lasie seemd in being ever last, / Or wearied with bearing of her bag / Of needments at his backe.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1616|author=George Chapman|title=The Odysseys of Homer|passage=Lazy beast! / Why last art thou now? Thou hast never used / To '''lag''' thus hindmost}}
#* '''1717''', ''The Metamorphoses of Ovid'' translated into English verse under the direction of Sir Samuel Garth by John Dryden, Alexander Pope, Joseph Addison, William Congreve and other eminent hands
#*: While he, whose tardy feet had '''lagg'd''' behind, / Was doom'd the sad reward of death to find.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1798|author=w:Samuel Taylor Coleridge|title=The Rime of the Ancient Mariner in seven parts|passage=Brown skeletons of leaves that '''lag''' / My forest-brook along}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=5 April 2004|journal=The New Yorker,
|passage=Over the next fifty years, by most indicators dear to economists, the country remained the richest in the world. But by another set of numbers—longevity and income inequality—it began to '''lag''' behind Northern Europe and Japan.}}
# To cover (for example, pipes) with felt strips or similar material {{gloss|[[W:Building insulation#The history of thermal insulation|referring to a time lag effect in thermal transfer]]}}.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1941|month=April|title=British Locomotive Developments|journal=Railway Magazine|page=173|text=Spun glass mattresses are used for '''lagging''' the boiler, which has three Ross pop safety valves on the front ring.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=c. 1974|author=w:Philip Larkin|journal=The Building
|passage=Outside seems old enough: / Red brick, '''lagged''' pipes, and someone walking by it / Out to the car park, free.}}
# {{lb|en|computing|informal|video games}} To [[respond]] slowly.
#: {{ux|en|My phone is starting to '''lag'''.}}
# {{lb|en|UK|slang|archaic}} To [[transport]] as a punishment for [[crime]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1847|author=w:Thomas De Quincey|title=Secret Societies"
|passage=She '''lags''' us if we poach.}}
# {{lb|en|UK|slang|archaic}} To [[arrest]] or [[apprehend]].
#* {{RQ:Doyle Land of Mist|passage="We must get the old dear out," said Lord Roxton to Malone. "He'll be had for manslaughter if we don't. What I mean, he's not responsible - he'll sock someone and be '''lagged''' for it."}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause to lag; to slacken.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1632|author=w:Thomas Heywood|title=The Iron Age
|passage=The weight would '''lagge''' thee that art wont to flye.}}

