Page 523 lug: -------- begin text --------
{{also|lúg|ług|Lug|LUG}}
==Translingual==

===Symbol===
{{mul-symbol}}

# {{ISO 639|2&3|lg|Luganda}}

==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP|GA|Canada}} {{enPR|lŭg}}, {{IPA|en|/lʌɡ/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-lug.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌɡ|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|luggen}}, possibly from Scandinavian source, (compare {{cog|sv|lugga}}, {{cog|no|lugge}}); also in English dialectal as {{m|en|lig|t=to lug}}. Noun is via {{der|en|sco|lugge}}, probably from {{der|en|non|-}} (compare {{cog|no|-}} and {{cog|sv|lugg}}). Probably related to {{m|en|slug|t=lazy, slow-moving}}, which may be from similar source(s). See {{m|en|slow}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# The act of hauling or dragging.
#: {{ux|en|a hard '''lug'''}}
# That which is hauled or dragged.
#: {{ux|en|The pack is a heavy '''lug'''.}}
# Anything that moves slowly.
#* {{RQ:Ascham Toxophilus|passage=whereof the one is quick of cast, trick, and trim both for pleasure and profit: the other is a '''lug'''}}
# A [[lug nut]].
# {{lb|en|electricity}} A device for terminating an electrical conductor to facilitate the mechanical connection; to the conductor it may be crimped to form a cold weld, soldered or have pressure from a screw.
# A part of something which [[stick out|sticks out]], used as a [[handle]] or [[support]].
# A large, clumsy, awkward man; a [[fool]].
#: {{syn|en|big lug}}
# {{lb|en|UK}} An [[ear]] or [[ear lobe]].
#: {{ux|en|While shaving, the poor sod had a fit and cut part of a '''lug''' off.}}
# A [[wood]] [[box]] used for transporting [[fruit]] or [[vegetable]]s.
# {{lb|en|slang}} A [[request]] for [[money]], as for [[political]] [[purposes]].
#: {{ux|en|They put the '''lug''' on him at the courthouse.}}
# A [[lugworm]].
# {{lb|en|informal}} A [[pull]] or [[drag]] on a [[cigarette]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2013|author=Paul Burke|title=The Man Who Fell In Love With His Wife
|passage=He took another long '''lug''' on his cigarette before continuing quietly, 'I've seen too much and it was seriously screwing me up. {{...}}}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{sense|protruding support}} {{l|en|launch lug}}
* {{l|en|lug bolt}}

=====Translations=====
{{trans-top|lug nut}}
* Bulgarian: {{t|bg|карабина на теглич|f}}
* Galician: {{t+|gl|trela|f}}, {{t+|gl|braga|f}}, {{t+|gl|targa|f}}
* Swedish: {{t|sv|hjulskruv|c}}, {{t|sv|hjulmutter|c}}
{{trans-bottom}}

{{trans-top|device for terminating an electrical conductor}}
* Bulgarian: {{t+|bg|кабелна обувка|f}}
* French: {{t+|fr|cosse|f}}
{{trans-bottom}}

{{trans-top|protrusion used as a handle or point of attachment}}
* French: {{t+|fr|oreille|f}}, {{t+|fr|tenon|m}}
* Maori: {{t|mi|taupopoia}}
{{trans-bottom}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|sometimes|figurative}} To [[haul]] or [[drag]] along (especially something heavy); to [[carry]]; to [[pull]].
#: {{ux|en|Why do you always '''lug''' around so many books?}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=c. 1700|author=w:Jeremy Collier|title=A Thought
|passage=They must divide the image among them, and so '''lug''' off every one his share.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1923|author=w:P. G. Wodehouse|title=The Inimitable Jeeves
|passage=As a rule, you see, I'm not '''lugged''' into Family [[row|Rows]]. On the occasions when Aunt is calling to Aunt like [[mastodon]]s bellowing across primeval swamps and Uncle James's letter about Cousin Mabel's peculiar behaviour is being shot round the family circle... the clan has a tendency to ignore me.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2021 July 14|author=Anthony Lambert|title=Grand designs on superior interiors|journal=RAIL|issue=935|page=48|text=Luggage areas need to be within sight, rather than at the end of carriages, despite the inconvenience of '''lugging''' cases further into a carriage.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[run]] at too [[slow]] a [[speed]].
#: {{ux|en|When driving up a hill, choose a lower gear so you don't '''lug''' the engine.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|nautical}} To carry an [[excessive]] amount of [[sail]] for the [[condition]]s [[prevailing]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive|horse-racing}} To pull toward the inside rail ("lugging in") or the outside rail ("lugging out") during a race.

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|luggage}}

=====Translations=====
{{trans-top|to haul something heavy}}
* Bulgarian: {{t+|bg|дърпам}}, {{t+|bg|влача}}
* Cebuano: {{t|ceb|baguod}}
* Finnish: {{t+|fi|kiskoa}}
* French: {{t+|fr|trimbaler}}, {{t+|fr|traîner}}
* Galician: {{t+|gl|ransir}}, {{t+|gl|restexar}}, {{t+|gl|acorzoar}}, {{t+|gl|arrousar}}
* Navajo: {{t|nv|yiłjiid|sc=Latn}}
* Occitan: {{t|oc|tirassar}}
* Polish: {{t|pl|zataskać|impf}}, {{t|pl|zataszczyć|pf}}
* Swedish: {{t+|sv|kånka}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|lugge|t=pole, stick, staff}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|UK|dialect}} A [[rod]] or [[pole]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1567|author= George Turberville|title=Epitome|passage=And from the bodies [of pines and oaks] the boughes and loftie '''lugges''' they beare. }}
# {{lb|en|UK|archaic|dialect}} A [[measure]] of [[length]] equal to {{frac|16|1|2}} [[foot|feet]].
#: {{syn|en|rod}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=II|canto=X|stanza=11|passage=eight '''lugs''' of grownd; / Into the which returning backe, he fell}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} A [[lugsail]].
# {{lb|en|harness}} The [[leather]] [[loop]] or [[ear]] by which a [[shaft]] is held up.[[File:Medieval harness pendant suspension mount (FindID 604459).jpg|thumb|right|Harness pendant suspension mount featuring two lugs (at the bottom). The pendant has one lug (also named loop), placed in the gap between the two lugs of the hanger.]]
# A [[loop]] (or [[protuberance]]) found on both [[arm]]s of a [[hinge]], featuring a [[hole]] for the [[axis]] of the hinge.
# A [[ridge]] or other [[protuberance]] on the surface of a body to increase [[traction]] or provide a [[hold]] for holding and moving it.

