==Romanian==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|ro|[va]}}

===Etymology 1===
Probably from the third-person singular present indicative of {{m|ro|vrea||to want}}, used in its special conjugation as an auxiliary verb (cf. the first-person {{m|ro|voi||(I) will}}, supposedly from Vulgar Latin {{m|la|voleo}}). An alternative etymology is that it began originally as the now rare word in etymology 2 below, from forms of Latin {{m|la|vādere||to go}}, and was confused with conjugated forms of {{m|ro|voi}} / {{m|ro|vrea}} in Romanian; compare {{m|ro|voi}} {{m|ro|cânta}} ("I will sing") to the constructions in French {{m|fr|je}} {{m|fr|vais}} {{m|fr|chanter}} and Spanish {{m|es|voy}} {{m|es|a}} {{m|es|cantar}} with the same meaning (literally, "I go to sing").<ref>http://www.dex.ro/va</ref>

====Verb====
{{ro-aux|vrea|person=3s|tense=f}}

# (he/she) [[will]]
#: {{ux|ro|'''va''' [[fi]] [[aici]] [[mai]] [[târziu]]?|will he/she be here later?}}

===Etymology 2===
{{inh+|ro|la|vādit}}, third person singular present active indicative of {{m|la|vādō}}. It is also rarely used as a second person singular imperative form, meaning "go", from Latin {{m|la|vāde}} (and plural form {{m|ro|vați}} from {{m|la|vāditis}}). 16th century Transylvanian documents also display respective variant forms {{m|ro||vă}} and {{m|ro||vareți}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alt|ro|vă||rare}}

====Verb====
{{head|ro|verb}}

# (to [[go]]), only used in ''mai va'' (see usage notes).
# {{lb|ro|regional|Crișana}} [[go]] (imperative) (''plural'' {{l|ro|vați}})

=====Usage notes=====
The conjugation for this verb is defective, with the only remaining form being ''va'', used in the expression "''[[mai]] [[va]]''", meaning "it will take longer or there is more to go (until then)".

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|to go}} {{l|ro|merge}}, {{l|ro|duce}}
* {{sense|go}} {{l|ro|[[du]]-[[te]]}}, {{l|ro|[[duceți]]-[[vă]]}}

