Page 562 Θῖνα: -------- begin text --------
{{also|θῖνα}}
==Ancient Greek==
{{wikipedia|Names of China}}

===Etymology===
{{unc|grc}}, but probably from {{der|grc|sa|^चीन||[[China]]}}, possibly via {{der|grc|ar|^صِين|t=[[China]]; the [[Chinese]]}} and usually held to derive from {{der|grc|och|^秦||[[Qin]]}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{grc-IPA|period=koi1}}

===Proper noun===
{{head|grc|proper noun|g=f|accusative||genitive|Θῑνός}}

# {{lb|grc|culture}} {{alt form|grc|Θῖναι}}: a [[people]] of [[East Asia]] [[usually]] [[identified]] as the [[southern]] [[Chinese]]; [[their]] [[homeland]], [[China]].
#* {{circa|{{AD}} 50}}, ''{{w|Periplus of the Erythraean Sea}}'', paragraph 64:
#*: {{quote|grc|Μετὰ δὲ ταύτην τὴν χώραν ὑπ’ αὐτὸν ἤδη τὸν βορέαν, ἔξωθεν εἰς '''Θινός''' τινα τόπον ἀποληγούσης τῆς θαλάσσης, παράκειται ἐν αὐτῇ πόλις μεσόγειος μεγίστη, λεγομένη Θῖναι, ἀφ ἧς τό τε ἔριον καὶ τὸ νῆμα καὶ τὸ ὀθόνιον τὸ Σηρικὸν εἰς τὰ Βαρύγαζα διὰ Βάκτρων πεζῇ φέρεται καὶ εἰς τὴν Λιμυρικὴν πάλιν διὰ τοῦ Γάγγου ποταμοῦ. Εἰς δὲ τὴν '''Θῖνα''' ταύτην οὐκ ἔστιν εὐχερῶς ἀπελθεῖν· σπανίως γὰρ ἀπ’ αὐτῆς τινὲς οὐ πολλοὶ ἔρχονται.|After this region under the very north, the sea outside ending in a land called '''This''', there is a very great inland city called Thinae, from which raw silk and silk yarn and silk cloth are brought on foot through Bactria to Barygaza, and are also exported to Damirica by way of the river Ganges. But the land of '''This''' is not easy of access; few men come from there, and seldom. ([[s:Periplus of the Erythraean Sea#64|translated]] by {{w|Wilfred Harvey Schoff}})}}
#* '''1848''', [[w:Samuel Wells Williams|Samuel Wells Williams]], [https://books.google.com.hk/books?id=N6osBgAAQBAJ&printsec=frontcover ''The Middle Kingdom'', Vol. II], [https://books.google.com.hk/books?id=N6osBgAAQBAJ&pg=PA408 p. 408]:
#*: {{quote|grc|The [[w:Periplus of the Erythraean Sea|Periplus of the Erythræan Sea]], however, refers to the same land [''[[sc.]]'', China] under the name {{lang|grc|Θὶν}}, or ''Thin'', at perhaps an earlier date.}}

====Declension====
Attested only in the accusative and genitive singular. It is unknown whether the nominative would have been {{lang|grc|Θίν}} (as assumed by Williams in the 1848 quote above) or {{lang|grc|Θίς}} (as assumed by Schoff's translation above).
{{grc-decl|form=F-sing|Θῖν|Θῑνός|NS=-}}

====Derived terms====
* {{sense|capital}} {{l|grc|Θῖναι}}
-------- end text --------
Page 563 Θῖναι: -------- begin text --------
==Ancient Greek==
{{wikipedia|Names of China}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|grc|Θῖνα}}

===Etymology===
From {{m|grc|Θῖνα}} + {{m|grc|-αι||''fem. pl.''}}, of uncertain etymology, but probably from {{der|grc|sa|^चीन||[[China]]}}, possibly via {{der|grc|ar|^صِين|t=[[China]]; the [[Chinese]]}} and usually held to derive from {{der|grc|och|^秦||[[Qin]]}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{grc-IPA|Θῖναι}}

===Proper noun===
{{grc-proper noun|head=Θῖναι|Θῑνῶν|m-p|first}}

# {{lb|grc|culture}} {{synonym of|grc|Σῖναι|nodot=a}}: A [[people]] of [[East Asia]] usually identified as the [[southern]] [[Chinese]]; their [[homeland]], [[China]]; their chief city.
#* {{circa|{{AD}} 50}}, ''{{w|Periplus of the Erythraean Sea}}'', paragraph 64:
#*: {{quote|grc|Μετὰ δὲ ταύτην τὴν χώραν ὑπ’ αὐτὸν ἤδη τὸν βορέαν, ἔξωθεν εἰς Θινός τινα τόπον ἀποληγούσης τῆς θαλάσσης, παράκειται ἐν αὐτῇ πόλις μεσόγειος μεγίστη, λεγομένη '''Θῖναι''', ἀφ ἧς τό τε ἔριον καὶ τὸ νῆμα καὶ τὸ ὀθόνιον τὸ Σηρικὸν εἰς τὰ Βαρύγαζα διὰ Βάκτρων πεζῇ φέρεται καὶ εἰς τὴν Λιμυρικὴν πάλιν διὰ τοῦ Γάγγου ποταμοῦ. Εἰς δὲ τὴν Θῖνα ταύτην οὐκ ἔστιν εὐχερῶς ἀπελθεῖν· σπανίως γὰρ ἀπ’ αὐτῆς τινὲς οὐ πολλοὶ ἔρχονται.|After this region under the very north, the sea outside ending in a land called This, there is a very great inland city called '''Thinae''', from which raw silk and silk yarn and silk cloth are brought on foot through Bactria to Barygaza, and are also exported to Damirica by way of the river Ganges. But the land of This is not easy of access; few men come from there, and seldom. ([[s:Periplus of the Erythraean Sea#64|translated]] by {{w|Wilfred Harvey Schoff}})}}
#* '''1878''', Thomas Rawson Birks translating Franz Delitzsch's citation of Neumann in his [https://archive.org/stream/commentaryonbook00birk#page/n7/mode/2up Commentary on the Book of Isaiah], [https://archive.org/stream/commentaryonbook00birk#page/246/mode/2up page 247]:
#*: The name {{lang|grc|Θῖναι}}, {{w|Strabo}}, {{m|grc|Σῖναι|tr=-}}, [[w:Claudius Ptolemy|Ptol.]], {{m|grc|Τζίνιτζα|tr=-}}, [[w:Cosmas Indicopleustes|Kosmas]], did not obtain currency first from the founder of the dynasty [[Tsin]]; but, long before this, Tsin was the name of a feudal kingdom in [[Shensi]], one of the western provinces of the Sinese land, and {{w|Feitsa}}, the first feudal King of Tsin, began to reign as early as {{smallcaps|b.c.}}&nbsp;897.

====Inflection====
{{grc-decl|Θῑ́νη|ης|form=P}}

===Further reading===
* {{R:Smith's Geography|Thinae}}
-------- end text --------
