==German==

===Etymology===
{{dercat|de|gem-pro|ine-pro}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|-}} and {{der|de|goh|lūt}}, akin to {{cog|osx|hlūd}}, from {{der|de|gmw-pro|*hlūd}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/laʊ̯t/}}
* {{audio|de|De-laut.ogg|audio (Germany)}}
* {{audio|de|De-at-laut.ogg|audio (Austria)}}

===Adjective===
{{de-adj|comp}}

# [[loud]], [[noisy]]
#: {{syn|de|geräuschvoll}}
#: {{ant|de|leise}}

====Declension====
{{de-adecl|comp}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|de|halblaut}}

===Preposition===
{{head|de|preposition}} (+ ''dative or genitive'')

# [[according to]]
#: {{syn|de|lt.}}

====Usage notes====
The preposition ''laut'' governs either the dative or genitive case. Usage with dative has been more common since 1900:
:* '''''laut''' einem Bericht (also: eines Berichts)'' — '''according to''' a report.
The dative case is always used for plural nouns not preceded by an article, determiner, or adjective:
:* '''''laut''' Berichten (not: Berichte)'' — '''according to''' reports.
An isolated noun of the strong declension remains uninflected in the singular:
:* '''''laut''' Bericht (not: Berichts)'' — '''according to''' the report.

====Descendants====
* {{desc|bor=1|eo|laŭ|laŭta}}

===Verb===
{{head|de|verb form}}

# {{lb|de|also|_|Early New High German|rare after that period|now archaic}} {{verb form of|de|lauten||3|s|pres}}; {{alternative form of|de|lautet}}

