Page 736 accident: -------- begin text --------
{{also|Accident}}
==English==
[[File:Verkehrsunfall L261 04.JPG|right|thumb|A car after an accident (unintended event causing damage).]]
{{wikipedia|mul=accident (philosophy)}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ḱh₂d-}}
* First attested in the late 14th century. From {{inh|en|enm|-}}, from {{der|en|fro|accident}}, from {{der|en|la|accidēns}}, present active participle of {{m|la|accidō||happen}}; from {{m|la|ad|t=to}} + {{m|la|cadō|t=fall}}. See {{m|en|cadence}}, {{m|en|case}}. In the sense "unintended pregnancy", first attested in 1932.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈæk.sɪ.dənt/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈæk.sə.dənt/|/ˈæk.sə.dɛnt/}}
** {{a|pin-pen}} {{IPA|en|/ˈæk.sə.dɪnt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-accident.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|unexpected bad event}} An [[unexpected]] [[event]] with [[negative]] [[consequences]] occurring without the [[intention]] of the one [[suffering]] the consequences, and (in the strict sense) not directly caused by humans.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Othello|I|iii|text=Wherein I spake of most disastrous chances, / Of moving '''accidents''' by flood and field{{...}}}}
#: {{ux|en|to die by an '''accident''' such as an act of God}}
#: {{cot|en|act of God}}
## {{senseid|en|legal}} {{lb|en|legal}} [[casus#Latin|casus]]; such [[unforeseen]], [[extraordinary]], [[extraneous]] [[interference]] as is out of the range of ordinary [[calculation]].
# {{lb|en|transportation}} A [[collision]] or [[crash]] of a vehicle, aircraft, or other form of transportation that causes [[damage]] to the transportation involved; and sometimes [[injury]] or [[death]] to the transportation's [[occupant]]s or [[bystander]]s in close [[proximity]]. {{lb|en|but see ''[[#Usage notes|Usage notes]]''}}
#: {{ux|en|There was a huge '''accident''' on I5 involving 15 automobiles.}}
#: {{ux|en|My insurance went up after the second '''accident''' in three months.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2013|month=July-August|author=[http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/philip-j-bushnell Philip J. Bushnell]
|magazine=w:American Scientist|title=[http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2013/4/solvents-ethanol-car-crashes-amp-tolerance Solvents, Ethanol, Car Crashes & Tolerance]
|passage=Surprisingly, this analysis revealed that acute exposure to solvent vapors at concentrations below those associated with long-term effects appears to increase the risk of a fatal automobile '''accident'''. Furthermore, this increase in risk is comparable to the risk of death from leukemia after long-term exposure to benzene, another solvent, which has the well-known property of causing this type of cancer.}}
# Any chance event.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2008|author=Celia Deane-Drummond|title=The Ethics of Nature|page=206
|passage=He also objects to the idea of women arising by an '''accident''' of nature, preferring the notion that they came about as a 'result of some strong mental impression', and so 'the sex of the progeny would have been settled by the decision of the progenitor'.}}
# {{senseid|en|random chance}} {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[chance|Chance]]; random chance.
#* '''c.1861-1863''', {{w|Richard Chevenix Trench}}, in 1888, ''Letters and memorials'', Volume 1,
#*: Thou cam'st not to thy place by '''accident''', / It is the very place God meant for thee;{{...}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1991|month=Autumn|volume=60|issue=4|page=589|title=Montaigne|author=Robert M. Adams|journal=American Scholar|passage=And so with his writing, which he proudly said was a perfect counterpart of his life. '''Accident''' played a major part in both.}}
# {{senseid|en|philosophical sense, versus essence or substance}} Any property, [[fact]], or [[relation]] that is the result of chance or is [[nonessential]] or [[nonsubstantive]].
#: {{syn|en|accidens}}
#: <small>See also:</small> {{w|accident (philosophy)}}
#: {{ux|en|Beauty is an '''accident'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|Lexical gaps are called accidental because their existence is by '''accident'''; it is not essential.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1883|author=w:J. P. Mahaffy|title=Social life in Greece from Homer to Menander
|passage=This '''accident''', as I call it, of Athens being situated some miles from the sea, which is rather the consequence of its being a very ancient site,{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1902|author=William James|title=The Varieties of Religious Experience|page=171|publisher=Folio Society|year_published=2008
|passage=If they went through their growth-crisis in other faiths and other countries, although the essence of the change would be the same{{...}}, its '''accidents''' would be different.}}
#* '''14thC''', {{w|Geoffrey Chaucer}}, ''[[s:The Canterbury Tales/The Pardoner's Prologue and Tale|The Pardoner's Prologue and Tale]]'' in ''{{w|The Canterbury Tales}}'',
#*: These cookes how they stamp, and strain, and grind, / And turne substance into '''accident''', / To fulfill all thy likerous talent!
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1677|title=Heraclitus Christianus: or, the Man of Sorrow|chapter=3|page=14
|passage=But as to Man, all the Fruits of the Earth, all sorts of Herbs, Plants and Roots, the Fishes of the Sea, and the Birds of the Air do not suffice him, but he must disguise, vary, and sophisticate, change the substance into '''accident''', that by such irritations as these, Nature might be provoked, and as it were necessitated.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1989|author=Iysa A. Bello|title=The medieval Islamic controversy between philosophy and orthodoxy|page=55
|passage=Nonetheless, those who have no evidence of the impossibility of the transformation of '''accident''' into substance believe that it is death itself which will be actually transformed into a ram on the Day of Resurrection and then be slaughtered.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2005|author=Muhammad Ali Khalidi|title=Medieval Islamic philosophical writings|page=175
|passage=It would also follow that God ought to be able to transmute genera, converting substance into '''accident''', knowledge into ability, black into white, and sound into smell, just as he can turn the inanimate into animate{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2010|author=T. M. Rudavsky|title=Maimonides|page=142
|passage=nor can God effect the transmutation of substances (from '''accident''' into substance, or substance into '''accident''', or substance without '''accident''').}}
## {{lb|en|grammar}} A [[property]] attached to a word, but not [[essential]] to it, such as [[gender]], [[number]], or [[case]].
##* ''a'' '''1799''', John Parkhurst, ''A Hebrew and English lexicon without points'', page 25
##*: An adjective, so called because adjectitious, or added to a substantive, denotes some quality or '''accident''' of the substantive to which it is joined {{...}}
# {{senseid|en|incontinence}} {{lb|en|euphemistic}} An instance of [[incontinence]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Marcia Stedron|title=My Roller Coaster Life as an Army Wife|publisher=Xlibris Corporation|isbn=1462817890|pageurl=http://books.google.de/books?id=by6CBEvNtWIC&lpg=PA56&pg=PA56&f=false#v=onepage&q&f=false|page=56
|passage=We weren’t there long when Karin asked about our dog. When we told her Chris was in the car, she insisted we bring him up to the apartment. I rejected her offer and said he might have an '''accident''' on the carpet and I didn’t want to worry about it.}}
## [[urine|Urine]] or [[feces]] [[excrete]]d due to incontinence.
# {{senseid|en|unintended pregnancy}} {{lb|en|euphemistic}} An unintended [[pregnancy]].
## {{senseid|en|person}} {{lb|en|derogatory|or|humorous}} A [[person]] born from an unintended [[pregnancy]].
##: {{ux|en|Taylor was our sweet little '''accident''', and we're so glad!}}
##: {{ux|en|Well I may be annoying but at least I'm not an '''accident''' like you are}}
# {{lb|en|geology}} An irregular surface feature with no apparent cause.
# {{lb|en|geology}} A sudden discontinuity of ground such as fault of great thickness, bed or lentil of unstable ground.<ref>{{cite-book|
| title = Dictionary of Civil Engineering: English-French
| publisher = Springer Science & Business Media
| date = 2007-05-08
| orig-year = 2004
| editor-first = Jean-Paul
| editor-last = Kurtz
| series = EngineeringPro
| isbn = 978-0-306-48474-2
| doi = 10.1007/b104633
| entry = geological accident - accident géologique
| quote = A sudden discontinuity of ground such as fault of great thickness, bed or lentil of unstable ground, etc.
| page = 573
| entry-url = https://books.google.de/books?id=Yp8SBwAAQBAJ&pg=PA573
| entry-url-access = limited
}}</ref>
# {{senseid|en|heraldry}} {{lb|en|heraldry}} A [[point]] or [[mark]] which may be [[retained]] or [[omitted]] in a [[coat of arms]].

====Usage notes====
Risk management and risk mitigation experts (such as actuaries, systems engineers, and others) generally do not approve of calling motor vehicle crashes (MVCs) "accidents", because they advisedly reserve that term for things not directly caused by human recklessness or negligence. Because it is [[predictably#Adverb|predictably]] obvious (and directly [[causal#Adjective|causal]]) that distracted driving (e.g., texting, IMing/DMing, videogaming, or intoxication while driving) produces MVCs, those MVCs are not "accidents". Nonetheless, among the general public, MVCs are quite often called "accidents" rather than "crashes" or "collisions", not only by {{l|en|idiom|id=language structure|idiomatic}} inertia<!--inertial resistance, by idiomaticness, to prescriptive change--> but also because [[connotation#Noun|connotatively]], it steers clear of broaching the topic of [[blame#Noun|blame]] assignment, whereas a phrase like "he crashed" connotes blame.<!--in other words, this is descriptively ubiquitous, and speakers have to know what the word often means, regardless of whether it should.-->

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|unexpected event with negative consequences}} {{l|en|mishap}}
* {{sense|unexpected event that takes place without foresight or expectation}} {{l|en|befalling}}, {{l|en|chance}}, {{l|en|contingency}}, {{l|en|casualty}}; See also [[Thesaurus:accident]]
* {{sense|chance}} {{l|en|fortune}}, {{l|en|luck}}; see also [[Thesaurus:luck]]
