==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|pratt}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/pɹat/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-prat.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
*: {{rhymes|en|æt|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|prat}}, from {{inh|en|ang|præt}}, {{m|ang|prætt|t=trick, prank, craft, art, wile}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*prattu}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*prattuz|t=boastful talk, deceit}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*brodno-|t=to wander about}}. Cognate with {{cog|stq|prat}}, {{cog|nl|pret||fun, pleasure, gaity}}, obsolete {{cog|nl|prat||cunning, strategem, scheme, a prideful display, arrogance}}, {{cog|nds|prot}}, {{cog|no|prette||trick}}, {{cog|is|prettur||a trick}}. Related to {{l|en|pretty}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|now|_|Scotland}} A cunning or mischievous [[trick]]; a [[prank]], a [[joke]]. {{defdate|from 10th c.}}

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|en|pretty}}

=====Translations=====
{{trans-top|cunning or mischievous trick}}
* Finnish: {{t+|fi|jekku}}
{{trans-bottom}}

====Adjective====
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[cunning|Cunning]], [[astute]]. {{defdate|13th|17th c.}}

===Etymology 2===
{{unk|en}}. Perhaps a specialised use of Etymology 1 (see above).

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|slang}} A [[buttock]], or the [[buttocks]]; a person's [[bottom]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* '''1608''', Thomas Dekker, ''The Canters Dictionarie'' in ''The Belman of London'' (second part ''Lanthorne and Candlelight'')
#*: ''Pratt'', a Buttock.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1707|author=John Shirley|title=The Triumph of Wit|chapter=The Maunder's Praise of his Strowling Mort|passage=No gentry mort hath '''prats''' like thine, / No cove e'er wap'd with such a one.
}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1952|author=Leonard Bishop|title=Down All Your Streets|page=218
|passage=Burt shook his head, wanting to tell Mac what a pain in the '''prat''' he was when he went on a take, but instead, repeated his instruction, keeping his voice at a whisper, moving his fingertips along the table {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1982|author=w:TC Boyle|title=Water Music|page=5|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2006
|passage=Mungo didn't like their attitude. Nor did he like exposing his '''prat''' in mixed company.}}
# {{lb|en|UK|Ireland|Australia|New Zealand|slang}} A [[fool]]. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=29 June 2023|journal=Metro|location=London|page=10|column=3|passage=Those protestors will have achieved nothing good. They are stupid '''prats'''.}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} The [[female]] [[genitals]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* '''1967''' (sourced to 1942), William A. Schwartz, ''[http://books.google.com.au/books?id=te7YAAAAMAAJ&q=%22her+prat%22&dq=%22her+prat%22&hl=en The Limerick: 1700 Examples with Notes, Variants and Examples]'' Vol 1, Greenleaf Classics 1967, p. 124:
#*: "She's a far better piece<br>Than the Viceroy's niece,<br>Who has also more fur on her '''prat'''."
#* '''1984''' John Murray, ed, ''[http://books.google.com.au/books?id=pvgqAQAAIAAJ&q=%22her+prat%22&dq=%22her+prat%22&hl=en Panurge]'', Vol 1–3, p. 39:
#*: "...they would kidnap a girl and take her back to their camp where they would pull down her knickers, hoping to find hairs on her '''prat'''."
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=Sherrie Seibert Goff|title=The Arms of Quirinus|url=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=p3D8rCQOt9cC&pg=PA135&dq=%22my+prat%22&hl=en|page=135|publisher=iUniverse
|passage="My '''prat''' was sore from the unfamiliar activities of the night before, but my virgin bleeding had ceased, and we rode most of the day in that unworldly haze that comes with lack of sleep."}}

=====Synonyms=====
* See also [[Thesaurus:fool]]
* See also [[Thesaurus:buttocks]]

