Page 988 鼻: -------- begin text --------
{{also|自}}
{{character info}}
{{character info|⿐}}
{{character info|0x2FA1C}}
==Translingual==
{{stroke order|type=animate}}
{{ja-forms|鼻|鼻|鼻}}

===Alternative forms===
The form of this character differs slightly between China and Japan:
: in China: <span style="font-size:xx-large">{{lang|zh|sc=Hani|鼻}}</span>,
: in Japan: <span style="font-size:xx-large">{{lang|ja|鼻}}</span>
That is, in China the bottom is [[丌]] (strokes T-junction), while in Japan the bottom is [[廾]] (strokes cross).

Due to {{w|Han unification}}, they share the same codepoint.

===Han character===
{{Han char|rn=209|rad=鼻|as=00|sn=14|four=26446|canj=HUWML|ids=⿱自畀}}

# {{mul-kangxi radical-def}}

====Derived characters====
* [[Appendix:Chinese radical/鼻]]
* {{l|mul|[[𠏿]], [[嚊]], [[𪤨]], [[嬶]], [[𡽶]], [[擤]], [[濞]], [[𮥭]], [[𭢷]], [[䑄]], [[襣]], [[𦤫]], [[𧓧]], [[𧗗]]}}
* {{l|mul|[[劓]], [[𨞳]], [[𦫱]], [[𩕬]], [[𫗅]], [[䕗]], [[𢋛]], [[𤻖]]}}

====References====
{{Han ref|kx=1530.010|dkj=48498|dj=2066.150|hdz=74779.010|uh=9F3B|ud=40763|bh=BBF3|bd=48115}}
{{Han ref|uh=2FA1C}}

==Chinese==
{{zh-forms|ss=𢍂}}

===Glyph origin===
{{Han etyl}}
{{Han compound|自|t1=nose|畀|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p}}.

{{och-l|自}} originally meant “nose” but came to be used to mean “self”, so the sense of “nose” has been replaced by {{och-l|鼻}}. Some scholars interpret {{och-l|鼻}} as a combination of a nose ({{och-l|自}}) and two lungs ({{och-l|畀}}).

===Etymology===
From {{inh|zh|sit-pro|*bi||nose}}; compare {{cog|ii|ꅳꁖ|ꅳ'''ꁖ'''|nose; snot}}.

Alternatively, it may be from {{inh|zh|sit-pro|*s-brit||sneeze; nose; swallow}}, whence {{cog|bo|སྦྲིད||sneeze}}, but there is no trace of ''r'' in Chinese ({{zh-ref|Schuessler, 2007}}).

In some modern lects, including Mandarin, Gan, Jin, Wu, and Xiang, and even in the literary layer of some Min dialects, the word reflects a form with final {{m|und|*-t}}. For example, in standard Mandarin, the word is pronounced ''bí'' (implying an old entering tone) instead of ''bì'' (the expected reflex from the departing tone in Middle Chinese). This is due to a phonological phenomenon in the northwest, either an early loss of {{m|und|*-s}} in the {{m|und|*-ts}} cluster before regular final cluster simplification occurred ({{zh-ref|Baxter, 1992}}), or a dialectal change from {{m|und|*-s}} to {{m|und|*-t}} ({{zh-ref|Pulleybank, 1998}}).

===Pronunciation===
