==English==
{{was wotd|2018|March|19}}

===Pronunciation===
[[File:Pingos near Tuk.jpg|thumb|Pingos near {{w|Tuktoyaktuk}} in the [[Northwest Territories]], [[Canada]]]]

* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈpɪŋɡəʊ/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-pingo.wav |Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈpɪŋɡoʊ/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪŋɡəʊ|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|pin|go}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{der|en|kl|pingu}} or {{der|en|iu|pingu||hummock, small hill}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|es|s}}

# {{lb|en|geomorphology}} A [[conical]] [[mound]] of [[earth]] with an [[ice#Noun|ice]] [[core #Noun|core]] caused by [[permafrost]] [[uplift#Noun|uplift]], particularly if lasting more than a [[year]]. {{defdate|from 1920s}}
#: {{syn|en|hydrolaccolith}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:J. Ross Mackay|J[ohn] Ross Mackay]]|title=The Mackenzie Delta Area, N.W.T.|series=Memoir (Geographical Branch, Department of Mines and Technical Surveys, Canada)|seriesvolume=8|location=Ottawa, Ont.|publisher=[[w:Natural Resources Canada|Department of Mines and Technical Surveys]]|year=1963|page=74|oclc=937121886|passage=The greatest variation in cover thickness, as determined from collapsed '''pingos''', is in irregularly shaped '''pingos''', or those with asymmetrically located ice-cores.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Roger J. E. Brown; Troy L. Péwé|chapter=Distribution of Permafrost in North America and Its Relationship to the Environment: A Review, 1963–1973: 13–28 July 1973, Yakutsk, U.S.S.R.|title=Permafrost: North American Contribution: Second International Conference|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=w:National Academy of Sciences|year=1973|page=80|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=SjErAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA80|column=2|isbn=978-0-309-02115-9|passage=Considerable progress has been made on the discovery and mapping of many open system '''pingos''' in central Alaska and Yukon Territory{{nb...}}, as well as the discovery of '''pingo'''-like mounds in the shallow waters of the Beaufort Sea north of the mouth of the Mackenzie River. The greatest advance in '''pingo''' research in the last decade has been a consideration and understanding of theory and rate of '''pingo''' growth {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=[[w:J. Ross Mackay|J[ohn] Ross Mackay]]|title=Oxygen Isotope Variations in Permafrost, Tuktoyaktuk Peninsula Area, Northwest Territories|journal=Current Research Part B = Recherches en Cours Partie B|series=Geological Survey Paper|seriesvolume=83-1B|location=Ottawa, Ont.|publisher=w:Geological Survey of Canada|year=1983|page=68|pageurl=https://www.iaea.org/inis/collection/NCLCollectionStore/_Public/16/029/16029558.pdf#page=74|isbn=978-0-660-11391-3|passage=With the exception of small '''pingos''', most '''pingo''' ice cores have several ice types. The bulk of the core can be segregated ice, intrusive ice formed from the freezing of bulk water, or any combination of the two types. In addition, dilation-crack ice (tension-crack ice, Brown and Kupsch, 1974) is commonly the main ice type beneath the summit of '''pingos''' with craters.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=I. B. Campbell; G. G. C. Claridge|chapter=|title=Antarctica: Soils, Weathering Processes and Environment|series=Developments in Soil Science|seriesvolume=16|location=Amsterdam; New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Elsevier|year=1987|page=106|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=WfhG-Qxz8psC&pg=PA106|isbn=978-0-444-42784-7|passage=Larger scale frost-heave features, such as '''pingoes''', are rare because there is insufficient water available, generally, for the growth of large ice bodies.}}

=====Translations=====
{{trans-top|conical mound of earth with an ice core}}
* Armenian: {{t|hy|հիդրոլակոլիթ}}
* Chinese: {{t+|zh|小丘}}
* Czech: {{t+|cs|pingo}}
