===Etymology 2===
[[File:Methi Water Boy.jpg|thumb|A young man in [[Mumbai]], [[India]], using a [[carrying pole]] to water [[fenugreek]] sprouts. A similar device is known in [[Sri Lanka]] as a pingo ''(etymology 2, sense 1)''.]]

Apparently from {{der|en|si}} (?),<ref>{{cite-book|entry=PINGO|editor={{w|Edward Balfour}}|title=Cyclopædia of India and of Eastern and Southern Asia, Commercial, Industrial and Scientific: Products of the Mineral, Vegetable and Animal Kingdoms, Useful Arts and Manufactures|edition=2nd|location=Madras|publisher=Printed at the Scottish, and Lawrence Presses|year=1873|volume=IV|page=580|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=BoNRAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA580|column=1|oclc=80069237|passage='''PINGO''', {{small|Singh[alese]}}, {{...}} an elastic stick loaded at both ends, poised on the shoulder, used in Ceylon for carrying burthens.}}</ref> but the word has not yet been identified.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|es|s}}

# {{lb|en|Sri Lanka|dated}} A [[flexible]] [[pole#Noun|pole]] [[support#Verb|supported]] on one [[shoulder#Noun|shoulder]], with a [[load#Noun|load]] [[suspend]]ed from each end.
#: {{syn|en|carrying pole|milkmaid's yoke|shoulder pole}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:James Emerson Tennent|J[ames] Emerson Tennent]]|chapter=Appendix to Chapter III. Narratives of the Natives of Ceylon Relative to Encounters with Rogue Elephants.|title=Sketches of the Natural History of Ceylon with Narratives and Anecdotes Illustrative of the Habits and Instincts of the Mammalia, Birds, Reptiles, Fishes, Insects, &c. Including a Monograph of the Elephant and a Description of the Modes of Capturing and Training It. With Engravings from Original Drawings|location=London|publisher=[[w:Longman|Longman, Green, Longman, and Roberts]]|year=1861|page=138|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=DgoXAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA138|oclc=311380996|passage=This done, he [an elephant] took up the '''pingo''' and moved away from the spot; but at the distance of about a fathom or two, laid it down again, and ripping open one of the bundles, took out of it all the contents, ''somans'' [''footnote'': Woman's robe], ''cambāyas'' [''footnote'': The figured cloth worn by men], handkerchiefs, and several pieces of white cambrick cloth, all which he tore to small pieces, and flung them wildly here and there. He did the same with all the other '''pingoes'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=S. M. Burrows|title=A Year’s Work at Polonnáruwa|journal=w:Journal of the Ceylon Branch of the Royal Asiatic Society|location=Colombo, Ceylon|publisher=G. J. A. Skeen, government printer, Ceylon|year=1887|year_published=1888|volume=X|issue=34|page=49|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/in.ernet.dli.2015.104379/2015.104379.Journal-Of-The-Ceylon-Branch-Of-The-Royal-Asiatic-Society-1887-88-Vol10#page/n55/mode/1up/|issn=0304-2235|oclc=1695542|passage=The ''Gańga-vaṇṣa minissu'' are the washers of the Oliya caste, who are not only a low caste, but come below the Paduvó and Berawáyó, and are the only caste who will carry the '''pingoes''' of the smiths.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:James Emerson Tennent|chapter=Vegetation.—Trees and Plants.|title=Ceylon: An Account on the Island Physical, Historical, and Topographical with Notices of Its Natural History, Antiquities and Productions|location=London|publisher=[[w:Longman|Longman, Green, Longman, and Roberts]]|year=1859|volume=I|section=part I (Physical Geography), footnote 2|page=109|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=DRg-AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA109|oclc=1003975740|passage=The following are only a few of the countless uses of this invaluable tree [the coconut]. {{...}} The ''stem of the leaf'', for fences, for '''pingoes''' (or yokes) for carrying burthens on the shoulders, for fishing-rods, and innumerable domestic utensils.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Ananda Coomaraswamy|Ananda Kentish Coomaraswamy]]|title=Mediaeval Sinhalese Art: Being a Monograph on Mediaeval Sinhalese Arts and Crafts, Mainly as Surviving in the Eighteenth Century, with an Account of the Structure of Society and the Status of the Craftsmen|location=Broad Campden, Gloucestershire|publisher=Essex House Press|year=1908|page=206|oclc=757385109|passage=Ceremonial '''pingoes''' may also be silver tipped, as in the case of a beautiful example at the Embekke Devale{{nb...}}.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Ali Foad Toulba|chapter=The Beautiful Mountain Railway to Kandy|title=Ceylon: The Land of Eternal Charm|location=London|publisher=[[w:Hutchinson (publisher)|Hutchinson & Co. (Publishers) Ltd.]], {{w|Paternoster Row}}, E.C.|year=1926|oclc=24653136|newversion=reprinted|location2=New Delhi; Madras|publisher2=J. Jetley, {{w|Asian Educational Services}}|year2=2000|page2=145|pageurl2=https://archive.org/stream/in.ernet.dli.2015.46756/2015.46756.Ceylon#page/n185/mode/1up/|isbn2=978-81-206-1494-9|passage='''Pingo''' bearers walk to and fro with their burdens of fruit and vegetables, representing many varieties quite strange to us. The '''pingo''' is a long and flat piece of wood from the kittul palm, very tough and pliable. The coolie, having suspended his load to the two ends in baskets or nets, places the stave upon his shoulder at the middle, and is thus enabled by the elastic spring and easy balance of the '''pingo''' to carry great weights for a considerable distance. Some '''pingoes''' are made from the leaf-stalk of the coconut palm, which is even more pliable than the kittul.}}
# {{lb|en|Sri Lanka|dated}} A [[measure#Noun|measure]] of [[weight]] equivalent to that which can be [[carry|carried]] using a pingo, perhaps about 55 [[pound#Noun|pounds]] (25 [[kilogram]]s) ''(see the 2013 quotation)''.
#: {{syn|en|picul}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|work=The Asiatic Journal and Monthly Register for British and Foreign India|passage=Upon reaching this quantity, the tribute decreased one '''pingo''' annually until the number was reduced to five.|title=The Chaliah Caste in Ceylon|page=275|year=1833}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Dandris De Silva Goonaratne|title=On Demonology and Witchcraft in Ceylon|journal=w:Journal of the Ceylon Branch of the Royal Asiatic Society|location=Colombo, Ceylon|publisher=F. Fonseka, printer, Fort, Colombo|year=1866|section=footnote|page=36|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=CXlkAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA36|issn=0304-2235|oclc=1695542|passage=About an hour or so before a bridegroom accompanied by his friends arrives at the house of the bride, a person, named for the occasion ''Gamana'' or ''messenger'', is sent forward with a number of betel leaves equal to the number of people, who accompany the bridegroom. The Gamana is to give these betel leaves to the bride's friends, together with the large '''''pingo''''' of plantains called ''Gira-mul-tada'', which in the Maritime districts is always a ''sine qua non'' of the presents, which a Singhalese bridegroom carries to his bride's house.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Karunasena Dias Paranavitana|chapter=The Portuguese ''Tombos'' as a Source of Sixteenth- and Seventeenth-century Sri Lankan History|editor=Jorge Flores|title=Re-exploring the Links: History and Constructed Histories between Portugal and Sri Lanka|series=Maritime Asia|seriesvolume=18|location=Wiesbaden|publisher=w:Harrassowitz Verlag|year=2007|page=74|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=LspBFqePhpwC&pg=PA74|isbn=978-3-447-05490-4|issn=1863-6268|passage=He {{...}} paid the lord of the village three '''''pingos''''' worth one ''larim'' and four ''fanões''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Lodewijk Wagenaar|chapter=The Apparition of the Cinnamon Peelers: Dutch Colonial Presence in Eighteenth-century Ceylon and Its Reflection in Non-literary Prose|editors=w:Jeroen Dewulf; Ole Praamstra; w:Michiel van Kempen|title=Shifting the Compass: Pluricontinental Connections in Dutch Colonial and Postcolonial Literature|location=Newcastle upon Tyne|publisher=Cambridge Scholars Publishing|year=2013|page=125|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=_LEwBwAAQBAJ&pg=PA125|isbn=978-1-4438-4228-0|passage=A certain Wieremunie Joan testified about facts which already had occurred in 1772 when he had delivered four and a half '''''pingo''''' [''footnote'': A "'''pingo'''" is circa fifty-five pound of cinnamon.{{nb...}}] of cinnamon above the fixed duty of five and that the Durea still owed him four and a half rixdollar.}}

