Page 1084 step: -------- begin text --------
{{also|STEP|štep|stęp|step-}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}
[[File:Age-friendly stairs with 2 anti-slip rubber lines.jpeg|thumb|Two steps [2] of a staircase]]

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP|GA}} {{IPA|en|/stɛp/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-step.ogg|Audio (GA)}}
* {{homophones|en|steppe}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛp|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|steppen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|steppan||to step, go, proceed, advance}}, {{m|ang|stepe||step}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*stappjan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*stapjaną||to step}}, {{m|gem-pro|*stapiz||step}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*stebʰ-||to support, stomp, curse, be amazed}}.

Cognate with {{cog|fy|stappe||to step}}, {{cog|frr|stape||to walk, trudge}}, {{cog|nl|stappen||to step, walk}}, {{cog|wa|steper||to walk away, leave}}, {{cog|de|stapfen||to trudge, stomp, plod}} and further to Slavic {{cog|pl|stąpać||to stomp, stamp, step, tread}}, {{cog|ru|ступать}} and {{cog|pl|stopień||step, stair, rung, degree}}, {{cog|ru|степень}}. Related to {{l|en|stamp}}, {{l|en|stomp}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# An advance or movement made from one foot to the other; a [[pace]].
#* {{RQ:Vance Nobody|III
|passage=Turning back, then, toward the basement staircase, she began to grope her way through blinding darkness, but had taken only a few uncertain '''steps''' when, of a sudden, she stopped short and for a little stood like a stricken thing, quite motionless save that she quaked to her very marrow in the grasp of a great and enervating fear.}}
#* {{seemoreCites|en}}<!-- Please no more than 3–4 quotes here, further quotes only in 'Citations' (see [[WT:QUOTE]])-->
# A rest, or one of a set of rests, for the foot in ascending or descending, as a [[stair]], or a [[rung]] of a [[ladder]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1624|author=w:Sir Henry Wotton|title=The Elements Of Architecture
|passage=The breadth of every single '''step''' or stair should be never less than one foot.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=4|passage=One morning I had been driven to the precarious refuge afforded by the '''steps''' of the inn, after rejecting offers from the Celebrity to join him in a variety of amusements. But even here I was not free from interruption, for he was seated on a horse-block below me, playing with a fox terrier.}}
#* {{quote-book|en| year=1967| author=w:Barbara Sleigh| title=w:Jessamy| year_published=1993|location=Sevenoaks, Kent| publisher=Bloomsbury| isbn=0 340 19547 9| page=122| passage=Through the open front door ran Jessamy, down the '''steps''' to where Kitto was sitting at the bottom with the pram beside him.}}
#* {{seemoreCites|en}}<!-- Please no more than 3–4 quotes here, further quotes only in 'Citations' (see [[WT:QUOTE]])-->
# The part of a [[spade]], [[digging stick]] or similar tool that a digger's foot rests against and presses on when digging; an [[ear]], a foot-rest.
# A distinct part of a process; [[stage]]; [[phase]].
#: {{ux|en|He improved '''step''' by '''step''', or by '''steps'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|The first '''step''' is to find a job.}}
# A [[running board]] where passengers step to get on and off the bus.
#: {{ux|en|The driver must have a clear view of the '''step''' in order to prevent accidents.}}
# The space passed over by one movement of the foot in walking or running.
#: {{ux|en|One '''step''' is generally about three feet, but may be more or less.}}
#* {{RQ:Newton Opticks|passage=To derive two or three general principles of motion from phenomena, and afterwards to tell us how the properties and actions of all corporeal things follow from those manifest principles, would be a very great '''step''' in philosophy.}}
# A small [[space]] or [[distance]].
#: {{ux|en|It is but a '''step'''.}}
# A print of the foot; a [[footstep]]; a [[footprint]]; [[track]].
# A [[gait]]; manner of walking.
#: {{ux|en|The approach of a man is often known by his '''step'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Chesnutt House Behind the Cedars|I|passage=Warwick passed through one of the wide brick arches and traversed the building with a leisurely '''step'''.}}
#* {{seemoreCites|en}}<!-- Please no more than 3–4 quotes here, further quotes only in 'Citations' (see [[WT:QUOTE]])-->
# Proceeding; measure; action; act.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1717|author=w:Alexander Pope|title=Preface to his collection of poems
|passage=The reputation of a man depends on the first '''steps''' he makes in the world.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=c. 1792|author=w:William Cowper|title=The Needless Alarm
|passage=Beware of desperate '''steps'''. The darkest day, Live till to-morrow, will have passed away.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1879|author=w:George Washington Cable|title=Old Creole Days
|passage=I have lately taken '''steps'''{{...}}to relieve the old gentleman's distresses.}}
#* '''2019''', [https://web.archive.org/web/20190311070055/https://learningenglish.voanews.com/a/south-korea-proposes-rain-project-with-china-to-cut-pollution/4819207.html VOA Learning English] (public domain)
#*: Moon has also requested that government officials take additional '''steps''' to help fight pollution, his spokesman said.
#*:: {{audio|en|Moon has also requested that government officials take additional steps to help fight pollution, his spokesman said.ogg|Audio (US)}}
# {{lb|en|in the plural}} A [[walk]]; [[passage]].
#* {{RQ:Dryden Aeneis|passage=Conduct my '''steps''' to find the fatal tree.}}
# {{lb|en|in the plural}} A portable framework of stairs, much used indoors in reaching to a high position.
# {{lb|en|nautical}} A framing in wood or iron which is intended to receive an upright shaft; specifically, a block of wood, or a solid platform upon the [[keelson]], supporting the heel of the mast.
# {{lb|en|machines}} One of a series of offsets, or parts, resembling the steps of stairs, as one of the series of parts of a cone pulley on which the belt runs.
# {{lb|en|machines}} A bearing in which the lower extremity of a spindle or a vertical shaft revolves.
# {{senseid|en|music}} {{lb|en|music}} The [[interval]] between two contiguous degrees of the scale.
#: '''Usage note:''' The word [[tone]] is often used as the name of this interval; but there is evident incongruity in using tone for indicating the interval between tones. As the word scale is derived from the Italian ''[[scala]]'', a ladder, the intervals may well be called steps.
# {{lb|en|kinematics}} A change of position effected by a motion of translation.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1878|author=w:William Kingdon Clifford|title=Elements of Dynamic: An Introduction to the Study of Motion
|passage=A change of position effected by a motion of translation will be called a '''''step'''''.}}
# {{lb|en|programming}} A constant difference between consecutive values in a series. <!--usu. "step size"-->
#: {{ux|en|Printing from 0 to 9 with a '''step''' of 3 will display 0, 3, 6 and 9.}}
# {{lb|en|slang|primarily [[Netherlands]]}} [[kick scooter|Kick scooter]].

