# {{alt case|mul|Golf|nodot=1}} {{ng|of the ICAO/NATO radiotelephony alphabet}}.

==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
The word is first known in English from the 17th century as a {{glossary|borrowing}} from {{bor|en|sco-smi|golf}}, {{m|sco-smi|gouff}}. Although the etymology is uncertain, the most likely origin is that it comes from the {{der|en|dum|colve}}, {{m|dum|colf||club}}, ultimately from {{der|en|gem-pro|*kulbaz||club}}, related to {{cog|de|Kolben||piston, rod}}, {{cog|sv|kolv||piston, rod}}, {{cog|ang|clopp||rock; cliff}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ɡɒlf/}}
** {{a|Conservative RP}} {{IPA|en|/ɡɒf/}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ɡɑlf/|/ɡɔlf/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-golf.ogg|Audio (US)}}
** {{a|New England}} {{IPA|en|/ɡɒf/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɒlf|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{lb|en|sports}} A [[ball game]] played by individuals competing against one another in which the object is to hit a ball into each of a series of (usually 18 or nine) holes in the minimum number of [[stroke]]s.
#* {{quote-web
|en
|date=OCTOBER 16, 2017
|author=Adam Jourdan; Matt Miller
|title=In the rough - China closes Wanda golf courses in chilly northeast
|editor=Himani Sarkar
|archiveurl=https://archive.is/Sg7Rb
|archivedate=18 March 2023
|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/uk-china-wanda-golf-idUKKBN1CL12R
|work=w:Reuters
|section=Business News
|text=In January, China’s state planner said it had ordered the closure of more than 100 '''golf''' courses in a multi-year campaign launched in 2011 to tackle illegal development in the sector.<br>'''Golf''' has also been added to a list of Communist Party disciplinary violations, and is often cited in cases of graft.}}
# {{lb|en|international standards}} {{alt case|mul|Golf|nodot=1}} {{ng|from the {{w|NATO Phonetic Alphabet|NATO/ICAO Phonetic Alphabet}}.}}

