Page 1130 loo: -------- begin text --------
{{also|Appendix:Variations of "loo"}}

==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/luː/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/lu/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-loo.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|uː|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
Clipped form of {{m|en|halloo}}.<ref>"[http://www.oed.com/view/Entry/110115 †&nbsp;loo, ''v.²'']" and "[http://www.oed.com/view/Entry/110116 loo, ''int.'']" in the ''Oxford English Dictionary'' (1903), Oxford: Oxford University Press.</ref>

====Interjection====
{{en-interjection}}

# ''A [[cry]] to [[urge]] on [[hunting]] [[dog]]s.''
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King Lear|1|xi||text=''[[Edgar|Edg]]''. [[pilicock|Pilicock]] sate on pelicocks hill, a '''[[loo|lo]] [[loo|lo]] [[loo|lo]]'''.}}

=====Alternative forms=====
* {{l|en|'loo}}; {{l|en|lo}}, {{l|en|lowe}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|looky-loo}}
* {{l|en|loo in}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|now|_|dialect|used|_|with|_|at|upon|or|_|infinitive}} To [[urge]] on with [[cries]] of ''loo'' or {{label|en|figuratively}} by [[other]] [[shouting]] or [[outcry]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1667|author=John Denham|url=https://books.google.com.hk/books?id=rQw1AAAAMAAJ&pg=PA31|title=Directions to a Painter|section=ll. 21 f
|passage=And therefore next uncouple either Hound [''[[sc.]]'' [[w:George Monck, 1st Duke of Albemarle|George Monck]] and [[w:Prince Rupert of the Rhine|Prince Rupert]]],<br>And [[loo]] them at two Hares ere one be found.}}

===Etymology 2===
{{wikipedia|Lanterloo}}
Clipped form of {{m|en|lanterloo}}.<ref>"[http://www.oed.com/view/Entry/110110 loo, ''n.¹'']" and "[http://www.oed.com/view/Entry/110114 loo, ''v.¹'']" in the ''Oxford English Dictionary'' (1903), Oxford: Oxford University Press.</ref>

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{alt form|en|lanterloo|nodot=a}}: the [[card game]].
#* {{RQ:Wycherley Country Wife|part=epilogue|passage=And my Intreagues ſo baſhfully diſown / That they may doubted be by few or none, / May kiſs the Cards at [[picquet|Picque]], [[ombre|Hombre]],—'''Lu''', / And ſo be taught to kiſs the Lady too; [...]}}
#* {{RQ:Pope Rape of the Lock|canto=iii|line=22|text=[[even|Ev'n]] mighty [[pam|Pam]] that Kings and Queens [[overthrow|o'erthrew]],<br>And [[mowed|mow'd]] down Armies in the Fights of [[loo|Lu]].}}
#* '''1731''', [[w:Jonathan Swift|Jonathan Swift]], To Dr. Helsham, 16:
#*: Yet, ladies are seldom at [[ombre]] or [[loo|lue]] sick.
#* {{RQ:Austen Pride and Prejudice|I|viii|page=80|text=On entering the drawing-room, she found the whole party at [[loo]].}}
# The [[penalty]] [[paid]] to the [[pool]] in [[lanterloo]] for [[break]]ing [[certain]] [[rule]]s or [[fail]]ing to [[take]] a [[trick]].
# An [[act]] that [[prompt]]s [[such]] a [[penalty]].
# A [[game]] of [[lanterloo]].
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} [[any|Any]] [[group]] of [[people]].

=====Alternative forms=====
* {{l|en|lu}}, {{l|en|liew}}, {{l|en|lue}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}

=====Derived terms=====
{{der3|en|for the good of the loo
|Irish loo
|limited loo
|loo club
|{{l|en|loo table}}, {{l|en|loo-table}}
|unlimited loo
}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[beat]] in the card game [[lanterloo]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1847|author=Henry Cockton|title=The Love Match|page=232
|passage=He was seldom indeed without ''two'' good trumps, and therefore almost invariably '''loo'd''' those who stood.}}
# To [[pay]] a [[penalty]] to the [[pool]] for [[break]]ing [[certain]] [[rule]]s or [[fail]]ing to [[take]] a [[trick]] in [[lanterloo]].
# {{label|en|figurative|now|_|dialect}} To [[pay]] [[any]] [[penalty]] to [[any]] [[community]].

=====Translations=====
{{trans-see|lanterloo}}

===Etymology 3===
[[File:Mauchon deVenitienneXVIe siecle.gif|thumb|right|150px|A 16th-century Venetian in a half-mask.]]
[[File:Guillaume SeignacPierrotemrace.jpg|thumb|right|150px|''Pierrot's Embrace'' (c. 1900), featuring a loo.]]
From {{der|en|fr|loup||[[wolf]]; [[mask]], [[eyemask]]}}.<ref>"[http://www.oed.com/view/Entry/110111 †&nbsp;loo, ''n.²'']" in the ''Oxford English Dictionary'' (1903), Oxford: Oxford University Press.</ref> {{doublet|en|lobo|lupus|wolf}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|fashion|obsolete}} A [[half-]][[mask]], ''[[particularly]]'' {{label|en|historical}} [[those]] [[velvet]] [[half-]][[mask]]s [[fashionable]] in the 17th [[century]] as a [[means]] of [[protect]]ing [[women]]'s [[complexion]] from the [[sun]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=a. 1685|author=Mary Evelyn|chapter=The Fop-dictionary|title=Mundus Muliebris|pageurl=http://quod.lib.umich.edu/e/eebo/A38815.0001.001/1:5?rgn=div1;view=fulltext|page=18
|passage=[[loo|Loo]] [[mask|Mask]]. An half Mask.}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|loo mask}}

=====See also=====
* {{l|en|domino mask}}

===Etymology 4===
From {{der|en|hi|उल्का}}, from {{der|en|sa|उल्का||[[flame]]}}.<ref>"[http://www.oed.com/view/Entry/110112 loo, ''n.³'']" in the ''Oxford English Dictionary'' (1976), Oxford: Oxford University Press.</ref>

====Noun====
{{en-noun|?}}

# {{lb|en|India}} A [[hot]] [[dust]]-[[bear]]ing [[wind]] [[found]] in [[Bihar]], [[Uttar Pradesh]] and the [[Punjab]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1888|author=w:Rudyard Kipling|chapter=w:The Man Who Would Be King|title=w:The Phantom 'Rickshaw and Other Tales|page=78
|passage=It was a pitchy black night, as stifling as a June night can be, and the '''''loo''''', the red-hot wind from the westward, was booming among the tinder-dry trees and pretending that the rain was on its heels.}}

===Etymology 5===
{{wikipedia|toilet|toilet (fixture)}}
{{wikipedia|toilet (room)}}
{{unc|en}}, although usually derived in some way from {{m|en|Waterloo}}, the site of [[w:Duke of Wellington|Wellington]]'s [[w:Battle of Waterloo|1815 victory]] over [[w:Napoleon|Napoleon]], likely via a pun based on {{m|en|water closet}}.<ref>"[http://www.oed.com/view/Entry/110113 loo, ''n.⁴'']" in the ''Oxford English Dictionary'' (1976), Oxford: Oxford University Press.</ref><ref name=ross>[[w:Alan S. C. Ross|Ross, Alan S.C.]] "Loo" in ''Blackwood's Magazine'' (October 1974), Vol. 316, pp. 309–316.</ref><ref name=party>{{R:Partridge|year=1984|page=1386}}</ref><ref name=www>"[http://www.worldwidewords.org/qa/qa-loo1.htm Loo]" in {{w|Michael Quinion}}'s ''[http://www.worldwidewords.org World Wide Words]'' (13 February 1999).</ref><ref name=oetd>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref><ref name=muir>[[w:Frank Muir|Muir, Frank]]. ''A Book at Bathtime'' (1982), Heinemann.</ref> Other suggested derivations include corruptions of {{der|en|fr|l'}}{{m|fr|eau||[[water]]}},<ref name=party/> {{m|fr|lieu||[[place]]}},<ref name=party/><ref name=muir/><ref name=words>"[https://books.google.com.hk/books?id=wGNaCwAAQBAJ&pg=PA56 Loo]" (17 July 1983) in John Silverlight's ''[https://books.google.com.hk/books?id=wGNaCwAAQBAJ&printsec=frontcover Words]'' (1985), London: Macmillan Press.</ref> {{m|fr|lieux d'aisances||'[[place]]s of [[convenience]]': a [[lavatory]]}},<ref name=www/><ref name=oetd/><ref name=nwcd>"[http://www.yourdictionary.com/loo#websters loo]" in ''Webster's New World College Dictionary'', 5th ed., Cleveland: Wiley Publishing.</ref> {{m|fr|lieu}} {{m|fr|à}} {{m|fr|l'}}{{m|fr|anglaise||'[[English]] [[place]]': a [[British]][[-style]] [[lavatory]]}}, {{m|fr|bordalou||a [[diminutive]] [[chamber pot]]}}<ref name=party/> or {{m|fr|gardez}} {{m|fr|l'}}{{m|fr|eau||'[[mind]] the [[water]]'}}, via {{der|en|sco|gardyloo}}, formerly used in [[Edinburgh]] while emptying [[chamber pot]]s out of windows;<ref name=party/><ref name=muir/><ref>"[http://www.oed.com/view/Entry/76755 gardyloo, ''n.'']" in the ''Oxford English Dictionary'' (1898), Oxford: Oxford University Press.</ref><ref>Daiches, David. ''Was'' (1975), London: Thames & Hudson.</ref> the supposed use of "Room 100" as the lavatory in [[Continental]] hotels;<ref name=ross/><ref name=muir/> a popularisation of {{m|en|lew}}, a regional corruption of {{m|en|lee||[[downwind]]}}, in reference to shepherds' privies or the former use of [[beakhead]]s on that side of the ship for urination and defecation;<ref name=party/><ref>Nixon, Graham. "Loo" in [http://collections.mun.ca/cdm/compoundobject/collection/lorelang/id/693/rec/8 ''Lore and Language'' (January 1978), Vol. II, No. 8], pp. 27–8.</ref><ref name=words/><ref name=www/> or a clipped form of the name of the unpopular 19th-century [[w:Thomas Anson, 2nd Earl of Lichfield|Countess of Lichfield]] Lady Harriett Georgiana Louisa Hamilton Anson, who was the subject of an 1867 prank whereby her bedroom's namecard was placed on the door to the lavatory, prompting the other guests to begin speaking of "going to Lady Louisa".<ref name=muir/><ref>[[w:Cecil Adams|Adams, Cecil]]. "[http://www.straightdope.com/columns/read/574/why-do-we-call-it-the-john Why Do We Call It the 'John'?]" in ''[https://web.archive.org/web/20130625024512/http://thestraightdope.com/ The Straight Dope]'' (18 October 1985), Sun-Times Media.</ref>

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|UK|Australia|NZ}} A [[lavatory]]: a [[room]] [[use]]d for [[urination]] and [[defecation]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1940|author=w:Nancy Mitford|title=Pigeon Pie|chapter=ii|page=27
|passage=I suppose it is unreal because we have been expecting it [''[[sc.]]'' [[w:World War II|World War II]]] for so long now, and have known that it must be got over before we can go on with our lives. Like in the night when you want to go to the '''loo''' and it is miles away down a freezing cold passage and yet you know you have to go down that passage before you can be happy and sleep again.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=Garth Thompson; et al|title=The Guide′s Guide to Guiding|edition=3rd|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=TN1Mx-4MR8wC&pg=PA160|page=160
|passage=Ensure that the tents are well-sited and clean, [[rubbish bin]]s empty, and that the '''loos''' have toilet paper.}}
# {{lb|en|UK|Australia|NZ}} A [[toilet]]: a [[fixture]] [[use]]d for [[urination]] and [[defecation]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Katharina Kane|title=Lonely Planet: The Gambia and Senegal|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=waW03E44v3AC&pg=PA275|page=275
|passage=The lack of running water in rural areas often makes Western-style '''loos''' hygienic disasters. Suddenly the noncontact squat toilet doesn′t look like such a bad option any more (as long as you roll up your trouser legs).}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Meegan Jones|title=Sustainable Event Management|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=IsnM1xkqsmMC&pg=PA206|page=206
|passage=Waterless urinals are a great way of keeping the guys out of the cubicle toilets, keeping the urine separated from the solid waste (when using composting '''loos''') and reducing water consumption if you have flush '''loos'''.}}

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|room}} ''See'' '''[[Thesaurus:bathroom]]'''
* {{sense|fixture}} ''See'' '''[[Thesaurus:toilet]]'''

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|loo paper}}
* {{l|en|loo roll}}
* {{l|en|portaloo}}

=====Translations=====
{{trans-see|toilet}}

===Etymology 6===
Clipped form of {{m|en|lieutenant}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|slang}} A [[lieutenant]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=J. D. Robb|title=New York to Dallas|publisher=Penguin|isbn=9780425246894|page=91
|passage=I asked my '''loo''' to let me escort you in. I wanted a moment to thank you personally.” “There's no need.” “So you said before, but there is. And was. I'll take you in to Lieutenant Ricchio.”}}

