Page 1167 10/40 Window: -------- begin text --------
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun|head=the [[10#Translingual|10]]/[[40#Translingual|40]] [[window|Window]]}}

# The [[geographic]] region between 10 degrees north and 40 degrees north [[latitude]], especially when considered as a target for [[Christian]] [[missionary]] work.
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Strongholds of the 10/40 window |author=George Otis; Mark Brockman |year=1995 |ISBN=0927545861 |passage=This intercessor's guide features maps and information on each '''10/40 Window''' nation, covering trends, religions, people groups, and history. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Missions: Biblical Foundations and Contemporary Strategies |author=Gailyn Van Rheenen |year=1996 |page=209 |ISBN=0310208092 |passage=All three major non-Christian religions — Islam, Hinduism, and Buddhism — originated in the '''10/40 Window''' and have their greatest strength there. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Between Past and Future |author=Jon Bonk |year=2003 |page=21 |ISBN=0878083847 |passage=The primary but not exclusive focus of AD2000 became the '''10/40 Window''', a term coined for the area of the world between ten degrees and forty degrees north latitude, stretching from northern Africa and southern Spain to Japan and the northern Philippines. }}

{{c|en|Christianity|Ten|Forty|Geography}}
-------- end text --------
Page 1168 905: -------- begin text --------
==English==
{{wikipedia|Area codes 905, 289, and 365}}

===Etymology===
So called after these areas being often assigned the {{w|Area codes 905, 289, and 365|area code 905}} in their telephone numbers. Compare with {{m|en|416|t=Toronto}}, which has a similar etymology.

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun|head=the 905}}

# {{lb|en|Canada|geography}} Suburban areas in the province of [[Ontario]] closely surrounding the city of [[Toronto]].
#* {{quote-book|en| author=Edward Relph | title=Toronto: Transformations in a City and Its Region | publisher=University of Pennsylvania Press| series=Metropolitan Portraits | year=2013 | isbn=978-0-8122-0918-1 | url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=4RCCAAAAQBAJ&pg=PA77 | page=77 |accessdate=2021-01-10|text=The part of the urban region outside the City of Toronto is often referred to locally as "the 905", the telephone area code that distinguishes it from the 416 area code of the City. Because '''the 905''' has mostly been built up since the 1970s it is also common to regard everything there as "the outer suburbs", a useful name that implies that while this recently developed area is an extension of the inner suburbs it is nevertheless different from them.}}
#* {{quote-book|en| author=Allen J. Scott | title=Global City-Regions: Trends, Theory, Policy| publisher=Oxford University Press| year=2001 | isbn=978-0-19-158941-6 |page=178 | url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=mHE0b45cEEUC&pg=PA178 |accessdate=2021-01-10|text=Essentially, Toronto had higher commercial property taxes than the '''905''' area and relatively lower residential property taxes.}}
-------- end text --------
Page 1169 abyssal: -------- begin text --------
==English==

===Etymology===
First attested in the 1690s. From {{der|en|ML.|abyssalis}},<ref name=SOED>{{R:SOED5|page=11}}</ref><ref name=RHD>{{R:RHCD|page=7}}</ref> from {{der|en|la|abyssus||abyss}} + {{m|la|-alis||-al}}.<ref name=WI3>{{R:MW3 1976|page=9}}</ref> Equivalent to {{suffix|en|abyss|al}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/əˈbɪs.l̩/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/əˈbɪs.l̩/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪsəl|s=3}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# {{lb|en|archaic}} Belonging to, or resembling, an [[abyss]]; [[unfathomable]]. {{defdate|First attested in the mid 17<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
# {{senseid|en|geography}} {{lb|en|geography}} Of or belonging to the [[ocean]] depths, especially below 2000 metres (6500 ft): ''[[abyssal zone]]''. {{defdate|First attested in the mid 19<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
# {{lb|en|geology}} Pertaining to or occurring at excessive depths in the earth's [[crust]]; [[plutonic]]. {{defdate|First attested in the late 19<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>

