===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[collection]] or [[mass]].
# {{lb|en|geology|vulcanology}} A mass of [[angular]] [[volcanic]] [[fragment]]s united by [[heat]]; distinguished from '''[[conglomerate]]'''.
# {{lb|en|meteorology}} An ice cover of [[floe]] formed by the [[freezing]] together of various forms of [[ice]].

====Synonyms====
* (''collection or mass''): [[agglomeration]], [[collection]], [[mass]]

====Translations====
{{trans-top|collection or mass}}
* Dutch: {{t+|nl|agglomeraat|n}}
* Polish: {{t+|pl|aglomerat|m}}
{{trans-bottom}}

{{trans-top|geology}}
* Chinese:
*: Mandarin: {{t+|cmn|集塊岩|tr=jíkuàiyán}}
* Dutch: {{t+|nl|agglomeraat|n}}
* Polish: {{t+|pl|aglomerat|m}}
{{trans-bottom}}

{{trans-top|meteorology}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[wind]] or [[collect]] into a [[ball]]; hence, to [[gather]] into a [[mass]] or anything like a mass.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1789|author=w:William Gilpin|title=Observations on the River Wye: and Several Parts of South Wales|location=London|publisher=R. Blamire|edition=2nd|section=Section 10, p. 122|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004863361.0001.000
|passage=The bustle of a croud is not ill-adapted to the pencil: but the management of it requires great artifice. The whole must be massed together, and considered as one body. ¶ I mean not to have the whole body so '''agglomerated''', as to consist of no detached groups: but to have these groups {{...}} appear to belong to one whole, by the artifice of composition, and the effect of light.}}
#* '''1820''', {{w|William Hazlitt}}, “Explanations—Conversation on the Drama with Coleridge” in ''Dramatic Essays'' London: Scott, 1895, p.{{nbsp}}197,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/dramaticessays00lowegoog/page/n235/mode/1up?q=agglomerate]</sup>
#*: His <nowiki>[</nowiki>{{w|Jean Racine}}’s<nowiki>]</nowiki> tragedies are not poetry, are not passion, are not imagination: they are a parcel of set speeches, of epigrammatic conceits, of declamatory phrases, without any of the glow, and glancing rapidity, and principle of fusion in the mind of the poet, to '''agglomerate''' them into grandeur, or blend them into harmony.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1937|author=w:Claude McKay|title=A Long Way from Home|location=New York|publisher=Arno Press|year_published=1969|chapter=3|page=35|url=https://archive.org/details/longwayfromhome00mcka/page/35/mode/1up?q=agglomerated
|passage=There were few white friends in the social life of the peasants. The white colony '''agglomerated''' in the towns and the peasants were 80 per cent of a population of a million.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2009|author=w:Peter Campion|chapter=Imperium|title=The Lions|page=14|publisher=University of Chicago Press
|passage=It feels like doing eighty on the freeway / as little towns '''agglomerate''' and blur:}}
# {{lb|en|geography}} To extend an [[urban]] area by [[contiguous]] development, so as to merge the built-up area of one or more central [[city|cities]] or [[settlement]]s and their suburbs (thus creating an [[agglomeration]]).

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|collect into a ball}} {{l|en|ball}}, {{l|en|ball up}}, {{l|en|bundle up}}, {{l|en|clew}}, {{l|en|conglobate}}, {{l|en|conglobe}}, {{l|en|globe}}, {{l|en|orb}}, {{l|en|wind}}
* {{sense|gather into a mass}} {{l|en|amass}}, {{l|en|gather}}, {{l|en|gather up}}, {{l|en|merge}}, {{l|en|pile up}}; see also [[Thesaurus:pile up]] or [[Thesaurus:coalesce]]

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|agglomeration}}
* {{l|en|agglomerative}}

====Translations====
{{trans-top}}
* Polish: {{t+|pl|aglomerować|impf}}
{{trans-bottom}}

