Page 1197 Arctic: -------- begin text --------
{{also|arctic}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|arctic}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|frm|artique}} (with ''-c-'' reintroduced after Latin in the 17th century), from {{der|en|la|arcticus}}, from {{der|en|grc|ἀρκτικός|t=northern, of the (Great) Bear}}, from {{m|grc|ἄρκτος|t=bear, Ursa Major}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₂ŕ̥tḱos|t=bear}}. Cognate to {{cog|la|ursus}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈɑː(k)tɪk/}} {{qualifier|see [[#Usage notes|Usage notes]]}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈɑɹ(k)tɪk/}} {{qualifier|see [[#Usage notes|Usage notes]]}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-Arctic.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɑː(ɹ)tɪk|ɑː(ɹ)ktɪk|s2=2}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# {{lb|en|astronomy|now only in compounds}} Pertaining to the [[celestial]] [[north pole]], or to the [[pole star]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
# {{lb|en|geography}} Pertaining to the northern polar region of the [[planet]], characterised by extreme [[cold]] and an icy landscape. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Marlowe Edward 2|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A07018.0001.001|text=What neede the '''artick''' people loue star-light,<br>To whom the sunne shines both by day and night.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|edition=1668|book=2|lines=706-711|url=https://archive.org/details/paradiselostpoem00milt_1|text={{...}} on th’ other side<br>Incenc’t with indignation ''Satan'' stood<br>Unterrifi’d, and like a Comet burn’d,<br>That fires the length of ''{{w|Ophiucus}}'' huge<br>In th’ '''Artick''' Sky, and from his horrid hair<br>Shakes Pestilence and Warr.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1788|author=w:Samuel Jackson Pratt|title=Humanity, or the Rights of Nature|location=London|publisher=T. Cadell|section=Book 2, p. 96|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004804259.0001.000
|passage=See <small>FREEDOM</small> smiling thro’ the realms of frost,<br>And glow on Labradore’s inclement coast,<br>Tho’ darkness sheds deep night thro’ half the year,<br>And snow invests the clime,—that clime is dear,<br>For there fair <small>LIBERTY</small> resides, and there<br>At large the native breasts the searching air,<br>Where blows the '''arctic''' tempests icy gale,<br>And famine seizes on the spermy whale,}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1968
|author=w:Robert Conquest
|chapter=A Nation in Torment
|title={{w|The Great Terror (book)|The Great Terror: Stalin's Purge of the Thirties}}
|url=https://archive.org/details/greatterrorstali0000conq/
|publisher=w:Macmillan Company
|LCCN=68-17513
|OCLC=1169910711
|OL=21272570M
|page=327
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/greatterrorstali0000conq/page/327/
|text=A medical examination determined who was to be sent on to Norilsk in the '''Arctic'''.}}
# [[extremely|Extremely]] [[cold]], [[snowy]], or [[having]] [[other]] [[properties]] of extreme winter associated with the Arctic. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1979|author=w:John Le Carré|title=Smiley's People|page=45|publisher=Folio Society|year_published=2010
|passage=‘Could you close that window, please!’ Strickland called, dialling again. ‘It's bloody '''arctic''' down this end.’}}
# Designed for use in very cold conditions. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}

====Usage notes====
* Like {{m|en|Antarctic}}, the English word was originally pronounced without {{IPAchar|/k/}}, but the spelling pronunciation has become the more common one. The "c" was originally added to the spelling for etymological reasons, and its pronunciation followed thereafter.<!--Information here accords with [[w:Arctic#cite_note-3]] as of 2023-02-21; changes to either place should accord with the other and with the references cited at the WP note.-->

