===Etymology 3===
{{back-formation|en|arms|pos=plural}}, from {{inh-lite|en|enm|armes}}, from {{der-lite|en|fro|armes}}, from {{cog-lite|la|arma||weapons}}, from {{der-lite|en|ine-pro|sc=Latnx|*h₂er-mo-}}, a suffixed form of {{m-lite|ine-pro|sc=Latnx|*h₂er-||to fit together}}, hence ultimately cognate with etymology 1.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|usually used in the plural}} A [[weapon]].
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1789
|title=w:Second Amendment to the United States Constitution
|text=A well regulated Militia, being necessary to the security of a free State, the right of the people to keep and bear '''Arms''', shall not be infringed.}}
#* {{RQ:Stevenson Treasure Island|text=The next thing I laid hold of was a brace of pistols, and as I already had a powder horn and bullets, I felt myself well supplied with '''arms'''.}}
# {{lb|en|in the plural}} {{1|heraldic}} [[bearing]]s or [[insignia]].
#: {{ux|en|The Duke's '''arms''' were a sable gryphon rampant on an argent field.}}
# {{lb|en|in the plural|obsolete}} {{1|war}}; [[hostilities]]; [[deed]]s or [[exploit]]s of war.

=====Usage notes=====
* Pubs and taverns often use this word in their names, as a reference to heraldic bearings, e.g. ''The Queen's Arms''.

