===Etymology 4===
From {{der|en|fro|bay}}, combined with aphetized form of {{m|en|abay}}; verbal form of {{m|fro|baier}}, {{m|fro|abaier}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# The excited [[howling]] of dogs when hunting or being attacked.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=c. 1588–1593| author=w:William Shakespeare| title=w:Titus Andronicus| section=act 2, scene 2| lines=1–6| passage=The hunt is up, the morn is bright and grey, / The fields are fragrant, and the woods are green. / Uncouple here, and let us make a '''bay''' / And wake the Emperor and his lovely bride, / And rouse the Prince, and ring a hunter's peal, / That all the court may echo with the noise.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} The climactic confrontation between hunting-dogs and their prey.
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} A state of being obliged to face an antagonist or a difficulty, when escape has become impossible.
#* {{RQ:Dryden Aeneis|passage=Embolden'd by despair, he stood at '''bay'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Isaac Taylor Saturday Evening|passage=The most terrible evils are just kept at '''bay''' by incessant efforts.}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|at bay}}

