====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# An instance of breaking something into two or more pieces.
#: {{syn|en|split}}
#: {{ux|en|The femur has a clean '''break''' and so should heal easily.}}
# A [[physical]] [[space]] that [[open up|opens up]] in something or between two things.
#: {{syn|en|breach|gap|space|Thesaurus:interspace|Thesaurus:hole}}
#: {{ux|en|The sun came out in a '''break''' in the clouds.}}
#: {{ux|en|He waited minutes for a '''break''' in the traffic to cross the highway.}}
# An [[interruption]] of [[continuity]]; [[departure]] from or [[rupture]] with.
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-asia-65743967|title=Pita Limjaroenrat: Thai election upstart who vows to be different|date=2023-05-29|author=Jonathan Head|work=BBC|passage=But the young activists of Move Forward outmanoeuvred the older party, and beat many of its candidates, with an imaginative, social media-based campaign offering voters a complete '''break''' with the past, and a different kind of political leadership.}}
# {{topics|en|Rest}} {{senseid|en|rest or pause, usually from work}} A [[rest]] or [[pause]], usually from [[work]].
#: {{syn|en|time-out|Thesaurus:pause}}
#: {{ux|en|Let’s take a five-minute '''break'''.}}
## {{lb|en|UK|education}} A time for students to talk or play between lessons.
##: {{syn|en|playtime<q:UK>|recess<q:US>}}
## A scheduled interval of days or weeks between periods of school instruction; a holiday.
##: {{ux|en|winter '''break''', spring '''break'''}}
# A short [[holiday]].
#: {{syn|en|day off|time off|Thesaurus:vacation}}
#: {{ux|en|a weekend '''break''' on the Isle of Wight}}
# A [[temporary]] [[split]] with a romantic partner.
#: {{ux|en|I think we need a '''break'''.}}
# An [[interval]] or [[intermission]] between two parts of a performance, for example a theatre show, broadcast, or sports game.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=December 29, 2010 |author=Chris Whyatt |title=Chelsea 1 - 0 Bolton |work=BBC |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/eng_prem/9309542.stm |passage=But they marginally improved after the '''break''' as Didier Drogba hit the post.}}
# A significant [[change]] in circumstance, [[attitude]], [[perception]], or [[focus]] of attention.
#: {{ux|en|big '''break'''}}
#: {{ux|en|lucky '''break''', bad '''break'''}}
# {{lb|en|finance}} A sudden [[fall]] in [[price]]s on the [[stock exchange]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1947|title=Reports of the Tax Court of the United States|volume=8|page=459
|passage=Following the invasion of France by the Germans in May of 1940, the securities markets experienced a '''break''' in prices.}}
# The [[beginning]] (of the morning).
#: {{syn|en|crack of dawn|Thesaurus:dawn}}
#: {{ux|en|[[daybreak|day'''break''']]}}
#: {{ux|en|at the '''break''' of day}}
# An act of [[escape|escaping]].
#: {{ux|en|make a '''break''' for it, for the door}}
#: {{ux|en|It was a clean '''break'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|prison '''break'''}}
# {{label|en|computing}} The separation between [[line]]s, [[paragraph]]s or [[page]]s of a written text.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2001|author=Nan Barber; David Reynolds|title=Office 2001 for Macintosh: The Missing Manual|page=138
|passage=No matter how much text you add above the '''break''', the text after the '''break''' will always appear at the top of a new page.}}
# {{lb|en|computing}} A [[keystroke]] or other [[signal]] that causes a [[program]] to terminate or suspend execution.
# {{lb|en|programming}} {{short for|en|breakpoint}}
# {{lb|en|British|weather}} A change, particularly the end of a spell of [[persistent]] good or bad [[weather]].
# {{lb|en|sports|_|and|_|games}}:
## {{senseid|en|tennis}} {{lb|en|tennis}} A [[game]] won by the [[receive|receiving]] [[player]](s).
## {{lb|en|billiards|snooker|pool}} The first [[shot]] in a game of [[billiards]].
## {{lb|en|snooker}} The number of points scored by one player in one visit to the table.
## {{lb|en|soccer}} The [[counter-attack]].
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=December 28, 2010|author=Owen Phillips| title=Sunderland 0 - 2 Blackpool| work=BBC| url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/eng_prem/9317216.stm| passage=Blackpool were not without their opportunities - thanks to their willingness to commit and leave men forward even when under severe pressure - and they looked very capable of scoring on the '''break'''.}}
## {{lb|en|golf}} The [[curve]] imparted to the ball's motion on the [[green]] due to slope or grass texture.
## {{lb|en|surfing}} A place where [[wave]]s break (that is, where waves [[pitch]] or [[spill]] forward creating [[white water]]).
##: {{ux|en|The final '''break''' in the Greenmount area is Kirra Point.}}
## {{lb|en|horse racing}} The start of a horse race.
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1999|author=Jay Hovdey|title=Cigar: America's Horse|page=63
|passage=Cigar was distracted at the '''break''' and let his five opponents get the jump.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=2010|author=John Alexander|title=Exotic Wagering the Winning Way|page=60
|passage=Perhaps it stumbles to its knees at the '''break''', effectively losing the race at the outset.}}
# {{lb|en|dated}} A large four-wheeled [[carriage]], having a straight body and [[calash]] top, with the driver's seat in front and the footman's behind.
# {{lb|en|equitation}} A sharp [[bit]] or [[snaffle]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1576|author=w:George Gascoigne|title=The Steele Glas
|passage=Pampered jades {{...}} which need nor '''break''' nor bit.}}
# {{lb|en|music}} A short section of music, often between verses, in which some performers stop while others continue.
#: {{ux|en|The fiddle '''break''' was amazing; it was a pity the singer came back in on the wrong note.}}
# {{lb|en|music}} The point in the musical [[scale]] at which a [[woodwind]] [[instrument]] is designed to [[overblow]], that is, to move from its lower to its upper register.
#: {{ux|en|Crossing the '''break''' smoothly is one of the first lessons the young clarinettist needs to master.}}
# {{lb|en|music}} The [[transition]] [[area]] between a [[singer]]'s [[vocal]] [[register]]s; the [[passaggio]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1862|title=The Serephina, Or, Christian Library of Church Music|author=John Winebrenner|url= https://www.google.co.uk/books/edition/The_Seraphina_Or_Christian_Library_of_Ch/IpUhrwLx2hwC?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=cross+the+break+voice+sing&pg=PA13&printsec=frontcover|page=13|passage=34. ''Of the Registers of the Voice'' - All singers have observed that there are certain parts of the Vocal Scale where a '''break''', as it is called, seldom fails to occur.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|title=The Baritone Voice: A Personal Guide to Acquiring a Superior Singing Technique|author=S. Anthony Frisella|url= https://www.google.co.uk/books/edition/The_Baritone_Voice/4wNVqRG99b4C?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=cross+the+break+voice+sing&pg=PT14&printsec=frontcover|page=14|passage=The point of division between the two vocal registers is most frequently referred to as the ''register’s '''break'''''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2018|title=The Evolving Singing Voice: Changes Across the Lifespan|author=Karen Brunssen|url= https://www.google.co.uk/books/edition/The_Evolving_Singing_Voice/phKGDwAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=cross+the+break+voice+sing&pg=PA76&printsec=frontcover|page=76|passage=Boys should continue in their high voice, across the '''break''' to the lower range, and end up with a voice that doesn’t have a '''break''' (Leck, 2009).}}
# {{lb|en|geography|mostly|in the plural}} An area along a river that features steep banks, bluffs, or gorges (e.g., {{w|Upper Missouri River Breaks National Monument}}, US).
# {{lb|en|obsolete|slang}} An [[error]]. {{defdate|late 19th–early 20th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1916|journal=The Saturday Evening Post|title=Three Kings and a Pair|author=w:Ring W. Lardner
|url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=g54xAQAAMAAJ&pg=GBS.RA10-PA18
|text="Maybe he will some day," says the Missus, and then her and Bessie pretended like they'd made a '''break''' and was embarrassed.
}}

=====Usage notes=====
* {{sense|short section of music}} The instruments that are named are the ones that carry on playing, for example a {{m|en|fiddle break}} implies that the fiddle is the most prominent instrument playing during the break.

