===Etymology 2===
{{wikipedia}}
From {{bor|en|fr|cape}}, from {{der|en|pro|capa}}, from {{der|en|LL.|cappa||cape}}. The second sense ("superhero") is metonymic from the fact that many superheroes wear capes. Likewise, the verb sense "defend, praise" alludes to the stereotypical depiction of [[superhero]]es wearing [[cape]]s when they come to people's defense. (Compare {{m|en|caped crusader}} and {{m|en|cape||a superhero}}.) {{doublet|en|capa|cappa}}.
[[Image:Pelerine1911.jpg|thumb|A young woman in a crocheted cape.]]

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A sleeveless [[garment]] or part of a garment, hanging from the neck over the back, arms, and shoulders.
#* {{RQ:Christie Autobiography|II|4|passage=Mind you, clothes were clothes in those days. [&hellip;]&nbsp; Frills, ruffles, flounces, lace, complicated seams and gores: not only did they sweep the ground and have to be held up in one hand elegantly as you walked along, but they had little '''capes''' or coats or feather boas.}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} A [[superhero]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2017|author=April Daniels|title=Dreadnought: Nemesis - Book One|publisher=Diversion Books| isbn=9781682300671| passage=Rows and rows of booths and pavilions stretch across the floor, draped with glowing holograms and shifting signs beckoning '''capes''' to try their wares. Bystander insurance. Hypertech components. Mystical ingredients. Training DVDs&nbsp;...}}

