====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To incite or attract (a [[bull]]) to [[charge]] a certain direction, by waving a cape.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=Odie Hawkins|title=The Black Matador, "Sugar"|page=140|publisher=AuthorHouse|isbn=9781481706629
|passage=“I became a novillero when I was fourteen, but I had already been going to the fields and '''caping''' bulls since I was about twelve."}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} To head or point; to keep a course.
#: ''The ship '''capes''' southwest by south.''
# To [[skin]] an animal, particularly a [[deer]].
# {{lb|en|US|slang|chiefly with "for"}} To [[defend]] or [[praise]], especially that which is unworthy.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2016|author=Ken Makin|chapter=Clinton-Trump debacle underscores gross misunderstanding of politics|title=Urban Pro Weekly|section=6 October - 12 October 2016, [https://issuu.com/urbanproweekly/docs/upw__oct_6_2016 page 5]
|passage=A lot of African-Americans believe the answer is Clinton, mostly because "she's not Trump" and because President Barack Obama is shamelessly '''caping''' for her.}}
#* '''2017''', Laila Nur, quoted in Jordan Green, "Far-right groups converge behind anti-sharia message in Raleigh", ''Triad City Beat'', 14 June - 20 June 2017, [https://issuu.com/brianclarey/docs/170614_black-lives-matter page 9]:
#*: Many times, you see white supremacist groups '''caping''' for women to mask their agenda of white nationalism.
#* '''2017''', Mindy Isser [organizer], quoted by Aubrey Whelan [journalist] in "For Philly's socialists, election wins signal momentum", ''The Philadelphia Inquirer'', 15 November 2017:
#*: "I can't believe I'm out here '''caping''' for a politician."
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=3 April 2019|author=Julian Lutz|title=Elizabeth Warren has authenticity|journal=The Hawk|publisher=Saint Joseph's University|pageurl=https://issuu.com/hawknewspaper/docs/april_3__2019_|page=8
|passage={{...}} Biden is the old man who once '''caped''' for systematic racism; {{...}}}}
#* {{seemoreCites|en}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[cover]] (as) with or like a cape.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Susan Lowell|title=Ganado Red: A Novella and Stories|publisher=w:Milkweed Editions|year=1988|page=86|isbn=0-915943-26-3|passage=The white fur that '''caped''' his neck and shoulders stood on end.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Terri Valentine|title=Outlaw’s Kiss|publisher=w:Zebra Books|year=1991|page=329|isbn=9780821733677|passage=He shook her gently, combing his fingers through her coppery curls that '''caped''' her shoulders like filigree.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Jim Schutze|title=By Two and Two: The Scandalous Story of Twin Sisters Accused of a Shocking Crime of Passion|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:William Morrow and Company|William Morrow and Company, Inc.]]|year=1995|page=62|isbn=0-688-12871-8|passage=He '''caped''' the children and draped them in long wraps, wigs, and swords, and then they all went off down the hall, around the house, and into the yard, Jack singing in his high little voice, “Let’s go off adventuring, adventuring, adventuring!”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Kate Meader|title=Hot and Bothered|publisher=Forever, {{w|Grand Central Publishing}}|year=2014|isbn=978-1-4555-9965-3|passage=Out of the corner of his eye, he saw blatant relief sketched on her face as the jacket '''caped''' her body.}}

