Page 1242 chersonese: -------- begin text --------
{{also|Chersonese}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
{{top2}}
* {{qualifier|{{lang|grc|χ}} transcribed as <nowiki><ch></nowiki>}}
** [[chersonesus]] {{qualifier|Latin form}}
** [[chersonesos]] {{qualifier|with Greek ending}}
** [[chersoness]] {{qualifier|obsolete}}
** [[chersonesse]] {{qualifier|obsolete}}
* {{qualifier|{{lang|grc|χ}} transcribed as <nowiki><kh></nowiki>}}
** [[khersonese]]
** [[khersonesos]] {{qualifier|with Greek ending}}
** [[khersonesus]] {{qualifier|with Latin ending}}
** [[khersoness]] {{qualifier|rare, obsolete}}
{{bottom}}

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|la|Chersonēsus}}, from {{uder|en|grc|χερσόνησος||[[originally]], the [[Gallipoli]] [[peninsula]]; [[later]], [[any]] [[peninsula]]}},<ref name="CAMG">''A Compendium of Ancient and Modern Geography: For the Use of Eton School'' by Aaron Arrowsmith (1831; E. Williams), [http://books.google.co.uk/books?id=xYM_AAAAIAAJ&pg=PA32 {{nowrap|page 32}}]:<br>A ''[[peninsula]]'' ({{lang|grc|[[χερσόνησος]]}} [[pæninsula]], ''i. e.'' [[pæne]] [[insula]]) or ''[[chersonese]]'', is a tract of land which is almost an island, being encompassed by water on all sides, except where it is joined to the main by a narrow neck of land; as the [[Thracian Chersonese]], the ''[[Morea]]'', and ''[[Spain]]''. The narrow ''[[neck]]'' of land, which joins a peninsula to the main, is called an ''[[Isthmus]]'' ({{lang|grc|[[ἰσθμός]]}} isthmus<sup>10</sup>) as the Isthmus of [[Corinth]], the Isthmus of ''[[Suez]]'', and the Isthmus of ''[[Darien]]''.</ref> from {{m|grc|χέρσος||dry land}} + {{m|grc|νῆσος||island}}.<ref name="OED">''Oxford English Dictionary'', {{nowrap|2nd ed.}} “[http://dictionary.oed.com/cgi/entry/50037803 chersonese, ''n.'']”. Oxford University Press (Oxford), 1989.</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈkɜːsəˌniːs/}}<ref name="OED"/>

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|geography|uncommon}} A [[peninsula]].<ref name="CAMG"/><ref name="OED"/>

====Usage notes====
Aside from dated, poetic, or rhetorical use,<ref name="OED"/> the word typically appears in English transcribing works or descriptions of classical geography: the anglicized form cherson<u>ese</u> being more common in general and generic use and the Latin form cherson<u>esus</u> appearing in the proper names of various famous peninsulas.

====Derived terms====
* ''See'' '''[[chersonesus#Derived terms|chersonesus]]'''
* {{l|en|Thracian Chersonese}} ([[Gallipoli]])

====References====
<references/>

[[Category:en:Landforms]]
-------- end text --------
