=====Translations=====
{{trans-top}}
* Walloon: {{t+|wa|Chimai}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===Etymology 2===
{{wikipedia|Lake Chimay}}
[[File:Spurious Lake Chiang Mai or Lake Chiamay as the source for rough representations of the Yangtze, Mekong, Salween and Brahmaputra rivers - a fiction first reported by the Portuguese Antonio de Faria y Sousa in 1543.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Lake Chiamay or Cunabetee on a 1692 map of the {{w|Mughal Empire}} by {{w|Vincenzo Coronelli}}.]]
Variant of earlier {{m|en|Chiamay}}, from {{der|en|pt|lago|Lago}} {{m|pt|de}} {{m|pt|Chiamay}} in João de Barros's 1553 ''Décadas da Ásia'', Dec. III, Bk. II, presumably from the {{m|pt|Chiamay}} and {{m|pt|Chiammay}} in manuscripts of {{w|Fernão Mendes Pinto}}'s ''Peregrinação'', variously referring to an imaginary lake and to the kingdom and town of [[Chiang Mai]] in northern Thailand, {{der|en|th|เชียงใหม่|tr=Chiiang Mai}}, from {{m|th|เชียง||[[walled]] [[city]]}} + {{m|th|ใหม่||[[new]]}}.

====Proper noun====
{{en-prop}}

# {{lb|en|geography|historical}} An [[imaginary]] [[lake]] [[thought]] for [[centuries]] to [[exist]] in [[southern]] [[China]] or [[northern]] [[Indochina]], [[once]] [[believe]]d to be the [[source]] of the [[Brahmaputra]] or [[Meghna]], the [[Irrawaddy]], the [[Salween]], and the [[Chao Phraya]] [[river|Rivers]].
#* '''1771''', [[:s:EB1|''Encyclopaedia Britannica'', 1st ed.]], Vol. II, p. 184:
#*: '''[[:s:Encyclopædia Britannica, First Edition/Chimay|CHIMAY]]''', the name of a great lake, lying in the province of [[Acham]], between the [[East-Indies|Eaſt-Indies]] and China.

=====Alternative forms=====
* {{l|en|Chiamay}}, {{l|en|Chiamai}}, {{l|en|Chiang Mai}}, {{l|en|Chaamay}}, {{l|en|Chiama}}, {{l|en|Cunebete}}, {{l|en|Singapamor}}, {{l|en|Jamahey}}

===References===
* Michael Pearson, "[https://www.researchgate.net/publication/327155061_'Lake_Chiamay_Asia's_mythical_mother_of_rivers'_The_Globe_Journal_of_the_Australian_and_New_Zealand_Map_Society_83_43-62_2018 Lake Chiamay: Asia's Mythical Mother of Rivers]", ''The Globe: Journal of the Australian and New Zealand Map Society'', No. 83 (2018), pp. 43 ff.

{{c|en|Mythological locations}}
