Page 1247 Chimera: -------- begin text --------
{{also|chimera|chimæra|chimaera|chiméra|Chimaera|Chiméra}}

==English==
{{wikipedia|Chimera (mythology)}}
{{wikipedia|Mount Chimaera}}
[[File:Chimera Apulia Louvre K362.jpg|thumb|250px|Chimera on a red-figure [[w:Apulia|Apulian]] plate, c.&nbsp;350&ndash;340 {{B.C.E.}} ([[w:Louvre|Musée du Louvre]])]]
[[Image:Chimera di Arezzo.jpg|thumb|250px|"{{w|Chimera of Arezzo}}": an {{w|Etruscan civilization|Etruscan}} [[bronze]]]]

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|Chimaera}}, {{l|en|chimera}}, {{l|en|chimaera}}

===Etymology===
As the mythological beast, a variant case form of {{m|en|chimera}}, q.v. As an ancient mountain, directly from {{bor|en|la|mons}} {{m|la|Chimaera||[[mount|Mount]] Chimera}}, from {{der|en|grc|Χίμαιρα}}, attested in {{w|Strabo}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/kaɪˈmɪɹə/|/kəˈmɪɹə/}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/kʌɪˈmɪəɹə/|/kɪˈmɪəɹə/}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{lb|en|Greek mythology}} A [[suppose]]d [[fire-breathing]] [[monster]] in [[Lycia]] with the [[head]] of a [[lion]], [[body]] of a [[goat]], and [[tail]] of a [[dragon]] or [[snake]], [[kill]]ed by the [[hero]] [[Bellerophon]].
#* '''1771''', [[:s:EB1|''Encyclopaedia Britannica'', 1st ed.]], Vol. II, p. 184:
#*: '''[[:s:Encyclopædia Britannica, First Edition/Chimera|CHIMERA]]''', a fabulous [[monster|monſter]]...
# {{lb|en|geography|Ancient Greece}} A [[fire]]-[[spewing]] [[mountain]] in [[Lycia]] or [[Cilicia]], [[presume]]d to be an [[ancient]] [[name]] for the [[Yanartaş]] [[region]] of [[Turkey]]'s [[Antalya]] [[province|Province]].
#* '''1771''', [[:s:EB1|''Encyclopaedia Britannica'', 1st ed.]], Vol. II, p. 184:
#*: '''[[:s:Encyclopædia Britannica, First Edition/Chimera|CHIMERA]]'''... The foundation of the fable was, that in Lycia there was a burning mountain, or [[vulcano]], of this name; that the top of this mountain was [[seldom|ſeldom]] without lions, nor the middle, which had very good [[grass|graſs]], without goats; that [[serpents|ſerpents]] bred at the bottom, which was [[marshy|marſhy]]; and that Bellerophon rendered the mountain habitable.
# {{lb|en|historical}} {{former name of|en|Himara|nodot=a}}, a [[port]] [[town]] in [[southern]] [[Albania]].
#* '''1771''', [[:s:EB1|''Encyclopaedia Britannica'', 1st ed.]], Vol. II, p. 184:
#*: '''[[:s:Encyclopædia Britannica, First Edition/Chimæra|CHIMÆRA]]''', in geography, a port town of [[Turkey|Turky]] in Europe, [[situated|ſituated]] at the entrance of the [[Gulf of Venice|gulph of Venice]], in the province of [[Epirus]], about thirty-two miles north of the city [[Corfu]], near which are the mountains of '''Chimæra''', which divide Epirus from [[Thessaly|Theſſaly]]...
# {{lb|en|historical}} {{former name of|en|Ceraunian Mountains|nodot=a}}, the [[Albanian]] [[mountain]] [[range]] near [[Himara]].

====Coordinate terms====
* {{sense|Greek myth}} {{l|en|Cerberus}}, {{l|en|Hydra}}, {{l|en|Typhon}}, {{l|en|Echidna}}

