* German: {{t+|de|Kimm|f}}
* Hungarian: {{t+|hu|gerinc}}
* Polish: {{t+|pl|obło|n}}
{{trans-bottom}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cut through the [[backbone]] of; to cut into chine pieces.
# To [[chamfer]] the ends of a [[stave]] and form the chine.

{{Webster 1913}}

===Etymology 2===
{{etymid|en|ravine}}
From {{inh|en|enm|chyne|id=crack}}, {{m|enm|chynne||crack, fissure, chasm}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ċine}}, {{m|ang|ċinu}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*kinō}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|Southern England|{{w|Vancouver}}}} A steep-sided ravine leading from the top of a cliff down to the sea.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1885|author=w:Jean Ingelow|title=A Cottage in a Chine
|passage=The cottage in a '''chine''', we were not to behold it.}}
#* {{RQ:Hollinghurst Swimming-Pool Library|page=169|passage=In the odorous stillness of the day I thought of the tracks that threaded Egdon Heath, and of benign, elderly Sandbourne, with its '''chines''' and sheltered beach-huts.}}

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|en|chine#Etymology_3}}
* {{l|en|chink#Etymology_1}}

===Etymology 3===
{{etymid|en|verb}}
From {{inh|en|enm|chynen|t=to crack, fissure, split}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ċīnan|t=to break into pieces, burst, crack}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*kīnan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*kīnaną||to split; crack; germinate; sprout}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete|}} To [[crack]], [[split]], [[fissure]], [[break]]. {{defdate|9th|16th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|The wayward son did '''chine''' his father's heart.}}
#: {{ux|en|A drought had caused the earth to '''chine''' and cranny.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1508|author=w:John Fisher|title=Treatise concernynge ... the seven penytencyall Psalms
|passage=After the erth be brent, '''chyned''' & chypped by the hete of the sonne.}}

=====Related terms=====
