Page 1251 cis-: -------- begin text --------
{{also|Appendix:Variations of "cis"}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From the {{bor|en|la|-}} preposition {{m|la|cis||on this side of}}. The earliest known sexuality-related use of the prefix in any language was in a 1914 German-language book on sexology.<ref>{{cite-book|year=1914|title=Lexikon des gesamten Sexuallebens|author=Ernst Burchard|authorlink=Ernst Burchard|lang=de}}{{quote-book|de|author=Ernst Burchard|title=Lexikon Des Gesamten Sexuallebens|page=32|pageurl=https://nbn-resolving.org/urn:nbn:de:kobv:11-713847/fragment/page=36|passage=Cisvestitismus, die Neigung, die Kleidung einer anderen Altersstufe, Volks- oder Berufsklasse des gleichen Geschlechts zum Zwecke sexueller Ent­spannung anzulegen, dem Transvestitismus verwandt (s. Verkleidungstrieb).|translation=Cisvestism, the tendency to don the clothes of a different age group, ethnic group, or profession of the same sex for the purpose of sexual relaxation, related to transvestism (see disguise instinct).|year=1914|location=Berlin|url=https://nbn-resolving.org/urn:nbn:de:kobv:11-713847|id=BV047570799}}</ref> According to the Oxford English Dictionary, the earliest use of the prefix in the context of gender in English dates from 1994.<ref>{{cite-web |url=http://public.oed.com/the-oed-today/recent-updates-to-the-oed/previous-updates/december-2015-update/new-words-notes-december-2015/ |title=New words notes December 2015 – Oxford English Dictionary |accessdate=7 November 2017 |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20171129230532/http://public.oed.com/the-oed-today/recent-updates-to-the-oed/previous-updates/december-2015-update/new-words-notes-december-2015/ |archivedate=29 November 2017 }}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/sɪs/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪs}}
* {{homophones|en|cis|sis}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-sis.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Prefix===
{{en-prefix}}

# {{lb|en|geography}} On this [[side]] of.
#: {{ant|en|trans-}}
#: {{prefixusex|en|alpine|cisalpine|gloss2=on this [the Roman] side of the Alps}}
#: {{prefixusex|en|Rhenane|cisrhenane|gloss2=on this [the speaker's] side of Rhine}}
#: {{prefixusex|en|Caucasia|Ciscaucasia}}
#: {{prefixusex|en|Jordan|Cisjordan}}
#: {{prefixusex|en|Neptunian|cis-Neptunian}}
# {{lb|en|chemistry}} {{non-gloss definition|Forming names of chemical [[compound]]s in which two [[atom]]s or [[group]]s are [[situated]] on the [[same]] side of some [[plane]] of [[symmetry]] passing through the compound.}}
#: {{prefixusex|en|diazene|cis-diazene}}
# {{lb|en|gender}} Being, or pertaining to being, [[cis#Adjective|cis]] {{gloss|[[cisgender]] or [[cissexual]]}}.
#: {{prefixusex|en|gender|cisgender}}
#: {{prefixusex|en|sexism|cissexism}}
#: {{prefixusex|en|normativity|cisnormativity}}

====Usage notes====
* In the first sense, “on this side of”, this prefix is usually attached directly to the word it modifies, or sometimes separated from it by a hyphen: {{m|en|cisrhenane}}, {{m|en|cis-Neptunian}}.
* In the gender-related sense, this prefix is attached directly to certain words, most notably {{m|en|cisgender}} and {{m|en|cissexual}} (which are almost always spelled thus, not as e.g. *''cis sexual''). In other cases, the related standalone adjective {{m|en|cis}} is used: hence one speaks of ''a cis perspective'' (not *''cisperspective''), etc. Compare {{m|en|trans-}} and {{m|en|trans}}.

====Antonyms====
* {{l|en|trans-}}
* {{l|en|ultra-}}

====Derived terms====
{{prefixsee|en}}
* {{sense|cisgender, cissexual}} {{l|en|cisman}}, {{l|en|ciswoman}}, {{l|en|cismale}}, {{l|en|cisfemale}} {{q|more commonly spaced as ''cis man'', etc.}}

====Translations====
