Page 1252 clove: -------- begin text --------
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/kləʊv/}}
* {{a|GenAm}} {{IPA|en|/kloʊv/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-clove.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|əʊv|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
[[File:Gewuerznelken.jpg|thumb|Cloves (1).]]
From {{inh|en|enm|clove}}, an alteration of earlier {{m|enm|clowe}}, borrowed from the first component of {{der|en|fro|[[clou]] (de [[girofle]])}} (modern {{cog|fr|clou de girofle}}), from {{der|en|la|clāvus||nail}} for its shape. Also see {{m|la|clāva||knotty branch, club}}. {{doublet|en|clou}}.
{{root|en|ine-pro|*(s)kleh₂w-}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable|countable}} A very [[pungent]] [[aromatic]] [[spice]], the unexpanded [[flower bud]] of the clove tree.
# {{lb|en|countable}} A {{vern|clove tree}}, of the species ''[[Syzygium aromaticum]]'' (syn. {{taxlink|Caryophyllus aromaticus|species}}), native to the [[Moluccas]] (Indonesian islands), which produces the spice.
# {{lb|en|countable}} An old English measure of weight, containing 7 [[pound]]s (3.2 kg), i.e. half a [[stone]]. {{attention|en|is this under the correct etymology?}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1843|title=The Penny Cyclopaedia of the Society for the Diffusion of Useful Knowledge|page=202
|passage=Seven pounds make a '''clove''', 2 '''cloves''' a stone, 2 stone a tod {{frac|6|1|2}} tods a wey, 2 weys a sack, 12 sacks a last. The 'Pathway' points out the etymology of the word '''cloves'''; it calls them ' ''claves'' or ''nails''.' It is to be observed here that a sack is 13 tods, and a tod 28 pounds, so that the sack is 364 pounds.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1866|author=James Edwin Thorold Rogers|title=A History of Agriculture and Prices in England|volume=1|page=169|passage=By a statute of 9 Hen. VI. it was ordained that the wey of cheese should contain 32 '''cloves''' of 7 lbs. each, i.e. 224 lbs., or 2 cwts.}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|clove camphor}}
* {{vern|clove gilliflower}}, {{vern|clove gillyflower}} (''[[Dianthus caryophyllus]]'')
* {{l|en|clove pink}} (''[[Dianthus caryophyllus]]'')

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|en|cloy}}
* {{l|en|cloying}}

