Page 1264 cornice: -------- begin text --------
==English==
[[Image:Louis Sullivan - cornice detail - Wainwright Building, Seventh + Chestnut Streets, Saint Louis, St. Louis City County, MO.jpg|thumb|right|150px|Cornice, Wainwright Building, St. Louis (Louis Sullivan)]]
[[File:Wechte.jpg|thumb|A snow cornice.]]

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|frm|corniche}} or {{der|en|it|cornice}}, from {{der|en|la|cornīx||crow}}.<ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vininn126-cornice.wav|Audio (US)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|architectural element}} A [[horizontal]] architectural element of a [[building]], projecting forward from the main [[wall]]s, originally used as a means of directing [[rainwater]] away from the building's walls.
# A [[decorative]] element applied at the topmost part of the wall of a [[room]], as with a [[crown molding]].
#* {{RQ:Landon Francesca Carrara|page=113|volume=III|text=That ceiling was covered with square compartments,[...] It was supported by a gilded '''cornice''', carved into a thousand curious shapes and emblems, among which the horned wolf, the crest of the Avonleigh family, was conspicuous.}}
#* {{RQ:Doyle Land of Mist|passage=Challenger looked up at the '''cornice''' and round at the skirting.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1963|author=w:Margery Allingham
|title=w:The China Governess
|chapter=1|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL2004261W
|passage=The half-dozen pieces […] were painted white and carved with festoons of flowers, birds and cupids. [&hellip;]&nbsp; The bed was the most extravagant piece.&nbsp; Its graceful cane halftester rose high towards the '''cornice''' and was so festooned in carved white wood that the effect was positively insecure, as if the great couch were trimmed with icing sugar.}}
# A decorative element at the topmost portion of certain pieces of [[furniture]], as with a [[highboy]].
# {{lb|en|geography|mountaineering}} An [[overhanging]] edge of [[snow]] on a [[ridge]] or the [[crest]] of a [[mountain]] and along the sides of [[gully|gullies]].
#: {{syn|en|snow cornice}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|title=Across Peaks & Passes in Kumaun Himalaya|author=Harish Kapadia|chapter=Ascents in the Panch Chuli Group|location=New Delhi|publisher=Indus Publishing Company|isbn=8173870969|page={{gbooks|nD95LUfpgzgC|136|cornice}}|passage=Looking to the east we could see Api and the mountains of west Nepal, shapely snow peaks in the distance, while in the immediate foreground, much lower but still dramatic, were the peaks of Panch Chuli IV and V (III was hidden by the lip of a huge '''cornice'''), Telkot and Nagling, all of them unclimbed, all steep and challenging.}}

====Derived terms====
{{der2|en|cornice ring|snow cornice}}

====Translations====
{{trans-top|horizontal architectural element}}
* Belarusian: {{t|be|карні́з|m|sc=Cyrl}}, {{t|be|гзымс|m|sc=Cyrl}}
* Bulgarian: {{t+|bg|корни́з|m}}
* Catalan: {{t+|ca|cornisa|f}}
