Page 1275 degree: -------- begin text --------
{{also|dégrée|dégréé|Degree}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|degre}}, borrowed from {{der|en|fro|degré}} (French: {{m|fr|degré}}), itself from {{der|en|la|gradus}}, with the prefix {{m|fro|de-}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|dĭgrē'}}, {{IPA|en|/dɪˈɡɹiː/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-degree.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|en-uk-degree.ogg|Audio (UK)}}
* {{rhymes|en|iː|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|education}} A stage of proficiency or qualification in a course of study, now especially an [[award]] bestowed by a [[university]] or, in some countries, a [[college]], as a certification of [[academic]] [[achievement]]. (In the United States, can include secondary schools.) {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|She has two bachelor's '''degrees''' and is studying towards a master's '''degree'''.}}
# {{senseid|en|geometry}} {{lb|en|geometry}} A unit of [[measurement]] of [[angle]] equal to {{frac|1|360}} of a [[circle]]'s circumference. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|A right angle is a ninety-'''degree''' angle.}}
#: {{ux|en|Most humans have a field of vision of almost 180 '''degrees'''.}}
# {{senseid|en|physics}} {{lb|en|physics}} A unit of measurement of [[temperature]] on any of several [[scales]], such as [[Celsius]] or [[Fahrenheit]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|212 '''degrees''' Fahrenheit is equivalent to 100 '''degrees''' Celsius.}}
#: {{ux|en|Water boils at 100 '''degrees''' Celsius.}}
# {{lb|en|algebra}} The sum of the [[exponent]]s of a [[term]]; the [[order]] of a [[polynomial]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|A quadratic polynomial is a polynomial of '''degree''' 2.}}
# {{lb|en|algebra|field theory}} The [[dimensionality]] of a [[field extension]].
#: {{ux|en|The set of [[complex number]]s constitutes a field extension of '''degree''' 2 over the [[real numbers]].}}
#: {{ux|en|The [[Galois field]] <math>\operatorname{GF}(125) = \operatorname{GF}(5^3)</math> has '''degree''' 3 over its subfield <math>\operatorname{GF}(5).</math>}}
# {{lb|en|graph theory}} The number of [[edge]]s that a [[vertex]] takes part in; a [[valency]].
# {{lb|en|logic}} The number of [[logical connective]]s in a formula.
# {{lb|en|surveying}} The [[curvature]] of a circular arc, expressed as the angle [[subtend]]ed by a fixed length of arc or chord.
# {{senseid|en|geography}} {{lb|en|geography}} A unit of [[measurement]] of [[latitude]] and [[longitude]] which together identify a [[location]] on the [[Earth]]'s surface.
# {{lb|en|grammar}} Any of the stages (like positive, comparative, superlative, elative) in the comparison of an adjective or an adverb.
# {{lb|en|obsolete|outside|heraldry}} A [[step]] on a set of [[stairs]]; the [[rung]] of a ladder. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
# An individual step, or stage, in any process or scale of values. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
# A stage of [[rank]] or privilege; social standing. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|format=full|Luke|20|21|folio=cx|passage=And they axed hym ſayinge: Maſter&nbsp;/ we knowe that thou ſayest&nbsp;/ and teacheſt ryght&nbsp;/ nether conſidereſt thou eny mãnes '''degre'''&nbsp;/ but teacheſt the waye of god truely.}}
#* {{RQ:Jack Straw|I|18|But when ''Adam'' delued, and ''Eue'' ſpan,<br>VVho was then a Gentleman.<br>Brethren, brethren, it were better to haue this communitie,<br>Then to haue this difference in '''degrees''':<br>The landlord his rent, the lawyer his fees.<br>So quickly the poore mans ſubſtance is ſpent {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|genealogy}} A ‘step’ in genealogical [[descent]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2002|author={{w|Colin Jones (historian)|Colin Jones}}|title=The Great Nation|page=140|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2003
|passage=Louis created the École militaire in Paris in 1751, in which 500 scholarships were designated for noblemen able to prove four '''degrees''' of noble status.}}
# {{lb|en|now|_|rare}} One's relative state or experience; [[way]], [[manner]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1851|author=w:Herman Melville|title=w:Moby-Dick|passage=If they but knew it, almost all men in their '''degree''', some time or other, cherish very nearly the same feelings towards the ocean with me.}}
# {{senseid|en|amount or extent}} The amount that an entity possesses a certain property; relative [[intensity]], [[extent]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|To what '''degree''' do the two accounts of the accident concur?}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=1|passage=In the old days, to my commonplace and unobserving mind, he gave no evidences of genius whatsoever. He never read me any of his manuscripts,&nbsp;[&hellip;], and therefore my lack of detection of his promise may in some '''degree''' be pardoned.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=March 11 2022|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2022/mar/11/chelsea-in-crisis-but-there-is-no-will-to-leave-club-on-their-knees-roman-abramovich|author=David Hytner|title=Chelsea are in crisis but there is no will to leave club on their knees|journal=w:The Guardian
|passage=Then there are the sums that Abramovich would be permitted to invest within the parameters of the profit and sustainability rules – £105m over a rolling three-year period. That, plainly, has stopped and so, to repeat, it is imperative that the transfer of ownership happens with a '''degree''' of speed.}}

====Usage notes====
* A person who is engaged in a course of study leading to the earning of a degree can be described (in the present progressive tense) as "doing a degree" in British English, and as "getting a degree" in American English. For example, in American English, ''"She is currently '''getting''' her master's degree at State University."'' In British English, ''"I am still confused about when to use 'an' instead of 'a'. Is it an hour or a hour, and if someone is '''doing''' a master's degree in arts, is it an MA or a MA?"'' ([https://web.archive.org/web/20071009132719/http://www.askoxford.com/asktheexperts/faq/aboutgrammar/anma?view=uk Ask Oxford.Com - Ask the Experts - Frequently Asked Questions (Grammar)]).

