# {{lb|en|geography}} The number of people under the age of 15 and over the age of 64 compared to the number of people active in the labor force.
-------- end text --------
Page 1277 depression: -------- begin text --------
{{also|Depression|dépression}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|depression}}, {{m|enm|depressioun}}, from {{der|en|fro|depression}}, from {{der|en|la|dēpressiō}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/dɪˈpɹɛʃən/}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/dəˈpɹɛʃ(ə)n/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-depression.ogg|Audio (GA)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛʃən|s=3}}
* {{hyphenation|en|de|pres|sion}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|psychology|usually|uncountable}} A state of mind producing serious, long-term lowering of enjoyment of life or inability to visualize a happy future.
#: {{ux|en|I used to suffer from '''depression''', but now I'm mostly content with my life.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=William Styron|title=Why Primo Levi Need Not Have Died|work=The New York Times|date=1988-12-19|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1988/12/19/opinion/why-primo-levi-need-not-have-died.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=It is that Mr. Levi's death could not be dissociated from the major '''depression''' with which he was afflicted, and that indeed his suicide proceeded directly from that illness.}}
# {{lb|en|psychology|countable}} A period of low [[morale]] or [[unhappiness]] (a period of experiencing the above-mentioned state of mind) which lasts longer than several weeks and may include [[ideation]] of self-inflicted injury or [[suicide]].
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2011-02-28|author=David J. Miklowitz|title=The Bipolar Disorder Survival Guide, Second Edition: What You and Your Family Need to Know|publisher=Guilford Press|isbn=9781609180140|page=106
|text={{...}} your illness reflects the textbook description of bipolar disorder (euphoric, grandiose, manic highs followed by deep '''depressions''',{{nb...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2014-09-16|author=Andrew Solomon|title=The Noonday Demon: An Atlas Of Depression|publisher=Simon and Schuster|isbn=9781451611038|page=73
|text=His first major breakdown occurred when his son was five years old; he continued to go to pieces periodically, with a particularly deep '''depression''' that lasted from the time Bill was in sixth grade until the time he finished junior high{{nb...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2016-01-14|author=Danuta Wasserman|title=Suicide: An unnecessary death|publisher=Oxford University Press|isbn=9780191026843
|text=Many alcoholics who die from suicide suffer from deep '''depressions''' that are quite frequently protracted,{{nb...}}}}
# {{lb|en|geography}} An area that is lower in topography than its surroundings.
#* {{RQ:Jefferies Amateur Poacher|chapter=1|passage=It was not far from the house; but the ground sank into a '''depression''' there, and the ridge of it behind shut out everything except just the roof of the tallest hayrick. As one sat on the sward behind the elm, with the back turned on the rick and nothing in front but the tall elms and the oaks in the other hedge, it was quite easy to fancy it the verge of the prairie with the backwoods close by.}}
# {{lb|en|meteorology}} An area of lowered air pressure that generally brings moist weather, sometimes promoting [[hurricane]]s and [[tornado]]es.
# {{lb|en|economics}} A period of major economic contraction.
# {{lb|en|economics|US}} Four consecutive quarters of negative, real [[GDP]] [[growth]]. See [[NBER]].
#: {{ux|en|The Great '''Depression''' was the worst financial event in US history.}}
# The act of lowering or pressing something down.
#: '''''Depression''' of the lever starts the machine.''
# {{lb|en|biology|physiology}} A lowering, in particular a reduction in a particular biological variable or the function of an organ, in contrast to [[elevation]].

