Page 1278 desakota: -------- begin text --------
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
Coined around 1990 by urban researcher Terry McGee, from Indonesian ''desa'', village, and ''kota'', city.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|geography}} An area in the extended surroundings of a large city, especially in Southeast Asia, in which urban and agricultural forms of land use and settlement coexist and are intensively intermingled.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=September 24, 2015|author=|title=Evidence for the Convergence Model: The Emergence of Highly Pathogenic Avian Influenza (H5N1) in Viet Nam|journal=PLOS ONE|url=http://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0138138|doi=10.1371/journal.pone.0138138|volume=|issue=|pages=
|passage=McGee identified six characteristics of '''desakota''' regions: 1) a large population of smallholder cultivators; 2) an increase in non-agricultural activities; 3) extreme fluidity and mobility of population; 4) a mixture of land uses, agriculture, cottage industries, suburban development; 5) increased participation of the female labor force; and 6) “grey-zones”, where informal and illegal activities group.}}
-------- end text --------
Page 1279 desire line: -------- begin text --------
==English==
{{was wotd|2017|September|1}}

===Pronunciation===
[[File:Desired Path in Barcelona.JPG|thumb|A desire line on the left of a [[paved]] [[path]] in Parc Central de Nou Barris in [[Barcelona]], [[Spain]]]]

* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/dɪˈzaɪə laɪn/}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/dɪˈzaɪɹ laɪn/|/di-/|/-ˈzaɪɚ/}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-desire line.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{hyphenation|en|de|si|re line}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[path]] that [[pedestrian#Noun|pedestrians]] or [[vehicle]]s take [[informally]] rather than taking a [[sidewalk]] or set [[route]], for example, a well-[[worn]] [[ribbon]] of [[dirt]] [[cut across|cutting across]] a [[patch#Noun|patch]] of [[grass#Noun|grass]], or a path in the [[snow#Noun|snow]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Missouri State Highway Department; [[w:Federal Highway Administration|Public Roads Administration]], {{w|Federal Works Agency}} [United States]|title=A Traffic Survey of St. Louis Metropolitan Area|location=[Jefferson, Mo.]|publisher=The Department|year=1947|page=24, column 2|oclc=7419242|passage=The grouping of like trips for all passenger cars, taxi-cabs and trucks resulted in 3580 '''desire lines''', varying in volume from one trip to 5200 trips. It is obvious that if all the '''desire lines''' were illustrated graphically on one chart the result would be a maze of lines that would be confusing.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Thomas Frick|title=Rebuilding Central Park|journal=[[w:MIT Technology Review|Technology Review]]|location=Cambridge, Mass.|publisher=Association of Alumni and Alumnae of the {{w|Massachusetts Institute of Technology}}|month=August|year=1987|issn=0040-1692|oclc=985742712|passage=Study participants also drew charts of pedestrian traffic to take note of what are delightfully termed "'''desire lines'''"&nbsp;– paths actually made by walkers as opposed to those created on the drawing board.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Patricia Leigh Brown|title=Whose sidewalk is it, anyway?|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2003/01/05/weekinreview/ideas-trends-whose-sidewalk-is-it-anyway.html|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20150527225906/http://www.nytimes.com/2003/01/05/weekinreview/ideas-trends-whose-sidewalk-is-it-anyway.html|archivedate=27 May 2015|newspaper=w:The New York Times|date=5 January 2003|passage=In areas with no sidewalks, beaten-down paths in the grass, known as "'''desire lines'''" in planning-speak, indicate yearning, said John La Plante, the chief traffic engineer for {{w|T. Y. Lin International}}, an engineering firm.}}

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|desire path}}

