{{rfc|en}}

===Etymology===
{{blend|en|Dixie|Tornado Alley}}. {{coin|en|Allen Pearson|in=1971|occ=meteorologist|nat=American|nobycat=1}}. Modelled after {{m|en|Tornado Alley}}. From being in {{m|en|Dixie||U.S. South}} with a concentration of {{l|en|tornado}} strikes

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|Q3032600}}{{lb|en|US|meteorology}} {{place|en|region|of the [[Gulf of Mexico]] coast|c/USA}} which has a large number of tornado occurrences.

====Usage notes====
Depending on the definition used for {{m|en|Tornado Alley}}, Dixie Alley is either a subregion of Tornado Alley, or a region neighboring the more restrictive versions of Tornado Alley. Depending on the authority defining the region, Dixie Alley may reach the Atlantic coast, in which case {{m|en|Carolina Alley}} is a subregion instead of being a neighboring region.

