Page 1298 ellipsoid: -------- begin text --------
{{also|Ellipsoid}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{suffix|en|ellipse|oid}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|mathematics|geometry}} A [[surface]], all of whose [[cross section]]s are [[elliptic]] or [[circular]] (including the [[sphere]]), that generalises the [[ellipse]] and in [[Cartesian coordinates]] (''x, y, z'') is a [[quadric]] with equation ''x<sup>2</sup>/a<sup>2</sup>'' + ''y<sup>2</sup>/b<sup>2</sup>'' + ''z<sup>2</sup>/c<sup>2</sup>'' = 1. [[Category:en:Surfaces]]
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|author=John Michael Hollas|title=Basic Atomic and Molecular Spectroscopy|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=VKk_XmhG9CsC&pg=PA133&dq=%22ellipsoid%22%7C%22ellipsoids%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwi7lJu3hNrNAhVElpQKHQ6XCZMQ6AEIxAEwHQ#v=onepage&q=%22ellipsoid%22%7C%22ellipsoids%22&f=false|page=133
|passage=Polarizability can be imagined as a three-dimensional '''ellipsoid''' centred on the centre of the molecule, as shown in Figure 10.4.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=Alfred Leick|title=GPS Satellite Surveying|edition=3rd|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=4qE6xYjYSHgC&pg=PA367&dq=%22ellipsoid%22%7C%22ellipsoids%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwi7lJu3hNrNAhVElpQKHQ6XCZMQ6AEIhAEwEQ#v=onepage&q=%22ellipsoid%22%7C%22ellipsoids%22&f=false|page=367
|passage=Because only '''ellipsoids''' of rotation have been adopted in practical geodesy and surveying and triaxial '''ellipsoids''' have been limited to theoretical studies, we will use the term '''''ellipsoid''''' for brevity to mean '''ellipsoid''' of rotation.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Jan Van Sickle|title=Basic GIS Coordinates|edition=2nd|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=LmXMBQAAQBAJ&pg=PA73&dq=%22ellipsoid%22%7C%22ellipsoids%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwi7lJu3hNrNAhVElpQKHQ6XCZMQ6AEIPTAF#v=onepage&q=%22ellipsoid%22%7C%22ellipsoids%22&f=false|page=73
|passage=As mentioned before, modern geodetic datums rely on the surfaces of geocentric '''ellipsoids''' to approximate the surface of the earth.}}
# {{lb|en|geography}} Such a surface used as a [[model]] of the shape of the [[earth]].
#: {{ux|en|Here the [[geoid]] is thirty meters below the '''ellipsoid'''.}}

====Usage notes====
The general case, with [[semiaxis|semiaxes]] ''a, b'' and ''c'' all different, is a {{m|en|triaxial ellipsoid}} (more rarely, {{m|en|scalene ellipsoid}}). If two are the same, say ''b = c'', the result is an {{m|en|ellipsoid of revolution}}, which may be {{m|en|oblate}} (if ''a < b'') or {{m|en|prolate}} (''a > b''). The [[degenerate]] case ''a = b = c'' is a [[sphere]]. An {{m|en|ellipsoid of revolution}} is also called a {{m|en|spheroid}}.

====Hypernyms====
* {{l|en|quadric}} surface, quadric

