===Etymology 2===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*pel- (skin)}}
From {{inh|en|enm|fell}}, {{m|enm|fel}}, {{m|enm|vel}}, from {{inh|en|ang|fel}}, {{m|ang|fell|t=hide, skin, pelt}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*fell}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*fellą}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*pel-|*pél-no-|skin, animal hide}}.

See  also {{cog|fy|fel}}, {{cog|nl|vel}}, {{cog|de|Fell}}, {{cog|la|pellis||skin}}, {{cog|lt|plėnė||skin}}, {{cog|ru|плена́||pelt}}, {{cog|sq|plah||to cover}}, {{cog|grc|πέλλᾱς||skin}}. Related to {{m|en|film}}, {{m|en|felt}}, {{m|en|pell}}, and {{m|en|pelt}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|pelt}} An animal [[skin]], [[hide]], [[pelt]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare As You Like It|3 |3 |line=35|text=Why, we are still handling our ewes, and their '''fells''', you know, are greasy.}}
# Human skin {{q|now only as a metaphorical use of previous sense}}.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=c. 1390|author=w:William Langland|title=Piers Plowman|section=I
|passage=For he is fader of feith · fourmed ȝow alle / Bothe with '''fel''' and with face.}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|fellmaker}}

