* Finnish: {{t+|fi|talja}}, {{t+|fi|vuota}}
* Norwegian: {{t+|no|fell|m}}
* Swedish: {{t+|sv|fäll|c}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===Etymology 3===
From {{inh|en|enm|fell}}, {{m|enm|felle|t=hill, mountain}}, from {{der|en|non|fell}}, {{m|non|fjall||rock, mountain}}, compare {{cog|nb|fjell}} 'mountain', {{cog|da|fjeld}} 'mountain', from {{der|en|gem-pro|*felzą}}, {{m|gem-pro|*fel(e)zaz}}, {{m|gem-pro|*falisaz}} (compare {{cog|de|Felsen}} 'boulder, cliff', {{cog|gml|vels}} 'hill, mountain'), from {{der|en|ine-pro|*pels-}}; compare {{cog|ga|aill||boulder, cliff}}, {{cog|grc|πέλλα|t=stone}}, {{cog|ps|پرښه||tr=parṣ̌a|rock, rocky ledge}}, {{cog|sa|पाषाण||tr=pāşāņá|stone}}. [[doublet|Doublet]] of ''[[fjeld]]''.

[[File:2014-07-26 Padjelantaleden, Sweden 5166.jpg|thumb|right|Typical fells in Scandinavia.]]

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|ridge}} {{lb|en|archaic|_|outside|_|UK}} A rocky ridge or chain of [[mountain]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1886|author=Amelia Edith Huddleston Barr| title=The Squire of Sandal-Side : A Pastoral Romance| url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/16258/16258-h/16258-h.htm| passage=Every now and then the sea calls some farmer or shepherd, and the restless drop in his veins gives him no peace till he has found his way over the hills and '''fells''' to the port of Whitehaven, and gone back to the cradling bosom that rocked his ancestors.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1937|author=J.R.R. Tolkien|title=The Hobbit|passage=The dwarves of yore made mighty spells,&nbsp;/ While hammers fell like ringing bells,&nbsp;/ In places deep, where dark things sleep,&nbsp;/ In hollow halls beneath the '''fells.'''}}
#* {{quote-book |en |year=1970 |author=James Herriot |title=If Only They Could Talk |passage=I got out and from where I stood, high at the head, I could see all of the strangely formed cleft in the hills, its steep sides grooved and furrowed by countless streams feeding the boisterous Halden Beck which tumbled over its rocky bed far below. Down there, were trees and some cultivated fields, but immediately behind me the wild country came crowding in on the bowl where the farmhouse lay. Halsten Pike, Alstang, Birnside—the huge '''fells''' with their barbarous names were very near.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1971|author=Catherine Cookson|title=The Dwelling Place|passage=She didn't know at first why she stepped off the road and climbed the bank on to the '''fells;''' it wasn't until she found herself skirting a disused quarry that she realised where she was making for, and when she reached the place she stood and gazed at it. It was a hollow within an outcrop of rock, not large enough to call a cave but deep enough to shelter eight people from the rain, and with room to spare.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|_|outside|_|UK}} A wild [[field]] or upland moor.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1612|author=w:Michael Drayton|title=w:Poly-Olbion|section=song 11 p. 174|sectionurl=http://poly-olbion.exeter.ac.uk/the-text/full-text/song-11/
|passage=As over Holt and Heath, as thorough Frith and '''Fell''';<!--glossed Lowe coppis in margin-->}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1948|month=March and April|author=O. S. Nock|title=Scottish Night Mails of the L.M.S.R.—2|journal=Railway Magazine|page=77|text=The night continued beautifully clear and fine, and as we came into the '''fell''' country the outlines of the hills showed up dark against the starlit sky.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2022 November 2|author=Paul Bigland|title=New trains, old trains, and splendid scenery|journal=RAIL|issue=969|page=57|text=And there are few better ways to enjoy the rugged bleakness of the '''fells''' than from a nice warm train, especially when the weather's constantly changing as the day slips away.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=29 June 2023|journal=Metro|location=London|page=15|column=3|passage=An artist dubbed the Borrowdale Banksy has created this slate work on a Lake District '''fell''' after past efforts were vandalised.}}

