===Etymology 4===
From {{inh|en|enm|fel}}, {{m|enm|fell|t=strong, fierce, terrible, cruel, angry}}, from  {{inh|en|ang|*fel}}, {{m|ang|*felo}}, {{m|ang|*fæle|t=cruel, savage, fierce}} (only in compounds, {{l|ang|wælfel|wæl''fel''|gloss=bloodthirsty}}, {{l|ang|ealfelu|eal''felo''|gloss=evil, baleful}}, {{l|ang|ælfæle|æl''fæle''|gloss=very dire}}, etc.), from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*fali}}, {{m|gmw-pro|*falu}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*faluz|t=wicked, cruel, terrifying}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*pol-|t=to pour, flow, swim, fly}}. Cognate with {{cog|ofs|fal|t=cruel}}, {{cog|dum|fel|t=wrathful, cruel, bad, base}}, {{cog|nds-de|fell|t=rash, swift}}, {{cog|da|fæl|t=disgusting, hideous, ghastly, grim}}. Compare also {{cog|gmh|vālant|t=imp}}. See {{m|en|felon}}.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|er}}

# Of a [[strong]] and [[cruel]] nature; [[eager]] and [[unsparing]]; [[grim]]; [[fierce]]; [[ruthless]]; [[savage]].
#: {{ux|en|[[one fell swoop|one '''fell''' swoop]]}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-3|II|vi|text={{...}}While we devise '''fell''' tortures for thy faults.}}
#* {{RQ:Butler Hudibras|part=1|canto=2|text=And many a serpent of '''fell''' kind, / With wings before, and stings behind}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1862|title=Quarterly Journal of the Geological Society of London|passage={{...}} but if it be solitary with the position of an incisor, will it even then bear out Professor Owen's hypothesis, that ''Thylacoleo'', which he infers to have been one of “the '''fellest''' and most destructive of predatory beasts, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1892|author=w:James Yoxall| chapter=5| title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL10504990W The Lonely Pyramid]| passage=The desert storm was riding in its strength; the travellers lay beneath the mastery of the '''fell''' simoom. Whirling wreaths and columns of burning wind, rushed around and over them.}}
#* {{RQ:Wodehouse Jeeves in the Offing|chapter=XIX|passage=No words had been exchanged between Upjohn and self on the journey out, but the glimpses I had caught of his face from the corner of the eyes had told me that he was grim and resolute, his supply of the milk of human kindness plainly short by several gallons. No hope, it seemed to me, of turning him from his '''fell''' purpose.}}
# {{lb|en|UK|_|dialectal|Scotland}} Strong and [[fiery]]; [[biting]]; [[keen]]; [[sharp]]; [[pungent]]
# {{lb|en|UK|_|dialectal|Scotland}} Very [[large]]; [[huge]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[eager|Eager]]; [[earnest]]; [[intent]].
#* {{RQ:Pepys Diary|VI|15 January 1667
|passage=I am so '''fell''' to my business.}}

