===Etymology 7===

====Verb====
{{head|en|verb form}}

# {{infl of|en|fall||spast}}
# {{lb|en|now|_|colloquial}} {{past participle of|en|fall|nocat=1}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1650|publisher=Richard Cotes|location= London|title=A New Light of Alchymie: Taken Out of the Fountaine of Nature, and Manuall Experience{{...}}|author={{w|Sendivogius|Micheel Sandivogius}}|translator=J. F.|page=121|url=https://books.google.co.nz/books?id=DexeAAAAcAAJ |passage= For I have heard that my Enemies have '''fell''' into that ſnare which they laid for mee. They which would have taken away my life have loſt their own;{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1796|location= London|title= The Life and Remarkable Conversion of T. Bennett, Etc. [Written by Himself.] |author=Thomas Bennett |isbn=9780857901446|page=31|url=https://books.google.co.nz/books?id=DexeAAAAcAAJ |passage=I ſhould have '''fell''' overboard, or been killed by the enemy ; for having ſo many things to carry along with me, which I knew not how to uſe{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=3 October 2013|publisher=w:Faber & Faber|title= Collected Stories |author=John McGahern|isbn= 9780571313105 |page=147|url=https://books.google.co.nz/books?id=4QhnAgAAQBAJ|passage= And when it got to ten past I said you must have '''fell''' in with company, but I was beginning to get worried.' 'You know I never fall in with company,' he protested irritably. 'I always leave the Royal at ten to, never a minute more nor less.'}}

