Page 1334 glaciology: -------- begin text --------
==English==
{{was wotd|2017|December|8}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*gel-}}
[[File:Fedchenko Glacier.jpg|thumb|upright|A {{w|Landsat 7}} [[satellite]] image of the {{w|Fedchenko Glacier}} in [[Tajikistan]]. At a length of 77 kilometres (48 miles), it is the longest [[glacier]] not in a [[polar]] [[region]].]]

From {{der|en|la|glaciēs||ice}} (from {{der|en|ine-pro|*gel-||to be cold; to freeze}}) {{suffix|en||ology|t2=study of a particular subject}}.<ref>{{R:Lexico}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˌɡleɪsɪˈɒlədʒi/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-glaciology.wav |Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˌɡleɪʃiˈɑləd͡ʒi/}}
* {{hyphenation|en|gla|ci|o|lo|gy}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|geography}} The [[study#Noun|study]] of [[ice#Noun|ice]] and its [[effect#Noun|effect]] on the [[landscape#Noun|landscape]], especially the study of [[glacier]]s. {{defdate|from late 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=W. S.-Y.|chapter=Greenland|editor=w:Thomas Stewart Traill|title=[[w:Encyclopædia Britannica|The Encyclopædia Britannica, or Dictionary of Arts, Sciences, and General Literature]]|edition=8th|location=Edinburgh|publisher=[[w:A & C Black|Adam and Charles Black]]|year=1856|volume=XI|page=40|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Gjw6AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA40|column=2|oclc=162676963|passage=From this high position in latitude, explorations (furthered for a time by the effective aid of their Esquimaux dogs) were perseveringly made, and rewarded by results of the most interesting nature in geography, hydrography, and '''''glaciology'''''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=L. A. Rasmussen|chapter=Discussion|title=Surface Velocity Variations of the Lower Part of Columbia Glacier, Alaska, 1977–1981|series=[[w:United States Geographical Survey|U.S. Geological Survey]] Professional Paper|seriesvolume=1258-H|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=[[w:United States Government Publishing Office|United States Government Printing Office]]|year=1989|page=H28|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=CMFRAQAAMAAJ&pg=RA3-PA28|oclc=165507411|passage=Until now, aerial photography had been used in '''glaciology''' only for mapping the glacier surface and the surface velocity field; other, much more expensive, methods have been used to determine the bed and mass balance.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:John T. Andrews (geologist)|J[ohn] T. Andrews]]|title=Glacial Systems: An Approach to Glaciers and Their Environments|series=Environmental Systems|location=North Scituate, Mass.|publisher=Duxbury Press|year=1975|page=56|oclc=1323894|passage=This general approach offers the possibility of reconstructing past '''glaciologies''' for a situation where (1) the outline of the glacier can be reconstructed, (2) the ELA [equilibrium line altitude] can be determined, (3) the area under the ELA can be measured, and (4) there is some information on the winter balance and ablation gradient of the former glacier.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Peter J. Robinson; w:Ann Henderson-Sellers|chapter=Moisture in the Atmosphere|title=Contemporary Climatology|edition=2nd|location=Abingdon, Oxon.; New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Routledge|year=1999|year_published=2014|page=56|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=812gBAAAQBAJ&pg=PA56|isbn=978-0-582-27631-4|passage=In order to understand and predict the actions of water in the climate system it is useful to think of the water as being part of a distinct system, sometimes called the ''hydrological cycle''{{nb...}}. A complete understanding of this system would {{...}} require excursions into geomorphology, pedology, botany, '''glaciology''', oceanography and, if human structures are included as part of the Earth's surface, civil engineering.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Adam Johnson (writer)|Adam Johnson]]|chapter=5|title=w:Parasites Like Us|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Viking Books|year=2003|isbn=978-0-670-03240-2|location2=London|publisher2=Black Swan|year2=2014|page2=152|pageurl2=https://books.google.com/books?id=d7XmAgAAQBAJ&pg=PA152|isbn2=978-1-78416-008-1|passage=I was leafing through deep-core '''glaciology''' results from Greenland's mid-rift when the telephone rang. It droned on forever before it finally quit and I could concentrate again. The Greenland data confirmed all the other studies: the earth suffered ninety thousand years of ice-age weather, then ten thousand years of warm, in a loop that repeated over and over, as far back as there was ice to record it.}}

