===Etymology 2===
The verb is derived from {{inh|en|enm|gorgen||to eat greedily; to gorge}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=gorǧen|pos=v|id=MED19096|accessdate=27 March 2019}}</ref> a borrowing from {{der|en|fro|gorger}}, {{m|fro|gorgier}} (modern {{cog|fr|gorger||to eat greedily; to gorge}}), from {{m|fro|gorge||throat}}; ''see further'' at [[#Etymology 1|etymology 1]].<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=80107|date=1900}}</ref>

The noun is derived from the verb.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=''n.{{sup|3}}''|noformat=yes|id=80106|date=1900}}</ref>

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive|reflexive}} To [[stuff]] the [[#English|gorge]] or [[gullet]] with [[food]]; to [[eat]] [[greedily]] and in [[large]] [[quantity|quantities]]. {{+preo|en|on}}
#: {{ux|en|They '''gorged''' themselves on chocolate and cake.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|entry=ANGLING|entryurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=6ItZAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA8-IA6|title=The Sportsman’s Dictionary: Or, The Country Gentleman’s Companion, in All Rural Recreations:{{nb...|With Full and Particular Instructions for Hawking, Hunting, Fowling, Setting, Fishing, Racing, Riding, Cocking. With the Method of Breeding, Curing, Dieting, and Ordering of Horses, Dogs, Pigeons, Cocks, &c. Extracted from the Most Celebrated English and French Authors, Ancient, and Modern: With Large Improvements, Made by Several Gentlemen Well Experienced in These Noble Exercises. Illustrated with Near Thirty Copper-plates, Representing the Different Kinds of Nets, Engines, and Traps, that are Made Use of in Taking All Sorts of Game.}}|location=London|publisher=Printed for C. Hitch,{{nb...|at the Red Lion}}, and C. Davis,{{nb...|both in Pater-Noster-Row}}; and S. Austen,{{nb...|at the Angel and Bible in St. Paul’s Church-Yard.}}|year=1735|volume=I|oclc=642366102|passage=[I]f the preceding night prove dark and cloudy, the ſucceeding day, will be no good day to angle in, unleſs it be for ſmall fiſh; for at ſuch time the larger prey abroad for the leſſer; who by inſtinct knowing the danger, hide themſelves till the morning; and having faſted all night, become then very hungry while the larger having '''gorged''' themſelves, lie abſconded all the day.}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Redgauntlet|volume=III|chapter=Narrative of Darsie Latimer, Continued|page=200|passage="Friend," he said, after watching him for some minutes, "if thou '''gorgest''' thyself in this fashion, thou wilt assuredly choak. Wilt thou not take a draught out of my cup to help down all that dry meat?"}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Janet L. Davies; Ellen H. Janosik|chapter=Adaptational Variations and Disruptions|title=Mental Health and Psychiatric Nursing: A Caring Approach|location=Boston, Mass.|publisher=[[w:Jones & Bartlett Learning|Jones and Bartlett Publishers]]|year=1991|section=part 2 (Variations and Disruptions in Mental Health)|page=359|pageurl=https://books.google.com.sg/books?id=ShKgSO8U3-QC&pg=PA359|column=1|isbn=978-0-86720-442-1|passage=Bulimia is an eating disorder that consists of '''gorging''' on food, followed by self-induced vomiting. This behavioral disorder may be part of anorexia nervosa or may constitute a distinct, separate syndrome.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[swallow]], especially with [[greediness]], or in large [[mouthful]]s or quantities.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Home|Homer]]|chapter=Book XI. Disasters of Achaian Chiefs.|translator=[[w:Francis William Newman|Francis W[illiam] Newman]]|title=[[w:Iliad|The Iliad of Homer: Faithfully Translated into Unrhymed English Metre]]|edition=2nd revised|location=London|publisher=[[w:Nicholas Trübner|Trübner & Co.]],{{nb...|60, Paternoster Row.}}|year=1871|page=155|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=lnxa_TE91QEC&pg=PA155|lines=175–176|oclc=559671054|passage=Seiz'd by his [a lion's] stalwart teeth, at once {{!}} his victim's [a cow's] neck is broken: / Thereafter, swilleth he the blood, {{!}} and all her entrails '''gorgeth'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|chapter=Fishing|title=Hunter’s & Trapper’s Complete Guide, a Manual of Instruction in the Art of Hunting, Trapping, and Fishing, with the Secrets of Making, Setting, and Baiting Traps, by an Old Hunter and Trapper.{{nb...|It also Contains the Art of Dog Training for Sporting Purposes, with the Most Approved Recipes for Preserving, Tanning and Dyeing All Kinds of Skins and Furs. An Indispensable Handbook for the Amateur or Professional Sportsman.}}|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Hurst & Co., publishers,{{nb...|75 Nassau Street.}}|year=1875|page=53|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=8Pc-AAAAYAAJ&pg=PA53|oclc=894203726|passage=If you use live bait, be exceedingly careful in determining when the fish has '''gorged''' it. You should give him several minutes after he has seized it, for this purpose. On seeing the bait, a pickerel will generally run off with it, and will then stop to '''gorge''' it, but does not always do so. {{...}} But if he has '''gorged''' the bait, he will soon start off a second time, and sometimes will stop and start off the third time. In these cases, you should never be in a hurry. when you are convinced that he has taken down the bait, draw a tight line, and strike for your fish.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[fill up]] to the [[throat]]; to [[glut]], to [[satiate]].
#: {{synonyms|en|sate|stuff}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Miscellaneous Works|volume=III|chapter=[Translations from [[w:Giovanni Boccaccio|Boccace]].] Sigismonda and Guiscardo.|page=270|passage=If in thy doting and decrepit age, / Thy ſoul, a ſtranger in thy youth to rage, / Begins in cruel deeds to take delight, / '''Gorge''' with my blood thy barb'rous appetite; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Joseph Addison|chapter=[[w:John  Milton|Milton]]’s Style Imitated, in a Translation of a Story out of the Third [[w:Aeneid|Æneid]]|title=The Dramatick Works of Joseph Addison. With the Authour’s Poems, on Several Occasions|location=Boston, Mass.|publisher=Printed by Snelling and Simons, for J. W. Armstrong,{{nb...|No. 4, Exchange-Buildings, Devonshire-Street.}}|year=a. 1720|year_published=1808|page=186|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/dramatickworksof00addi/page/186/mode/1up|oclc=10360557|passage=The giant, '''gorg'd''' with flesh, and wine, and blood, / Lay stretch'd at length and snoring in his den, / Belching raw gobbets from his maw, o'ercharged / With purple wine and curdled gore confus'd.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[fill up]] (an [[organ]], a [[vein]], etc.); to [[block up]] or [[obstruct]]; {{lb|en|US|specifically}} of [[ice]]: to [[choke]] or [[fill]] a [[channel]] or [[passage]], [[cause|causing]] an [[obstruction]].
#: {{synonyms|en|engorge}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Ellwood Morris|title=Notice of a Railroad upon an Ice Grade|editor=[[w:John Fries Frazer|John F[ries] Frazer]]|journal=Journal of the Franklin Institute of the State of Pennsylvania for the Promotion of the Mechanic Arts|location=Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher=Published by the {{w|Franklin Institute}}, at their hall|month=March|year=1852|volume=XXIII (Third Series; volume LIII overall)|number=3|page=161|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=OutCAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA161|oclc=1013447426|passage=At the mouth of the river there is shoal water, in which the ice grounds, and in severe weather, it forms a point of support for successive floating masses, until it sometimes '''gorges''' up for many miles above the ferry of the railway line.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Robert Christison|chapter=Of the Poisonous Gases|title=A Treatise of Poisons, in Relation to Medical Jurisprudence, Physiology, and the Practice of Physic|edition=3rd|location=Edinburgh|publisher=[[w:A & C Black|Adam & Charles Black]],{{nb...|North Bridge}}; London: [[w:Longman|Longman, Rees, Orme, Brown, Green, & Longman]]|year=1836|page=752|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=kr40AAAAIAAJ&pg=PA752|oclc=651714163|passage=The morbid appearances left in the body after poisoning with carbonic acid gas have been chiefly observed in persons killed by charcoal vapour. {{...}} the heart and great veins are '''gorged''' with black fluid blood; the eyes are generally glistening and prominent, the face red, and the tongue protruding and black. '''Gorging''' of the cerebral vessels seems to be very common.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Linda Anderson|chapter=16|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=KnMBCwAAQBAJ&pg=PT252|location=[Bloomington, Ind.]|title=The Secrets of Sadie Maynard|publisher=w:Xlibris|date=13 November 2015|isbn=978-1-5144-2645-6|passage=He'd meant to only kiss her, to play a bit with her mouth, to place small kisses on the fragile bones of her cheeks, but when their tongues met, the gentleness flamed to full-fledged wanting. His cock '''gorged''' swiftly, and he pressed her tight against him between his legs.}}

