Page 1340 great circle: -------- begin text --------
==English==
[[Image:Sphere_halve.png|thumb|right|A great circle divides the sphere in two equal [[hemisphere]]s]]

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|geometry}} A circle defined as the [[intersection]] of the [[surface]] of a [[sphere]] and a [[plane]] which passes through the [[centre]] of the sphere.
# A [[segment]] of such circle representing the [[shortest]] [[distance]] between two points on the surface of a sphere.
#: {{syn|en|great-circle arc}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1902|author=S. L. Penfield|chapter=Use of the Stereographic Projection for Geographical Maps and Sailing Charts|title=American Journal of Science
|passage=Wherever a navigator may be, it certainly would be to his advantage to be able to find the '''great circle''', or shortest course, and the distance, to his port of destination, [....]}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2003|author=Jeff Toghill|title=The Navigator's Handbook: Essential Methods and Equipment--and how to Use Them
|passage=The shortest distance between two points on an ocean Mercator chart is not a straight line - it is a curve known as a ''''great circle''''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2005|author=Phil Medina|title=Homework Helpers: Earth Science|page=52|passage=Because the Earth's surface is curved, the shortest distance is actually a curve over the surface called a '''great circle'''.}}

