===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[engulf]].
#* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|X||[…] if with thee the roaring wells<br>⁠Should '''gulf''' him fathom-deep in brine;<br>⁠And hands so often clasp’d in mine,<br>Should toss with tangle and with shells.}}
# {{lb|en|Oxbridge slang|transitive}} To [[award]] a [[degree]] to somebody who has only just [[pass]]ed sufficiently.
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1852
|author=w:Charles Astor Bristed
|title=Five Years in an English University
|page=228–229
|passage=The mention of '''gulfed''' and plucked men brings me back to myself.
}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1863
|author=w:Henry Kingsley
|title=Austin Elliot
|page=123
|passage=The good Professor scolded, predicted that they would all be either '''gulfed''' or ploughed.
}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1876
|author=[[w:Sir George Trevelyan, 2nd Baronet|Sir George Otto Trevelyan]]
|title=The life and Letters of Lord Macaulay
|url=https://archive.org/details/lifelettersoflor01trevuoft
|volume=1
|page=83
|passage=Everyone who knows the Senate House may anticipate the result. When the Tripos of 1822 made its appearance his name did not grace the list. In short, to use the expressive vocabulary of the university, Macaulay was '''gulfed''':—a mishap which disabled him from contending for the Chancellor's medals, then the crowning trophies of a classical career.
}}

