Page 1349 handle: -------- begin text --------
==English==
{{wikipedia|Handle (grip)|mul=Handle (mathematics)}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈhæn.dl̩/}}
* {{audio|en|EN-AU ck1 handle.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
** ''([[w:/æ/ raising|/æ/ raising]])'' {{IPA|en|/ˈhɛən.dl̩/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-handle.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{hyphenation|en|han|dle}}
* {{rhymes|en|ændəl|s=2}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{der|en|enm|handel}}, {{m|enm|handle}}, from {{der|en|ang|handle||a handle}}, from {{m|ang|handlian||to handle, feel, deal with, discuss}}. See verb below. Cognate with {{cog|da|handel||a handle}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# The part of an object which is (designed to be) held in the hand when used or moved.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1854|title=Silver-knife: or, The hunters of the Rocky Mountains| author=John Hovey Robinson| page=133| url=http://books.google.com/books?id=0NURAAAAYAAJ| passage=Once his fingers strayed to the '''handle''' of his hunting-knife, and I should have interfered had I not been conscious that Wickliffe was on his guard.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Journal of the Chemical Society| volume=81| section=part 2| title=Atomic Weight of Lanthanum| year=1902| url=http://books.google.com/books?id=ocg3AAAAMAAJ| passage=By pushing the fork downwards so that its teeth pass the '''handle''' of the stopper, and then turning the cover of the desiccator 90°, the '''handle''' of the stopper falls into the furrows and rests upon them.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Proceedings of the Royal Society of London| volume=37| title=Origin of the Respiratory Sounds| year=1905| passage=By keeping the '''handle''' of the bellows fixed in any given position the lung within the chamber could be kept for a short time at any desired degree of distension, and by pressing at intervals upon the bag, air could be forced to and fro between the bad and the lung outside the chamber, without distending the air within it.|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=l-kAAAAAYAAJ}}
#* {{quote-song|en|title=w:Billy the Mountain|author=w:Frank Zappa|year=1972|passage=But first they were gonna stop in Las Vegas / It's off to Las Vegas / To check out the lounges / Pull a few '''handles'''}}
# An [[instrument]] for effecting a purpose (either literally or figuratively); a [[tool]], or an [[opportunity]] or [[pretext]].
#* {{RQ:South Twelve Sermons|passage=They overturned him to all his interests by the sure but fatal '''handle''' of his own good nature.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Essay on the prevailing methods of the evangelization of the non-Christian world|page=70|author=Robert Needham Cust| year=1894| url=http://books.google.com/books?id=5mxCAAAAIAAJ| passage=Nothing can be more reprehensible, or wicked, than to make Christian Missions a '''handle''' for political expansion.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Advances in immunology| author=William Hay Taliaferro; John Herbert Humphrey| year=1978| page=224| url=http://books.google.com/books?id=8Qom0nHQxQ8C| passage=Many investigators feel that the double requirement for the antigen-recognition by cytotoxic T cells or DTH-reactive T cells may provide a '''handle''' for solving the T-cell receptor puzzle, and that anti-Id reagents are to be used in this approach.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=A prologue to revolution: the political career of George Grenville| page=95| author=Allen S. Johnson|year=1997| url=http://books.google.com/books?id=UP9juWhjl8IC|isbn=9780761806004| passage=Indeed, at the beginning of the session he was careful to make "no declarations of what might hereafter be measures, so as to give anybody a '''handle''' for fixing him down to any particular system."}}
# {{lb|en|gambling}} The [[gross]] amount of [[wagering]] within a given period of time or for a given event at one of more establishments.
#: {{ux|en|The daily '''handle''' of a Las Vegas casino is typically millions of dollars.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Gambling in America: an encyclopedia of history, issues, and society|page=421|author=William Norman Thompson| year=2001| isbn=9781576071595| url=http://books.google.com/books?id=-9eNVovFFMoC| passage=For a casino table game,the handle is difficult to determine, as it consists of all the bets made in every game, whether by chip or by cash play.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Travel industry economics: a guide for financial analysis|page=139|author=Harold L. Vogel| year=2001| isbn=9780521781633| url=http://books.google.com/books?id=Sw0awii2kdEC| passage=Note here, however, that the casino's "edge" (its expected value per unit bet, or, in casino jargon, the house p.c.) in table games is expressed as a percentage of the '''handle''' and not as a percentage of the drop (even though these might sometimes be the same).}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The economics of casino gambling| page=77| author=Douglas M. Walker| year=2007| isbn=9783540351023| url=http://books.google.com/books?id=MiO7kYe8ztUC| passage=The results for the dog racing model indicate that increases in lottery sales and decreases in horse racing '''handle''' and casino revenues in the state in question statistically increase dog racing '''handle'''.}}
# {{lb|en|textiles}} The tactile qualities of a fabric, e.g., softness, firmness, elasticity, fineness, resilience, and other qualities perceived by touch.
# {{lb|en|slang}} A [[name]] or [[nickname]], especially as an [[identifier]] over the [[radio]] or [[Internet]].
#: {{cot|en|alias|call sign|pseudonym}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=A 4th course of chicken soup for the soul| year=1997| page=312| author=Jack Canfield; Hanoch McCarty| isbn=9781558744592| url=http://books.google.com/books?id=Um3-9MdLr_0C| passage=We sat together at the restaurant and asked him about his '''handle''' (CB name).}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Talisman|author=Stephen King; Peter Straub| year=2001| isbn=9780375507779| url=http://books.google.com/books?id=dyIWVNGh-zcC| passage=This was so unexpected that Jack came close to gabbling out his real name instead of the one he had used at the Golden Spoon, the name he also used if the people who picked him up asked for his '''handle'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Invisible Armies| year=2007| page=253| author=Jon Evans| isbn=9780312368678| url=http://books.google.com/books?id=It4tu1sNN6EC| passage="I don't actually know his birth name. He just uses his '''handle'''."}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} A [[title]] attached to one's name, such as ''Doctor'' or ''Colonel''.
#: {{ux|en|The successful businessman was knighted and acquired a '''handle''' to his name.}}
# {{lb|en|computing}} A [[reference]] to an object or structure that can be stored in a [[variable]].
#: {{ux|en|This article describes how to find the module name from the window '''handle'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Proceedings of the Fifteenth International Conference on Very Large Data| page=383| author=Petrus Maria Gerardus Apers; Gio Wiederhold| year=1989| url=http://books.google.com/books?id=wZlMPiNGVM8C| passage=A '''handle''' for a type instance is similar to an open file descriptor; it is used to reference that type instance when performing operations on it.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=MATLAB programming for engineers|page=354|author=Stephen J. Chapman| year=2008| isbn=9780495244493| url=http://books.google.com/books?id=fhpotPvv7v8C| passage=By contrast, when a host function creates a handle for a nested function and returns that '''handle''' to a calling program, the host function's workspace is created and ''remains in existence for as long as the function handle remains in existence.''}}
# {{lb|en|Australia|chiefly|_|Northern Territory|New Zealand}} A 10 fluid ounce (285 mL) glass of beer.
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Breakwater|page=86|author=Kate Duignan| year=2002| isbn=9780864734174| publisher=Victoria University Press| url=http://books.google.com/books?id=4IFrIicZvJoC| passage=A shudder passes over him and he orders another '''handle''' of beer.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Lateral Connection|page=68|author=Rod Hylands| year=2006| isbn=9780476015296| url=http://books.google.com/books?id=_hRTI4XKDkMC| passage=Imagine staring into the heavens on a clear night and seeing a '''handle''' of beer floating amongst the stars, or an angel, or the face of a famous celebrity.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Fodor's 2009 New Zealand|page=571| author=Stephanie E. Butler| year=2008| isbn=9781400019526| url=http://books.google.com/books?id=KknHa1mntBsC| passage=When ordering a beer, you'll get either a '''handle''' (mug) or a one-liter jug (pitcher).}}
# {{lb|en|US}} A half-gallon (1.75-liter) bottle of alcohol.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2014|author=Ray Stoeser; Josh Cuffe|title=Bury My Body Down By the Highway Side|page=83
|passage=Josh bought a fifth of Evan Williams for Andrew as a token of gratitude and Ray, because of the financial constraints, purchased the cheapest '''handle''' of whiskey he could find: Heaven Hill.}}
# {{lb|en|geography|Newfoundland|and|Labrador|rare}} A [[point]], an extremity of land.
#: {{ux|en|the '''Handle''' of the Sug in Newfoundland}}
# {{lb|en|topology}} A [[topological space]] [[homeomorphic]] to a [[ball]] but viewed as a [[product]] of two lower[[-dimensional]] balls.
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Topology and geometry of manifolds| author=Gordana Matić; Clint McCrory| year=2003| page=182| url=http://books.google.com/books?id=8zHRpkRwnrAC|isbn=9780821835074| passage=Such a 2-'''handle''' cancels the 1-'''handle''' so the manifold is D<sup>4</sup>.}}
# {{lb|en|algebraic geometry}} The [[smooth]], [[irreducible]] [[subcurve]] of a [[comb]] which connects to each of the other [[component]]s in exactly one point.
# {{lb|en|UK|informal}} A traditional [[dimpled]] [[glass]] with a handle, for serving a [[pint]] of [[beer]].
#: {{syn|en|jug}}

