===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|handlen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|handlian|t=to handle, feel, deal with, discuss}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*handulōn}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*handulōną|t=to take, grip, feel}}, equivalent to {{suf|en|hand|le|id2=verbal frequentative}}. Cognate with {{cog|fy|handelje}}, {{m|fy|hanneljen}}, {{m|fy|hanljen|t=to handle, treat}}, {{cog|nl|handelen|t=to handle, deal, act, negotiate}}, {{cog|de|handeln|t=to act, trade, negotiate, behave}}, {{cog|sv|handla|t=to buy, trade, deal}}, {{cog|is|höndla|t=to handle}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To touch; to feel or hold with the hand(s).
#* {{RQ:Spenser Amoretti|sonnet=1
|passage=Happy, ye leaves! when as those lilly hands [...] Shall '''handle''' you.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Luke|24|39|passage='''Handle''' me, and see; for a spirit hath not flesh.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Regained|book=1|passage=[...] about his altar, '''handling''' holy things}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|author=Adele Pillitteri|title=Pocket Guide for Maternal & Child Health Nursing|page=63|passage=Encourage the client to '''handle''' her breasts to grow accustomed to touching them, thus enabling milk production in the first few days after birth.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=February 12, 2011|author=Les Roopanarine| title=Birmingham 1 - 0 Stoke| work=BBC| url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/eng_prem/9391008.stm| passage=Robert Huth '''handled''' a Bentley shot, only for the offence to go unnoticed.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|rare}} To accustom to the hand; to take care of with the hands.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1679|author={{w|Sir William Temple, 1st Baronet|William Temple}}|title=An essay upon the advancement of trade in Ireland.
|passage=The hardness of the winters forces the breeders to house and '''handle''' their colts for at least six months every year.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To manage, use, or [[wield]] with the hands.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King Lear|4|6|passage=That fellow '''handles''' his bow like a crowkeeper}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1976|author=Mel Hallin Bolster|title=Crazy Snake and the Smoked Meat Rebellion|page=66
|passage=Light on his feet for a big man, he '''handled''' the rifle like a pistol.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To manage, control, or direct.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Measure|5|1|passage=You shall see how I'll '''handle''' her}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=December 16, 2011|author=Denis Campbell| title=Hospital staff 'lack skills to cope with dementia patients'| work=Guardian| url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/society/2011/dec/16/hospital-staff-dementia-patients| passage=The findings emerged from questionnaires filled in by 2,211 staff in 145 wards of 55 hospitals in England and Wales and 105 observations of care of dementia patients. Two-thirds of staff said they had not had enough training to provide proper care, 50% said they had not been trained how to communicate properly with such patients and 54% had not been told how to '''handle''' challenging or aggressive behaviour.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2015|author=Nora Quick|title=Case of the Missing Millionaire|passage=“You also '''handle''' the accounts for Julie Wojakowski, what about her? Any recent deposits in that amount?”}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2019|month=October| title=Consultation on University Station designs| journal=Modern Railways| page=17| text=University station opened in May 1978 and was designed to '''handle''' around 500,000 passengers a year, a significantly lower figure than the 3.5 million passengers who now use it.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To treat, to deal with (in a specified way).
#: {{ux|en|she '''handled''' the news with grace}}
#: {{ux|en|the Persians '''handled''' the French ambassador shamefully}}
#* {{RQ:Marlowe Tamburlaine|part=1|scene=iii|page=50|passage=I will not tell thee how Ile '''handle''' thee,<br>But euery common ſouldier of my Camp<br>Shall ſmile to see thy miſerable ſtate.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-2|1|4|passage=How wert thou '''handled''' being prisoner?}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To deal with (a subject, argument, topic, or theme) in speaking, in writing, or in art.
#* {{RQ:Bacon Essayes|chapter=Of Envy|passage=We will '''handle''' what persons are apt to envy others...}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1976|author=Krishna Chaitanya|title=A History of Indian Painting: The modern period|page=21|passage=If traditional painting '''handled''' the same themes again and again, a truth which people are apt to overlook is that we often get startlingly different compositions of the same theme or episode.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To receive and transfer; to have pass through one's hands; hence, to buy and sell.
#: {{ux|en|a merchant '''handles''' a variety of goods, or a large stock}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|rare}} To be concerned with; to be an expert in.
#* {{RQ:KJV|Jeremiah|2|8||They that '''handle''' the law knew me not}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[put up with]]; to endure (and continue to function).
#: {{ux|en|I can't '''handle''' this hot weather.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|author=Andrew Stellman; Jennifer Greene|title=Learning Agile: Understanding Scrum, XP, Lean, and Kanban|isbn=1449363857
|passage=For example, a program that loads data from a file needs to '''handle''' the case where that file is not found.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To use the hands.
#* {{RQ:KJV|Psalms|115|7|passage=They [idols made of gold and silver] have hands, but they '''handle''' not}}
# {{lb|en|soccer|intransitive}} To illegally touch the ball with the hand or arm; to commit [[handball]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[behave]] in a particular way when handled (managed, controlled, directed).
#: {{ux|en|the car '''handles''' well}}

