===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[attach]] a hook to.
#: {{ux|en|'''Hook''' the bag here, and the conveyor will carry it away.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[catch]] with a hook {{qualifier|hook a fish}}.
#: {{ux|en|He '''hooked''' a snake accidentally, and was so scared he dropped his rod into the water.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To work yarn into a fabric using a hook; to [[crochet]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1917|author=L M Montgomery|title=Anne's House of Dreams|passage=No one seems to want anything but '''hooked''' mats now.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[insert]] in a curved way reminiscent of a hook.
#: {{ux|en|He '''hooked''' his fingers through his belt loops.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[ensnare]] or [[obligate]] someone, as if with a hook.
#: {{ux|en|She's only here to try to '''hook''' a husband.}}
#: {{ux|en|A free trial is a good way to '''hook''' customers.}}
# {{lb|en|UK|US|slang|archaic}} To [[steal]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[connect]] (''hook into'', ''hook together'').
#: {{ux|en|If you '''hook''' your network cable into the jack, you'll be on the network.}}
# {{lb|en|usually|in passive}} To make [[addicted]]; to [[captivate]].
#: {{ux|en|He had gotten '''hooked''' on cigarettes in his youth.}}
#: {{ux|en|I watched one episode of that TV series and now I'm '''hooked'''.}}
# {{lb|en|cricket|golf|basketball}} To play a [[hook shot]].
#* '''2012''', Roland Johnson, ''Jeremya Jones: A King's Kid'' (page 93)
#*: "Hey, Sara! Watch this hook shot!" he shouted as he '''hooked''' the ball right through the net—swish!
# {{lb|en|rugby}} To succeed in [[heel]]ing the ball back out of a [[scrum]] (used particularly of the team's designated [[hooker]]).
# {{lb|en|field hockey|ice hockey}} To engage in the illegal maneuver of [[hooking]] (i.e., using the hockey stick to trip or block another player)
#: {{ux|en|The opposing team's forward '''hooked''' me, but the referee didn't see it, so no penalty.}}
# {{lb|en|soccer|bowling}} To [[swerve]] a ball; kick or throw a ball so it [[swerve]]s or [[bend]]s.
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|date=December 29, 2010
|author=Sam Sheringham
|title=Liverpool 0 - 1 Wolverhampton
|work=BBC
|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/eng_prem/9308904.stm
|page=
|passage=The Reds carved the first opening of the second period as Glen Johnson's pull-back found David Ngog but the Frenchman '''hooked''' wide from six yards.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|slang}} To engage in [[prostitution]].
#: {{ux|en|I had a cheap flat in the bad part of town, and I could watch the working girls '''hooking''' from my bedroom window.}}
# {{lb|en|Scrabble}} To play a word [[perpendicular]] to another word by adding a single letter to the existing word.
# {{lb|en|bridge|slang}} To [[finesse]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To seize or pierce with the points of the horns, as cattle in attacking enemies; to [[gore]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[bend]]; to be [[curved]].
#* '''2020''', Roman Martin, ''Azores: The finest coastal and mountain walks'' (page 229)
#*: 10 mins later, at the point where the road '''hooks''' sharp to the left, continue straight on through the wood along a mule track {{...}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To move or go with a sudden turn. <!--Webster 1913 supplement-->

