Page 1392 latitude: -------- begin text --------
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*-tus}}
Borrowed into {{inh|en|enm|-}} from {{der|en|fro|latitude}}, from {{der|en|la|lātitūdō||breadth, width, latitude}}, from {{m|la|lātus||broad, wide}}, from older {{m|la|stlātus}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈlæt.ɪ.tjuːd/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈlæt.ɪ.tud/|/ˈlæt.ə.tjud/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-latitude.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|en-GB-latitude.ogg|Audio (UK)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|geography|astronomy|countable}} The [[angular]] distance north or south from a [[planet]]'s [[equator]], measured along the [[meridian]] of that particular point.
#: {{cot|en|longitude}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Food: Some Account of Its Sources, Constituents and Uses|passage=The oat is hardier than wheat, and ripens in higher '''latitudes'''.|year=1880|author=Arthur Herbert Church|page=72|authorlink=Arthur Herbert Church|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/b28092053/page/72/mode/2up|publisher=Chapman and Hall|location=London|url=https://archive.org/details/b28092053}}
# {{lb|en|geography}} An imaginary line (in fact a circumference) around a planet running [[parallel]] to the planet's equator.
# {{lb|en|figurative}} The relative [[freedom]] from restrictions; [[scope]] to do something.
#: {{ux|en|His parents gave him a great deal of '''latitude'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Landon Francesca Carrara|page=208|volume=III|text="Love!" ejaculated the Comtesse; "And so throw away the chances of a life upon a month of honey!—I say a month, which is allowing a '''latitude''' tenderness never took...}}
# {{lb|en|astronomy}} The angular distance of a heavenly body from the [[ecliptic]].
# {{lb|en|photography}} The extent to which a light-sensitive material can be over- or underexposed and still achieve an acceptable result.
# [[extent|Extent]] or [[scope]]; e.g. breadth, width or amplitude.

====Usage notes====
* When used to refer to distances or imaginary lines around a planet, ''latitude'' is relative to the [[Earth]]'s [[Equator]] unless another planet is specified.

====Hyponyms====
* {{l|en|ascending latitude}}
* {{l|en|geolatitude}}
* {{l|en|high latitude}} <!--, that part of the earth's surface near either pole, esp. that part within either the arctic or the antarctic circle. -->
* {{l|en|low latitude}}<!--, that part of the earth's surface which is near the equator. -->

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|high-latitude}}
* {{l|en|isolatitude}}
* {{l|en|latitude by account}}
* {{l|en|low-latitude}}
* {{l|en|mid-latitude}}, {{l|en|middle latitude}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|circle of latitude}}
* {{l|en|horse latitudes}}

