Page 1394 ley line: -------- begin text --------
{{also|leyline}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{compound|en|ley|line|gloss1=meadow}}. Coined by archaeologist {{w|Alfred Watkins}}, who believed that the toponyms {{m|en|ley}}, {{m|en|lea}} and {{m|en|leigh}} indicated [[Neolithic]] paths.

===Noun===
{{wikipedia|lang=en}}
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|chiefly|Forteana}} A supposed [[alignment]] of ancient sacred sites, such as [[megalith]] structures, along straight lines of mystical power.
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1982|author=w:Douglas Adams|title=Life, the Universe and Everything|page=99|text=Glastonbury had long been associated with myths of ancient kings, witchcraft, '''ley-lines''' and wart curing[.]}}

====See also====
* {{l|en|geopathic}}
* {{l|en|layline}}
* {{l|en|vortex}}

{{c|en|Geography|Forteana}}
-------- end text --------
