Page 1396 line: -------- begin text --------
{{also|Line|linę|líne|líné|-line|LINE}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}
{{wikipedia|Line (unit)}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|līn}}, {{IPA|en|/laɪn/}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-line.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{a|AU}} {{IPA|en|/lɑɪn/|[lɑe̯n]}}
** {{audio|en|en-au-line.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪn|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|line}}, {{m|enm|lyne}}, from {{inh|en|ang|līne||line, cable, rope, hawser, series, row, rule, direction}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*līnā}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*līnǭ||line, rope, flaxen cord, thread}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*līną||flax, linen}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*līno-||flax}}.
{{rel-top|cognates}}
Cognate with {{cog|sco|line||line}}, {{cog|frr|liin||line}}, {{cog|fy|line||line}}, {{cog|nl|lijn||rope, cord}}, {{cog|de|Leine||line, rope}}, {{cog|da|line||rope, cord}}, {{cog|sv|lina||line, rope, wire}}, {{cog|is|lína||line}}. Related also to {{cog|nl|lijn||flax}}, {{cog|de|Lein||flax, linen}}, {{cog|got|𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌽||linen, cloth}}, {{cog|la|linea||linen, thread, string, line}}, {{cog|la|linum||flax, thread, linen, cable}}, {{cog|grc|λίνον||flax, linen, thread, garment}}, {{cog|cu|линъ||flax}}, {{cog|ru|лён||flax}}, {{cog|lt|linai||flax}}, {{cog|ga|lin|līn}}, {{m|ga|lion||flax}}.
{{rel-bottom}}
Influenced in {{der|en|enm|-}} by {{der|en|frm|ligne||line}}, from {{der|en|la|linea}}. More at {{m|en|linen}}.

The oldest sense of the word is “rope, cord, thread”; from this the senses “path”, “continuous mark” were derived.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[path]] through two or more [[point]]s (''compare ‘[[segment]]’''); a continuous mark, including as made by a [[pen]]; any [[path]], curved or straight. <!-- should this be split into two senses: "path" and "mark" ? -->
#: {{ux|en|The arrow descended in a curved '''line'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1816|author={{w|Percy Bysshe Shelley|Percy Shelley}}
|title=[http://www.gutenberg.org/etext/4654 The Daemon of the World]
|passage=The atmosphere in flaming sparkles flew; / And where the burning wheels / Eddied above the mountain’s loftiest peak / Was traced a '''line''' of lightning.}}
#* {{RQ:Ferguson Zollenstein|IV
|passage=So this was my future home, I thought!{{...}}Backed by towering hills, the but faintly discernible purple '''line''' of the French boundary off to the southwest, a sky of palest Gobelin flecked with fat, fleecy little clouds, it in truth looked a dear little city; the city of one's dreams.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Jory Sherman|title=Sidewinder
|passage=For their present position, he drew an inverted V. Then he drew a '''line''' and on either side he inscribed landmarks, ridges, passes. At the other end he drew a number of inverted Vs to represent the Arapaho village.}}
## {{lb|en|geometry}} An [[infinite]]ly [[extend]]ing [[one-dimensional]] [[figure]] that has no [[curvature]]; one that has [[length]] but not [[breadth]] or [[thickness]].
##: {{syn|en|straight line}}
## {{lb|en|geometry|informal}} A [[line segment]]; a [[continuous]] [[finite]] [[segment]] of such a figure.
##: {{syn|en|line segment}}
## {{lb|en|graph theory}} An [[edge]] of a [[graph]].
## {{lb|en|geography}} A [[circle]] of [[latitude]] or of [[longitude]], as represented on a [[map]].
## {{lb|en|geography|'the line' or 'equinoctial line'}} The [[equator]].
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1789|author=w:Olaudah Equiano|title=The Interesting Narrative|volume=I|chapter=1
|passage=Benin {{...}} is situated nearly under the '''line''', and extends along the coast about 170 miles {{...}}.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1851|author=w:Herman Melville|title=s:Moby Dick
|chapter=54|passage=She [a ship called Town-Ho] was somewhere to the northward of the '''Line'''.}}
##* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1887|author=Harriet W. Daly|title=Digging, Squatting, and Pioneering Life in the Northern Territory of South Australia|page=111|passage=And all this, too, in a country only twelve degrees south of the '''line'''; I wonder the men worked as well and as steadily as they did, for I never saw Europeans work in any tropical country as our Port Darwin labourers did.}}
##* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1918|author=w:Norman Lindsay|title=The Magic Pudding|publisher=Angus and Robertson|location=Sydney|page=52|passage="Ho, aboard the ''Salt Junk Sarah'',<br>Rollin" home across the '''line''',<br>The Bo'sun collared the Captain's hat<br>And threw it in the brine."}}
## {{lb|en|music}} One of the straight [[horizontal]] and [[parallel]] prolonged [[stroke]]s on and between which the [[note]]s are placed.
## {{lb|en|cricket}} The [[horizontal]] [[path]] of a [[ball]] towards the [[batsman]] (see also [[length]]).
## {{lb|en|soccer}} The [[goal line]].
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=October 1, 2011|author=Clive Lindsay|work=BBC Sport
|title=[http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/scot_prem/15045413.stm Kilmarnock 1-2 St Johnstone]
|passage=St Johnstone's Liam Craig had to clear off the '''line''' before Steven Anderson sent a looping header into his own net for the equaliser on 36 minutes.}}
## {{lb|en|motoring}} A particular path taken by a [[vehicle]] when driving a [[bend]] or [[corner]] in the road.
##* {{quote-journal|1=en|date=Feb 2021|journal= The Road Ahead|location=Brisbane|page=10|column=3|text="I guess it's like race cars - if you get the right '''line''' you can come out at top speed."}}
# A [[rope]], [[cord]], [[string]], or [[thread]], of any thickness.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1884|author=w:Mark Twain|title=w:The Adventures of Huckleberry Finn|chapter=9
|passage=Then we hunted up a place close by to hide the canoe in, amongst the thick willows. We took some fish off of the '''lines''' and set them again, and begun to get ready for dinner.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Robert Newcomb|title=A March Into Darkness|page=29
|passage={{...}}he found preparing the hook far less fun than dangling the '''line''' in the water and waiting for a fish to come along. Finally succeeding, he beamed a smile up at his father, then lowered his '''line''' into the swift-moving Sippora.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Joshua Plunkett; Jeanne K. Hanson|title=The Complete Idiot's Guide to Trees and Shrubs|page=164
|passage=Use fabric or nursery grade webbing around stakes and trunk, loosely tying the '''line''' to the tree about 6 inches below the point where the tree bounces back in your hand when you grab the trunk.}}
# A [[hose]] or [[pipe]], of any size.
#: {{ux|en|a brake '''line'''}}
#: {{ux|en|the main water '''line''' to the house}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1973|title=Final Environmental Statement for the Geothermal Leasing Program|publisher=US department of the interior
|passage=There is the possible hazard of an oil spill in case the '''line''' breaks but normal pipeline maintenance and safety measures, etc., are designed to prevent large or long continued spillage.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1981|month=October|journal=Popular Science|volume=219|number=4|page=113
|passage=To the end of the metal fuel '''line''' (where it fits into the carb) you attach a four-foot length of flexible fuel '''line'''.}}
# [[direction|Direction]], [[path]].
#: {{ux|en|the '''line''' of sight}}
#: {{ux|en|the '''line''' of vision}}
# The [[wire]] connecting one [[telegraphic]] [[station]] with another, a telephone or internet cable between two points: a [[telephone]] or [[network]] [[connection]].
#: {{ux|en|I tried to make a call, but the '''line''' was dead.}}
#: {{ux|en|a dedicated '''line''';&emsp; a shared '''line'''}}
#: {{ux|en|Please speak up, the '''line''' is very faint.}}
# A [[clothesline]].
#: {{ux|en|We need to take the clothes off the '''line'''. The news reported a [[front]] is coming in from the east, and we can expect heavy rain and maybe [[hail]].}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Joan Morrison|title=Share House Blues|publisher=Boolarong Publications|year=1985|page=26|passage=She feels guilty for pampering him, and salves her conscience by bossily ordering him to go and fetch the clothes from the '''line'''[.]}}
# A [[letter]], a written form of [[communication]].
#: {{syn|en|epistle|letter|note}}
#: {{ux|en|Drop me a '''line'''.}}
# A [[connect]]ed series of public [[conveyance]]s, as a [[roadbed]] or [[railway]] [[track]]; and hence, an [[establish]]ed [[arrangement]] for forwarding [[merchandise]], etc.
#: {{ux|en|a '''line''' of stages}}
#: {{ux|en|an express '''line'''}}
# {{lb|en|military}} A [[trench]] or [[rampart]], or the non-physical demarcation of the extent of the territory occupied by specified forces.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1917|author=w:John Masefield
|title=[http://www.gutenberg.org/etext/20616 The Old Front Line]
|passage=This description of the old front line, as it was when the Battle of the Somme began, may some day be of use.{{...}}It is hoped that this description of the '''line''' will be followed by an account of our people's share in the battle.}}
# The exterior [[limit]] of a figure or territory: a [[boundary]], [[contour]], or [[outline]]; a [[demarcation]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1674|author=w:John Milton|title=s:Paradise Lost
|volume=IV|passage=''Eden'' stretch'd her '''Line''' / From ''Auran'' Eastward to the Royal Towrs / Of great ''Seleucia'',}}
# A long tape or ribbon marked with units for measuring; a [[tape measure]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A measuring line or cord.
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Isaiah|44|13|passage=The carpenter stretcheth out his rule; he marketh it out with a '''line'''; he fitteth it with planes, and he marketh it out with the compass, and maketh it after the figure of a man, according to the beauty of a man; that it may remain in the house.}}
# That which was measured by a line, such as a field or any piece of land set apart; hence, [[allot]]ted place of [[abode]].
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Psalms|16|6|passage=The '''lines''' are fallen unto me in pleasant places; yea, I have a goodly heritage.}}
# A threadlike [[crease]] or [[wrinkle]] marking the face, hand, or body; hence, a characteristic mark.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1651|author=w:John Cleveland|chapter=Fuscara
|title=Minor poets of the Caroline period|editor=w:George Saintsbury|year_published=1921)
|passage=He tipples palmistry, and dines On all her fortune-telling '''lines'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1812-1818|author=w:Lord Byron|title=w:Childe Harold's Pilgrimage
|passage=Though on his brow were graven '''lines''' austere.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1975|composer=w:Bob Dylan|title=w:Tangled Up in Blue|album=Blood on the Tracks
|passage=I muttered somethin' underneath my breath / She studied the '''lines''' on my face / I must admit I felt a little uneasy / When she bent down to tie the laces of my shoe / Tangled up in blue.}}
# {{1|lineament}}; [[feature]]; [[figure]] (of one's body).
#* {{quote-book|en|year=c.1609|author=w:William Shakespeare|title=s:The Tragedy of Cymbeline|passage=I mean, the '''lines''' of my body are as well drawn as his.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2002|author=Mark C. Carnes|title=Invisible Giants|page=220
|passage=By subtly altering classical ballet steps, she extended the '''line''' of the dancer's body and introduced a new kind of athleticism and verve to ballet.}}
# A more-or-less [[straight]] [[sequence]] of people, objects, etc., either arranged as a [[queue]] or [[column]] and often waiting to be [[process]]ed or [[deal with|dealt with]], or arranged [[abreast]] of one another in a [[row]] (and contrasted with a [[column]]), as in a military [[formation]]. {{defdate|from mid-16th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|lineup|q1=Canada|queue|q2=UK, Ireland}}
#: {{ux|en|The '''line''' forms on the right.}}
#: {{ux|en|There is a '''line''' of houses.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1817|author={{w|Percy Bysshe Shelley|Percy Shelley}}|title=s:The Revolt of Islam
|passage=A band of brothers gathering round me, made, / Although unarmed, a steadfast front{{...}}now the '''line''' / Of war extended, to our rallying cry / As myriads flocked in love and brotherhood to die.}}
## {{lb|en|military|nautical}} {{ellipsis of|en|line of battle}}
# {{lb|en|military}} The regular [[infantry]] of an [[army]], as distinguished from [[militia]], [[guards]], volunteer corps, [[cavalry]], [[artillery]]{{,}} etc.
# {{lb|en|music}} A series of [[note]]s forming a certain part (such as the bass or melody) of a greater work.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2015|author=Mícheál Houlahan; Philip Tacka|title=Kodály in the Third Grade Classroom
|passage=Students and the instructor sing the harmony '''line''' while the instructor plays the melody '''line''' on the piano.}}
# {{senseid|en|lineage}} A [[series]] or [[succession]] of [[ancestor]]s or [[descendant]]s of a given person; a [[family]] or [[race]]; compare {{m|en|lineage}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Geoffrey Chaucer|title=s:The Canterbury Tales|year=1387
|passage=Of his lineage am I, and his offspring'' / ''By very '''line''',}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=c.1604|author=w:William Shakespeare|title=s:Macbeth
|passage=They hail'd him father to a '''line''' of kings.}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Psalms|19|4|passage=Their '''line''' is gone out through all the earth, and their words to the end of the world. In them hath he set a tabernacle for the sun. <!--does this belong under this sense, or another (extant or to-be-added) one?-->}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1651|author=w:Thomas Hobbes|title=s:Leviathan
|passage=[T]he rest of the history of the Old Testament derives the succession of the '''line''' of David to the Captivity, of which '''line''' was to spring the restorer of the kingdom of God{{nb...}}.}}
# A small amount of text. Specifically:
## A [[written]] or [[printed]] [[row]] of [[letter]]s, [[word]]s, [[number]]s{{,}} or other [[text]], especially a row of words extending across a page or [[column]], or a blank in place of such text.
##: {{syn|en|row}}
##: {{ux|en|The answer to the comprehension question can be found in the third '''line''' of the accompanying text.}}
## A [[verse]] (in poetry).
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1609|author=w:William Shakespeare|title=[[s:Sonnet 71 (Shakespeare)|Sonnet 71]]
|passage=Nay if you read this '''line''', remember not, / The hand that writ it.}}
## A [[sentence]] of [[dialogue]], especially {{defdate|from late 19th c.}} in a play, movie{{,}} or the like.
##: {{ux|en|He was perfecting his pickup '''lines''' for use at the bar.}}
##: {{ux|en|"It is what it is" was one of his more annoying '''lines'''.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|title=Actor training|page=138|author=Alison Hodge|year=2010|passage=Anyone who has worked with Littlewood will wince at the memory of going over single '''lines''' time and time again, each actor in turn speaking the '''line''' until the valid intonation, phasing and emphasis emerged.}}
## A [[lie]] or [[exaggeration]], especially one told to gain another's [[approval]] or prevent losing it.
##: {{ux|en|Don't feed me a '''line'''!}}
# Course of [[conduct]], thought, [[occupation]], or [[policy]]; method of [[argument]]; [[department]] of [[industry]], trade, or intellectual activity. {{defdate|from earlier 17th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Specimens of the table talk of the late Samuel Taylor Coleridge|page=45|author=w:Samuel Taylor Coleridge|editor=Henry Nelson Coleridge|year=1835|passage=He is uncommonly powerful in his own '''line'''; but it is not the '''line''' of a first-rate man.}}
# The [[official]], [[state]]d [[position]] (or set of positions) of an individual or group, particularly a political or religious [[faction]]. {{defdate|from later 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|author=Jonathan Guyer
|journal=The American Prospect
|date=2019-9-10
|url=https://prospect.org/article/never-again-except-right-now
|issue=Fall 2019
|passage=Omar has challenged Elliott Abrams’s record in Latin America, taken a firm '''line''' against Saudi Arabia and the Emirates, and advocated for—wait for it—the two-state solution for Israel and Palestine (even though the headlines have focused on her expressing support for the right to boycott as a tactic).}}
#: {{ux|en|Remember, your answers must match the party '''line'''.}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} [[information|Information]] about or [[understanding]] of something. {{ngd|(Mostly restricted to the expressions ''get a line on'', ''have a line on'', and ''give a line on''.)}}
#: {{ux|en|Judy gave me a '''line''' on a lawyer who's supposed to be the best in the business.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Saturday Evening Post|date=11 March 1916|title=His Folks|author=Charles E. Van Loan
|url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=g54xAQAAMAAJ&pg=GBS.RA10-PA74
|text=She's got the best '''line''' on Hickey. Maybe she knows a way to put the heart back into him.
}}
# A set of [[product]]s or [[service]]s sold by a [[business]], or by extension, the business itself. {{defdate|from early 19th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''line''' of business, product '''line'''}}
#: {{ux|en|How many buses does the '''line''' have?}}
#: {{ux|en|The air'''line''' is in danger of bankruptcy.}}
# {{lb|en|stock exchange}} A number of [[share]]s taken by a [[jobber]].
# Any of an ill-defined set of [[unit]]s of [[length]], varying according to the country, [[discipline]], industry, and date of application, commonly with no indication of the intended magnitude:
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1847|author=Sir Erasmus Wilson|title=On Diseases of the Skin. Second edition
|passage=Withof estimates that the hair of the beard grows one line (French) in the course of a week, let us call it one line and a half (Engish); this would amount to six inches and a half yearly...}}
## {{lb|en|historical}} A tsarist-era [[Russian]] unit of measure, approximately equal to one [[tenth]] of an English inch, used especially when measuring the calibre of firearms.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1906|title=Reports of military observers to the armies in Manchuria|page=261
|passage=The arm of the Russian infantry is the three-'''line''' rifle, model 1891 (caliber 0.299 inch){{nb...}}.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|title=The United States in the First World War: An Encyclopedia|page=561|ISBN=1135684464
|passage=A “line” was a unit of measurement used in tsarist Russia and equal to about a tenth of an inch. The 3-'''line''' rifle, therefore, had a bore of three '''lines''', or approximately .30 caliber.}}
## One [[twelfth]] of an [[inch]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1883|author=Alfred Swaine Taylor; Thomas Stevenson|title=The principles and practice of medical jurisprudence
|passage=The cut is measured in thickness from a quarter of a '''line''' to a '''line''' and a half (a '''line''' is one-twelfth of an inch).}}
## One [[sixteenth]] of an inch.
## One [[fortieth]] of an inch.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1922|title=Hearings Before the Committee on Finance, United States Senate|chapter=Statement of James Turner, Representing Universal Button Fastening Co., Detriot, Mich.|page=5337
|passage=In case any of the committee do not understand what is meant by a rate per '''line''', I may say that buttons, being very small, are not measured by the foot or inch, but by the line, a line being one-fortieth of an inch. For example, that is a 27-'''line''' button{{nb...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|advertising}} {{short for|en|agate line}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1912|journal=Miscellaneous Series|issue=7-11|page=25
|passage=Advertising rates, line contract, less than 500 agate lines, 12 cents per line; 1,000 to 2,000 lines, 7 cents; 5,000 to 10,000 lines, 5 cents.}}
# {{lb|en|historical}} A [[maxwell]], a unit of [[magnetic]] [[flux]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1898|author=Alfred Eugene Wiener|title=Practical calculation of dynamo-electric machines|page=47
|passage=At the same time, however, for calculation in the metric system, one metre is taken as the unit for the length of the conductor, one metre per second as the unit velocity, and one '''line''' per square centimetre as the unit of field density.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1903|author=William Richard Kelsey|title=Continuous current dynamos and motors and their control|page=39
|passage=The density will now be only one quarter of a '''line''' per square centimetre, and therefore a unit pole placed at a distance of 2 centimetres from a similar pole, will only be acted on with a force of one quarter of a [[dyne]],{{nb...}}.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1904|author=Silvanus Phillips Thompson|title=Dynamo-electric machinery: a manual for students of electrotechniques: Volume 1, Part 1|page=74
|passage=The Paris Congress of 1900 adopted the name gauss as that of the unit of intensity of field, one ''gauss'' signifying one '''line''' per square centimetre. The same Congress also named one ''line'' as one ''maxwell'', but everybody still uses the term ''line''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1909|author=Henry Metcalf Hobart|title=Electricity: a text book designed in particular for engineering|page=58
|passage=A magnetic flux is said to have a density of one '''line''' per square centimeter when it exerts on a unit north pole a force of one [[dyne]].}}
# {{lb|en|baseball|slang|1800s|with "the"}} The [[batter]]'s [[box]].
# {{lb|en|fencing}} The [[position]] in which the [[fencer]]s hold their [[swords]]. <!-- but: -->
#: {{synonyms|en|line of engagement}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1861|author=George Chapman|title=Foil Practice, with a Review of the Art of Fencing|page=12
|passage=Thus, for example, in the '''line''' of Quarte, the direct thrust is parried by dropping the point under the adversary's blade and circling upwards, throwing off the attack in the opposite '''line''' (that of Tierce), and upon the direct thrust in the '''line''' of Tierce, by a similar action throwing off the attack in the opposite '''line''' (that of Quarte).}}
# {{lb|en|engineering}} [[proper|Proper]] relative [[position]] or [[adjustment]] (of parts, not as to design or proportion, but with reference to smooth working).
#: {{ux|en|the engine is in '''line''' / out of '''line'''}}
# A small path-shaped [[portion]] or [[serving]] of a [[powdery]] [[illegal]] [[drug]], especially [[cocaine]].
#* {{quote-song|en|title=White Lines (Don't Do It)|artist=Grandmaster Melle Mel|year=1983|passage=Buy white '''line''', go a long way / Either up your nose or through your vein / With nothing to gain except killing your brain}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998|author=Luke Davis|title=Candy
|passage="Let's have a '''line'''." He pulled a razor blade from his pocket and scooped out a couple of mounds. He laid out seven thick '''lines''' on a mirror. He rolled up a fifty-dollar note and snorted a '''line'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=Burl Barer|title=Broken Doll|page=64
|passage="Yes, we did. We both did a '''line''', but maybe close to a half gram of crystal meth. I did a '''line''' and he did a way much bigger '''line'''."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=D. C. Fuller|title=Meth Monster: Crankin' Thru Life a Look Into the Abyss|page=474
|passage=Snorting it was a much slower blast off and a longer less intense buzz, that was much easier to function on. A few minutes after you snort a '''line''' you can feel the niacin rush coming up your back and washing over your head,{{nb...}}.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Jeph Jacques|title=w:Questionable Content|format=webcomic|year=2013|section=Number 2386: The Thin White Line|sectionurl=https://questionablecontent.net/view.php?comic=2386|text="You know that scene in Lady and the Tramp where they're slurping up both ends of a noodle and end up kissing?"  "Y-yes..."  "It was like that, but with a '''line''' of cocaine instead of pasta."}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} {{1|instruction}}; [[doctrine]].
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Psalms|19|4|passage=Their '''line''' is gone out through all the earth, and their words to the end of the world. In them hath he set a tabernacle for the sun. <!--does this belong under this sense, or another (extant or to-be-added) one?-->}}
# {{lb|en|genetics}} A [[population]] of cells derived from a single cell and containing the same genetic makeup.
# (perfusion line) a set composed of a spike, a drip chamber, a clamp, a Y-injection site, a three-way stopcock and a catheter.
# {{lb|en|ice hockey}} A group of [[forward]]s that play together.
# {{lb|en|Australian rules football}} A set of positions in a team which play in a similar position on the field; in a traditional team, consisting of three players and acting as one of six such sets in the team.
# {{lb|en|medicine|colloquial}} A [[vascular]] [[catheter]].
#: {{ux|en|patient had a '''line''' inserted}}
#: {{ux|en|'''line''' sepsis}}
# {{lb|en|South Korean idol fandom}} A group of people born in a certain year ({{m|en|[[liner]]s}}).
#: {{ux|en|[[maknae line|maknae '''line''']]; [[hyung line|hyung '''line''']]; 97 line}}

