Page 1404 macrocephalic: -------- begin text --------
==English==

===Etymology===
from {{uder|en|grc|μακρός||long}} + {{m|grc|κεφαλή||head}}.

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Having an [[abnormally]] [[large]] or [[elongate]]d [[head]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1873|author=w:John Wells Foster|title=Pre-Historic Races of the United States|location=Chicago|publisher=S.C. Griggs & Co.|chapter=9|page=327|url=https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/100533967
|passage=In 1849, [[w:Martin Rathke|M. Rathke]] stated that artificially-formed skulls had been found near [[w:Kerch|Kertch]], in the Crimea, and called attention to certain passages in the works of {{w|Hippocrates}} and {{w|Strabo}}, overlooked by medical writers, in which these authors speak of the practice of modifying the shape of the head by means of bandages, as being in use among the '''macrocephalic''' (long-headed) Scythians.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1937|author=w:H. G. Wells|title=w:Star Begotten|section=Chapter 6, § 2|url=http://www.gutenberg.net.au/ebooks07/0701231h.html
|passage=This intimation, breaking through his resistances, evoked first the dread of an abnormal child, prematurely wise, '''macrocephalic''', with dreadful tentacular hands.... So his essential humanity presented the thing.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1979|author=w:Philip Roth|title=w:The Ghost Writer|location=New York|publisher=Farrar, Straus & Giroux|section=Part I
|passage={{...}} that I couldn’t return her gaze directly had also to do with this unharmonious relation between body and skull, and its implication, to me, of some early misfortune, of something vital lost or beaten down, and, by way of compensation, something vastly overdone. I thought of a trapped chick that could not get more than its beaked skull out of the encircling shell. I thought of those '''macrocephalic''' boulders the Easter Island heads.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2003|author=w:Mary Jane West-Eberhard|title=Developmental Plasticity and Evolution|publisher=Oxford University Press|section=Part II, Chapter 11, p. 228|url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=7DQNTPYaHlYC&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false
|passage=The males of ''[[w:Perdita portalis|P. portalis]]'' [bees] are strikingly dimorphic: fewer than 50% of them are “normal” winged males that mate with females at flowers, and the remainder are large, flightless, '''macrocephalic''' fighters that remain in the natal nest, where they fight to the death with competitors.}}
#* '''2010''', Susan Klugman and Susan J. Gross, “Ashkenazi Jewish Screening in the Twenty-first Century,” in Anthony R. Gregg and Joe Leigh Simpson (eds.), ''Genetic Screening and Counseling'', an issue of ''Obstetrics and Gynecology Clinics of North America'', March 2010, Volume 37, No. 1, p. 40,<sup>[https://books.google.ca/books?id=S4kRx74eXMQC&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false]</sup>
#*: [[w:Tay–Sachs disease|TSD]] is a neurodegenerative disorder that presents in the first year of life and is fatal in early childhood. {{...}} Infants are classically '''macrocephalic''' because of storage material accumulation in the brain and have the characteristic cherry red spot on their macula.
# {{lb|en|geography}} Characterized by a [[disproportionate]] [[concentration]] of population and activities in a single centre, to the [[detriment]] of other areas.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2003|author=Michael Bess|title=The Light-Green Society: Ecology and Technological Modernity in France, 1960-2000|publisher=University of Chicago Press|section=Part I, Chapter 2, p. 49|url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=zpDArm2CxtoC&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false
|passage=In 1947, the geographer {{w|Jean-François Gravier}} published a book that became an instant sensation: ''Paris et le désert français''. Gravier argued that France was becoming a seriously deformed nation, a '''macrocephalic''' invalid: Paris, the megalopolis, was sucking all the lifeblood from the provinces, leading to a dangerous imbalance between center and periphery.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=R. Kalra|chapter=High Technology and Urban Development in Bangalore, India|editors=Jay D. Gatrell; Neil Reid|title=Enterprising Worlds: A Geographic Perspective on Economics, Environments & Ethics|location=Dordrecht|publisher=Springer|page=74|url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=R72wXh7zXmsC&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false
|passage=Several developing countries are characterized by rapid urbanization, '''macrocephalic''' urban systems, high urban densities and various socio economic and environmental problems.}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|macrocephaly}}

====Translations====
{{trans-top|having an abnormally large head}}
* Catalan: {{t|ca|macrocèfal}}
* Italian: {{t+|it|macrocefalo}}
* Portuguese: {{t|pt|macrocéfalo}}
* Spanish: {{t|es|macrocéfalo}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===See also===
* {{l|en|normocephalic}}
* {{l|en|microcephalic}}
-------- end text --------
Page 1405 macrocephaly: -------- begin text --------
==English==

===Etymology===
{{confix|en|macro|cephaly}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|en|ɛfəli|s=5}}

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun|-|+}}

# {{lb|en|medicine}} A condition in which the [[head]] is abnormally [[large]].
# {{lb|en|geography}} The [[excessive]] [[concentration]] of [[population]] and [[development]] in a single centre, to the [[detriment]] of other areas.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Alan Riding|title=Problems of Mexico City: Warning to the Third World|journal=w:New York Times|date=15 May 1983|titleurl=http://www.nytimes.com/1983/05/15/world/problems-of-mexico-city-warning-to-third-world.html?pagewanted=all
|passage=Already Mexico City is a warning to other developing countries where centralized government, a high birth rate, neglect of agriculture and the rush to industrialize have combined to encourage migration to the cities. Twenty percent of the country's population lives here, and the term urban '''macrocephaly''' has been coined to describe the phenomenon.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|author=Natacha Aveline-Dubach|chapter=Understanding Globalization in Urban Asia: Moving from Single to Plural Dimensions and Scales|editors=Natacha Aveline-Dubach; Sue-Ching Jou; Hsin-Huang Michael Hsiao|title=Globalization and New Intra-Urban Dynamics in Asian Cities|location=Taipei|publisher=National Taiwan University Press|page=38|url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=rI_xBQAAQBAJ&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false
|passage=Another physical impact of urbanization in Asia has been the intensification of the primacy of capital cities. Most countries in the region—except China, Vietnam, and India—are well known for suffering from “'''macrocephaly''' disease.” Capital cities such as Tokyo, Taipei, Seoul, Jakarta, Manila, and Bangkok have a population that is more than double that of the next largest metropolitan area.}}

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|macrocephalism}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|macrocephalic}}

====Translations====
{{trans-top|a condition in which the head is abnormally large}}
