Page 1413 marginal: -------- begin text --------
==English==

===Etymology===
Borrowing from {{bor|en|ML.|marginālis}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈmɑː.d͡ʒɪ.nəl/}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈmɑɹ.d͡ʒɪ.nəl/}}
* {{a|AU}} {{IPA|en|/ˈmaː.d͡ʒɪ.nəl/}}
** {{audio|en|en-au-marginal.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-|more}}

# {{lb|en|uncomparable}} Of, relating to, or located at or near a [[margin]] or [[edge]]; ''also figurative usages of location and margin (edge)''.
#: {{ux|en|The '''marginal''' area at the edge of the salt-marsh has its own plants.}}
#: {{ux|en|In recent years there has been an increase in violence against '''marginal''' groups.}}
## [[written|Written]] in the margin of a [[book]].
##: {{ux|en|There were more '''marginal''' notes than text.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|author=R. I. Page|title=Introduction to English Runes|publisher=Boydell Press|page=198
|passage=The early pages had '''marginal''' notes most of which were lost when rats nibbled away the manuscript edges.}}
## {{lb|en|geography}} Sharing a [[border]]; [[geographically]] [[adjacent]].
##: {{ux|en|Monmouthshire is a Welsh county '''marginal''' to England.}}
# {{lb|en|comparable}} Determined by a small margin; having a salient characteristic determined by a small margin.
## Of a value, or having a characteristic that is of a value, that is close to being unacceptable or leading to exclusion from a group or category.
##: {{ux|en|His writing ability was '''marginal''' at best.}}
##: {{ux|en|Having reviewed the test, there are two students below the required standard and three more who are '''marginal'''.}}
##: {{ux|en|The pilots lacked experience flying in '''marginal''' weather conditions.}}
## {{lb|en|of [[land]]}} [[barely|Barely]] [[productive]].
##: {{ux|en|He farmed his '''marginal''' land with difficulty.}}
## {{lb|en|politics|chiefly|UK|Australia|NZ|of a [[constituency]]}} Subject to a change in sitting member with only a small change in voting behaviour, this usually being inferred from the small winning margin of the previous election.
##: {{ux|en|In Bristol West, Labour had a majority of only 1,000, so the seat is considered highly '''marginal''' this time around.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|author=Andrew Geddes; Jonathan Tonge|title=Labour′s Second Landslide: The British General Election 2001|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=yicN4gyNOCwC&pg=PA79&dq=%22more%7Cmost+marginal+%22+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=AEmoT8SEN66fmQXM38DhBA&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22more%7Cmost%20marginal%20%22%20-intitle%3A%22%22%20-inauthor%3A%22%22&f=false|page=79
|passage=In ‘battleground’ seats with the Conservatives, Liberal Democrat vote shares increased most in the most '''marginal''' seats.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Robert Waller; Byron Criddle|title=The Almanac of British Politics|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=YnAmle9e7AMC&pg=PA58&dq=%22more%7Cmost+marginal+%22+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=TxWpT5j8AazFmQWr2czhBA&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22more%7Cmost%20marginal%20%22%20-intitle%3A%22%22%20-inauthor%3A%22%22&f=false|page=58
|passage=In Outer London, Harrow East is now a more '''marginal''' Labour hold than Harrow West.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Nick Economou; Zareh Ghazarian|title=Australian Politics For Dummies|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=P7JPEyeUVroC&pg=PT160&dq=%22more%7Cmost+marginal+%22+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=AEmoT8SEN66fmQXM38DhBA&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22more%7Cmost%20marginal%20%22%20-intitle%3A%22%22%20-inauthor%3A%22%22&f=false|page=unnumbered
|passage=The pendulum lists the seats from least '''marginal''' to most '''marginal''' for the government on one side, and least '''marginal''' to most '''marginal''' for the opposition on the other side.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=2021 December 1|title=Network News: Integrated Rail Plan: Osborne predicts HS2 eastern leg will return|journal=RAIL|issue=945|page=8|text=He justified his comment by noting that the Labour Party had quickly committed to delivering a high-speed Leeds-Manchester line, and argued that pressure would grow on the Government because there were several '''marginal''' parliamentary seats around Leeds and Bradford.}}
# {{lb|en|economics|uncomparable}} Pertaining to changes resulting from a unit increase in production or consumption of a good.

