Page 1418 meander: -------- begin text --------
{{also|meänder|Meander}}
==English==
[[File:City of Carauari, the Juruá River and its tributaries, taken from the International Space Station.jpg|alt=Meanders of the Juruá River, in the Brazilian Amazon|thumb|'''Meanders''' of the Juruá River, in the Brazilian Amazon]]

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|mæander||archaic}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|la|Maeander}}, from {{der|en|grc|Μαίανδρος}} – a river in [[Asia Minor]] (present day Turkey) known for its winding course (modern {{m+|tr|Menderes}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/miˈændə(ɹ)/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-meander.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/miˈændɚ/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|often plural}} One of the turns of a winding, crooked, or involved course.
#: {{ux|en|the '''meanders''' of an old river, or of the veins and arteries in the body}}
# {{lb|en|geography}} One of a series of regular sinuous curves, bends, loops, turns, or windings in the channel of a river, stream, or other watercourse
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1712|author=w:Sir Richard Blackmore|title=Creation: A Philosophical Poem
|passage=See, how the streams advancing to the main, / Through crooked channels draw their crystal train! / While lingering thus they in '''meanders''' glide, / They scatter verdant life on either side.}}
# A [[tortuous]] or winding journey.
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2014-08-01|author=Enders A. Robinson; Dean Clark|title=Remote Sensing in Action: The Curious Case of Sherlock Holmes and Albert Einstein|publisher=SEG Books|isbn=9781560803133|page=30
|text=That journey in the spring of 1891 tracked historical underpinnings of the edifice that Einstein built. Our story will be an extremely interesting mental '''meander''' to trace the evolution some of the aspects of relativity theory from the beginning.}}
# {{synonym of|en|Greek key}}, a [[decorative]] [[border]]; [[fretwork]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1912|author=Field Museum of Natural History|title=Anthropological Series|page=227
|text=The scales are conceived of as '''meander''' fretwork; but I do not know whether, for this reason, this fish is associated with thunder.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Todd Merrill|title=James Mont: The King Cole Penthouse|publisher=Lulu.com|isbn=9780615179964
|text=A chair rail adorned with Chinese fretwork molding was a specific '''meander''' that repeated throughout the home. Moldings shaped as cartouches lined each wall,{{nb...}}}}
# {{lb|en|math}} A self-avoiding [[closed curve]] which [[intersect]]s a [[line]] a number of times.
# {{lb|en|possibly|obsolete}} A path on which the directions, distances, and elevations are noted, as a part of a land [[survey]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1894|author=United States Bureau of Land Management|title=Manual of Surveying Instructions for the Survey of the Public Lands of the United States and Private Land Claims|page=44
|text=Markings on '''Meander''' Corners. On all '''meander''' corners, the letters “M. C.” ( for '''meander''' corner) will be cut into the side facing the stream or lake to be meandered. On post or tree '''meander''' corners, within township exteriors{{nb...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1911|author=United States House Committee on Public Lands|title=Providing for Appeals from Decisions of the Interior Department in Land Matters: Hearing Held Before the Committee on the Public Lands of the House Representatives, June 22, 1910, on H.R. 27071, "To Provide for Appeals from Decisions of the Secretary of the Interior to the Court of Appeals of the District of Columbia, and for Other Purposes."|page=56
|text=And as to the lands that were within the national '''meander''' boundaries, that description was based upon a state survey?}}

