Page 1420 meridian: -------- begin text --------
{{also|Meridian}}
==English==
{{was wotd|2022|February|22}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/məˈɹɪ.dɪ.ən/|/mɪ-/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-AcpoKrane-meridian.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/məˈɹɪ.di.ən/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪdiən|s=4}}
* {{hyphenation|en|me|ri|di|an}}

===Etymology 1===
{{PIE word|en|médʰyos}}{{root|en|ine-pro|*dyew-}}
From Late {{inh|en|enm|meridian}}, {{m|enm|meridien|t=relating to midday or noon; southern; (''astronomy'') relating to the celestial meridian}}{{nb...|meridene, meridiane, meridyan, meridyen, merydien, merydyen|otherforms=1}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=merī̆diā̆n|pos=adj|id=MED27443}}</ref> from {{der|en|frm|meridien}}, {{der|en|fro|meridiane|t=relating to midday; southern}} (whence {{cog|xno|meridien}}; modern {{cog|fr|méridien}}), and from their {{glossary|etymon}} {{der|en|la|merīdiānus|t=relating to midday; southern}}, from {{m|la|merīdiēs|t=midday, noon; the south (due to the southward orientation of the sun at noon in the Northern Hemisphere)}} + {{m|la|-ānus|pos={{glossary|suffix}} meaning ‘of or pertaining to’}}. {{m|la||Merīdiēs}} is a {{glossary|dissimilated}} form of {{der|en|itc-ola|medīdiēs}} (with the ''-d-'' sound shifted to ''-r-''), from {{m|la|medius|t=middle}} (ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*médʰyos|t=middle}}) + {{m|la|diēs|t=day}} (ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*dyew-|t=heaven, sky; to be bright}}).<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=adj|id=116769|date=December 2021|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=adj}}</ref>

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|-}}

# Relating to a [[meridian#Noun|meridian]] (''in various senses''); [[meridional#Adjective|meridional]].
#* {{RQ:Pliny Holland Historie of the World|tome=1|book=II|chapter=Generall Rules of Lightning|page=27|passage=[T]he Tuſcanes have devided the Heaven into 16 parts. The firſt, is from the North to the Sunnes riſing in the Equinoctiall line: the ſecond, to the '''Meridian''' line, or the South: the third, to the Sunne ſetting in the Equinoctiall: and the fourth, taketh up all the reſt from the ſaid VVest to the North ſtarre.}}
#* {{RQ:Browne Hydriotaphia|chapter=Thomas Le Gros|url=https://archive.org/details/hydriotaphiaurne00browuoft/page/n17/mode/1up|passage=The Reliques of many lie like the ruines of ''[[w:Pompeii|Pompeys]]'', in all parts of the earth; And vvhen they arrive at your hands, theſe may ſeem to have vvandred far, vvho in a direct and '''''Meridian''''' Travell, have but few miles of knovvn Earth betvveen your ſelf and the Pole|footer=Used to refer to travel along a terrestrial meridian.}}
#* {{RQ:Defoe Crusoe 2|page=220|passage=I ſhall not peſter my Account, or the Reader, with Deſcriptions of Places, Journals of our Voyages, Variations of the Compaſs, Latitudes, '''Meridian'''-Diſtances, Trade-Winds, Situation of Ports, and the like; {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|except|literary}} Relating to [[midday]] or [[noon#Noun|noon]].
#* {{RQ:Gibbon Roman Empire|volume=III|chapter=Plan of the Fifth and Sixth Volumes.—Succession and Characters of the Greek Emperors of Constantinople, from the Time of {{w|Heraclius}} to the Latin Conquest|pages=18–19|pageref=18|passage=At the '''meridian''' hour he &#91;{{w|Philippikos Bardanes}}&#93; withdrew to his chamber, intoxicated with flattery and wine, and forgetful that his example had made every ſubject ambitious, and that every ambitious ſubject was his ſecret enemy.}}
#* {{RQ:Trollope Wortle|volume=II|chapter=Everybody's Business|page=31|passage=[It may be] that two glasses of alcoholic mixture in the middle of the day shall seem, when imputed to him, to convey a charge of downright inebriety. But the writer has perhaps learned to regard two glasses of '''meridian''' wine as but a moderate amount of sustentation.}}
#* {{RQ:London White Fang|chapter=The She-wolf|page=15|passage=Daylight came at nine o'clock. At midday the sky to the south warmed to rose-color, and marked where the bulge of the earth intervened between the '''meridian''' sun and the northern world.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}}
## Relating to the [[culmination]] or [[high#Adjective|highest]] [[point#Noun|point]].
##* {{RQ:Gibbon Roman Empire|volume=I|chapter=Of the Union and Internal Prosperity of the Roman Empire, in the Age of the Antonines|page=38|passage=This obvious difference marked the two portions of the empire with a diſtinction of colours, which, though it was in ſome degree concealed during the '''meridian''' ſplendor of proſperity, became gradually more viſible, as the ſhades of night deſcended upon the Roman world.}}
##* {{RQ:Hardy Far from the Madding Crowd|volume=II|chapter=Joseph and His Burden—Buck’s Head|page=139|passage=[I]n the '''meridian''' times of stage-coach travelling [the Buck's Head inn] had been the place where many coaches changed and kept their relays of horses.}}
## Relating to the [[south#Noun|south]]; meridional, [[southern#Adjective|southern]].
##* {{RQ:Byron Letters and Journals|volume=II|chapter=Notices of the Life of Lord Byron|year=1819|page=214|passage=A stranger loves the lady of the land, / Born far beyond the mountains, but his blood / Is all '''meridian''', as if never fann'd / By the black wind that chills the polar flood.}}

