{{trans-bottom}}

===Etymology 2===
{{multiple images
|direction = vertical
|image1 = The meridian line.svg
|caption1 = An [[illustration]] of a [[celestial#Adjective|celestial]] meridian ''(sense 1.1)'', which is a [[great circle]] [[pass#Verb|passing]] through the [[pole#Noun|poles]] of the [[celestial sphere]] (a [[notional#Adjective|notional]] [[sphere#Noun|sphere]] which [[serve#Verb|serves]] as the [[imaginary]] [[backdrop#Noun|backdrop]] for [[celestial object]]s) and the [[zenith]] for a particular [[point#Noun|point]] on the [[Earth]]’s [[surface#Noun|surface]].
|image2 = Meridian-International.svg
|caption2 = The [[prime meridian]] at 0° [[longitude]] is a [[terrestrial#Adjective|terrestrial]] meridian ''(sense 1.2)''. This illustration shows [[half]] of a great circle [[extend]]ing from the terrestrial [[North Pole]] to the terrestrial [[South Pole]].
|image3 = JRO-Globus.jpg
|caption3 = The meridian ''(sense 2.2)'' of this [[artificial]] [[globe]] is a half-[[ring#Noun|ring]] with [[marking#Noun|markings]] within which the globe [[spin#Verb|spins]].
}}

{{root|en|ine-pro|*dyew-}}
The {{glossary|noun}} is derived from Late {{inh|en|enm|meridian}}, {{m|enm|meridien|t=midday, noon; position of the sun at noon; the south; longitude of a place; (''astronomy'') celestial meridian}}{{nb...|meridene, meridiane, meridien, meridyan, meridyen, merydien, merydyen, |otherforms=1}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=merī̆diā̆n|pos=n|id=MED27442}}</ref> from {{der|en|xno|meridien|t=midday}}, {{der|en|frm|meridien|t=midday; the south; terrestrial meridian; (''astronomy'') celestial meridian}} (modern {{cog|fr|méridien}}), and {{der|en|fro|meridiane}}, {{m|fro|meridiiene}}, and from their {{glossary|etymon}} {{der|en|la|merīdiānum|t=midday; position of the sun at noon; the south}}, a {{glossary|noun}} use of the {{glossary|neuter}} form of {{m|la|merīdiānus|t=relating to midday; southern}}; see further at [[#Etymology 1|etymology 1]].<ref name="OED">{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=116769|date=December 2021|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref>

Sense 1.1 (“celestial meridian”) is ultimately modelled after {{noncog|la|[[merīdiāna]] [[līnea]]|t=meridian line}}. Sense 5.2 (“midday rest; siesta”) is modelled after {{cog|LL.|meridiana|t=midday; midday rest}}, probably short for {{noncog|la|[[merīdiāna]] [[hōra]]|t=midday time}}.<ref name="OED"/>

The {{glossary|verb}} is derived from the noun.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=233374|date=September 2018}}</ref>

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|astronomy}}
## ''In full'' '''celestial meridian''': a [[great circle]] [[pass#Verb|passing]] through the [[pole#Noun|poles]] of the [[celestial sphere]] and the [[zenith]] for a particular [[point#Noun|point]] on the [[Earth]]'s [[surface#Noun|surface]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
## {{lb|en|also|geography}} ''In full'' '''terrestrial meridian''': a great circle on the Earth's surface, passing through the [[geographic]] poles (the [[terrestrial#Adjective|terrestrial]] [[North Pole]] and [[South Pole]]); also, [[half]] of such a [[circle#Noun|circle]] [[extend]]ing from pole to pole, all points of which have the same [[longitude]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=[[w:James Ferguson (Scottish astronomer)|James Ferguson]]|title=VI. The Phænomena of ''Venus'', Represented in an Orrery Made by ''James Ferguson'', Agreeable to the Observations of Seignior ''[[w:Francesco Bianchini|Bianchini]]''.|journal=[[w:Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society|Philosophical Transactions. Giving Some Account of the Present Undertakings, Studies, and Labours, of the Ingenious, in Many Considerable Parts of the World]]|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} T. Woodward, {{...|at the Half-Moon, between the Temple-Gates in Fleetstreet}}; and C. Davis {{...|in Paternoster-row,}} printers to the {{w|Royal Society}}|month=March 31 (Gregorian calendar)|year=1746|volume=XLIV|issue=479|section=paragraph 15|page=140|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/jstor-104792/page/n15/mode/1up|doi=10.1098/rstl.1746.0027|oclc=630046584|passage=In this Place of ''Venus'' the Hour and Amplitude of the Sun's Riſing, for one Half of the Year, are the ſame with thoſe of his Setting in the other Half; which will alſo happen in all Places under the firſt '''Meridian''', where he riſes and ſets: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|chapter=Biographical|page=62|passage=Thought he, it’s a wicked world in all '''meridians'''; I’ll die a pagan.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}}
## The [[place#Noun|place]] on the [[celestial#Adjective|celestial]] meridian where it is [[cross#Verb|crossed]] by the [[sun#Noun|sun]] or a [[star#Noun|star]] at its [[high#Adjective|highest]] point.
##* {{RQ:Pope Dunciad|year=1729|page=154|passage=This vvonderful perſon ſtruck Medals, vvhich he diſperſed as Tickets to his ſubſcribers: The device, a Star riſing to the '''Meridian''', vvith this Motto, {{smallcaps|Ad Summa}} [To the highest]; and belovv, {{smallcaps|Inveniam Viam aut faciam}} [I shall either find a way or make one].}}
### {{lb|en|figuratively}} The highest or most [[developed#Adjective|developed]] point, or most [[splendid]] [[stage#Noun|stage]], of something; [[culmination]], [[peak#Noun|peak]], zenith. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
###* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 8|act=III|scene=ii|page=221|column=2|passage=I haue touch'd the higheſt point of all my Greatneſſe, / And from that full '''Meridian''' of my Glory, / I haſte novv to my Setting. I ſhall fall / Like a bright exhalation in the Euening, / And no man ſee me more.}}
###* {{RQ:Dryden Fables|chapter=Preface|page=23|passage={{...}} ''{{w|Ovid}}'' liv'd vvhen the ''Roman'' Tongue vvas in its '''Meridian'''; [[w:Geoffrey Chaucer|[Geoffrey] ''Chaucer'']], in the Davvning of our Language: {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|volume=V|chapter=XXIII|page=67|passage=This was the moment at which the fortunes of Montague reached the '''meridian'''. The decline was close at hand.}}
### {{lb|en|figuratively|specifically}} ''Chiefly followed by'' '''[[of]]''': the [[middle#Adjective|middle]] [[period#Noun|period]] of someone's [[life]], when they are at their [[full#Adjective|full]] [[ability|abilities]] or [[strength#Noun|strength]]; one's [[prime#Noun|prime]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
###* {{RQ:Bacon Essayes|chapter=Of Youth and Age. XLII.|pages=247–248|pageref=247|passage=Natures that haue much Heat, and great and violent deſires and Perturbations, are not ripe for Action, till they haue paſſed the '''Meridian''' of their yeares: As it was with ''[[w:Julius Caesar|Iulius Cæſar]]'', and ''[[w:Septimius Severus|Septimius Seuerus]]''.}}
###* {{RQ:Howell Epistolae|chapter=LX. To Tho. Young, Esq|date=28 April 1645|section=VI|page=284|passage=You ſeem to marvel I do not Marry all this vvhile, conſidering that I am paſt the '''''Meridian''''' of my Age, and that to you Knovvledge there have been overtures made me of Parties above my Degree.}}
###* {{RQ:Byron Letters and Journals|author=Moore|volume=I|chapter=Notices of the Life of Lord Byron|year=1819|page=211|passage=And here [Missolonghi],—it is impossible not to pause, and send a mournful thought forward to the visit which, fifteen years later, he paid to this same spot,—when, in the full '''meridian''' both of his age and fame, he came to lay down his life as the champion of that land, through which he now wandered a stripling and a stranger.}}
## A [[ring#Noun|ring]] or half-ring with [[marking#Noun|markings]] in which an [[artificial]] [[globe]] is [[install]]ed and may [[spin#Verb|spin]].
##* {{RQ:Herbert Temple|chapter=The Size|page=132|passage=Call to mind thy dream, / An earthly globe, / On whoſe '''meridian''' was engraven, / ''Theſe ſeas are tears, and heav'n the haven.''}}
## {{lb|en|mathematics}} A [[line#Noun|line]] passing through the poles of any [[sphere]]; a [[notional#Adjective|notional]] line on the surface of a [[curved#Adjective|curved]] or [[round#Adjective|round]] [[body#Noun|body]] (in particular, an [[eyeball#Noun|eyeball]]). {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
# {{lb|en|US|printing|dated}} The [[size#Noun|size]] of [[type#Noun|type]] between [[double great primer]] and [[canon]], [[standardize]]d as 44-point.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}}
## The [[south#Noun|south]]. {{defdate|14th–17th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Pliny Holland Historie of the World|tome=1|book=II|chapter=Of the Unequall Rising of the Starres: Of the Eclipse, both Where and How It Commeth|page=34|passage=[T]he figure of the very earth, vvhich together vvith the vvater, is by the ſame arguments knovvne to be like a Globe: for ſo doubtleſſe it commeth to paſſe, that vvith us the ſtars about the North pole, never go dovvn; and thoſe contrarivviſe of the '''Meridian''', never riſe.}}
## [[midday|Midday]], [[noon#Noun|noon]]. {{defdate|14th–19th c.}}
##: {{synonyms|en|noontide|Thesaurus:midday}}
##* {{RQ:Heywood Londini Speculum|chapter=The Speech of the Second Shovv, Delivered in Paules Church-yard|sig=C|verso=1|page=13|passage=He acts his vvhole life on this earthly ſtage, / In Child-hood, Youth, Man-hood, Decripit age. / The very day that doth afford him light, / Is Morning, the '''Meridian''', Evening, Night.}}
## A midday [[rest#Noun|rest]]; a [[siesta#Noun|siesta]].
##* {{RQ:Scott Monastery|volume=II|chapter=V|pages=187–188|pageref=187|passage="As we have," he said, "in the course of this our toilsome journey, lost our '''meridian''', indulgence shall be given to those of our attendants who shall, from very weariness, be unable to attend the duty at prime, and this by way of misericord or ''indulgentia''."|footer=Footnote * after the word ''meridian'' reads: “The hour of repose at noon, which, in the middle ages, was employed in slumber, and which the monastic rules of nocturnal vigils rendered necessary.”}}
## A particular [[area]] or [[situation]] [[consider]]ed as having a [[specific#Adjective|specific]] [[characteristic#Noun|characteristic]] or [[identity]]; also, the characteristics, [[habit#Noun|habits]], or [[taste#Noun|tastes]] of a specific [[group#Noun|group]], [[locale]], etc. {{defdate|16th–19th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy|edition=2nd|chapter=Diet Rectified in Substance|partition=2|section=2|member=1|subsection=1|pages=200–201|url=https://archive.org/details/2319035R.nlm.nih.gov/page/n280/mode/1up|passage=Diet, {{...}} comprehends thoſe ſixe non naturall things, vvhich I haue before ſpecified, are eſpeciall cauſes, and being rectified, a ſole or chiefe part of the Cure. {{...}} VVhich hovvſoeuer I treat of, as proper to the '''Meridian''' of melancholy, yet neuertheleſſe that vvhich is here ſaid, vvill generally ſerue moſt other diſeaſes, and eaſe them likevviſe, if it be obſerued.}}
##* {{RQ:Jonson Staple of News|chapter=Court Prologue|page=6|passage=A VVorke not ſmelling of the Lampe, to night, / But fitted for your ''Maieſties'' diſport, / And vvrit to the '''''Meridian''''' of your ''Court'', / VVe bring; and hope it may produce delight: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Hale Mankind|chapter=The Introduction, Declaring the Reason of the Choice of This Subject, and the Method of the Intended Discourse|page=7|passage=All other knowledge meerly or principally ſerves the concerns of this Life, and is fitted to the '''meridian''' thereof: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Arbuthnot Law|part=3|chapter=The Publisher’s Preface|page=5|passage=I repreſented to him the good Reception the two firſt Parts had met, that tho' they had been calculated by him, only for the '''Meridian''' of ''Grub-ſtreet'', yet they were taken notice of by the better ſort; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Smollett Roderick Random|volume=I|chapter=XXVIII|page=253|passage=This ſuggeſtion, improbable as it vvas, had the deſired effect upon the captain, being exactly calculated for the '''meridian''' of his intellects; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Smollett Peregrine Pickle|chapter=The Two Friends Eclipse All Their Competitors in Gallantry, and Practise a Pleasant Project of Revenge upon the Physicians of the Place|page=202|column=2|passage=[H]is accompliſhments were exactly calculated for the '''meridian''' of female taſte; and with certain individuals of that ſex, his muſcular frame, and the robuſt connection of his limbs, were more attractive than the delicate proportions of his companion.}}
##* {{RQ:Irving Abbotsford|chapter=Superstitions of the Abbey|page=140|passage=She loves to gossip about the Abbey and {{w|Lord Byron}}, and was soon drawn into a course of anecdotes, though mostly of a humble kind, suited to the '''meridian''' of the housekeeper's room and servants' hall.}}
## {{lb|en|Scotland}} An [[alcoholic#Adjective|alcoholic]] [[drink#Noun|drink]] [[take#Verb|taken]] at midday. {{defdate|18th–19th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Scott Tales of My Landlord 2|volume=I|chapter=III|page=110|passage=Plumdamas joined the other two gentlemen in taking their '''''meridian''''' (a bumper-dram of brandy), as they passed the well-known low-browed shop in the Lawn-Market, where they were wont to take that refreshment.|footer=Due to an error, there are two chapters numbered III in this volume; this is the second one.}}

