====Verb====
{{en-verb}} {{term-label|en|rare|also|figuratively}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[cause#Verb|cause]] an [[object#Noun|object]] to [[reach#Verb|reach]] the [[meridian#Noun|meridian]] or [[high#Adjective|highest]] [[point#Noun|point]] of (something).
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Frederic Alva Dean|chapter=Description of the Ancient Petoséga|title=The Heroines of Petoséga{{nb...|A Novel}}|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Hawthorne Publishing Company{{nb...|42 West 23d Street}}|year=1889|page=10|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=IMimfXA4us0C&pg=PA10|oclc=27037819|passage=Simultaneously with the coming of the mist over earth and sea, where both seem merged into one, slowly and exactly at the same time on each side to the right and left rise and form gorgeous rainbows, that move gently up the sky. They ascend in pairs of the most brilliant color and hue. Upward they move until all the sky is '''meridianed''' with bows, which meet in a grand symphony of color in the zenith.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|chapter=The Eighty-fifth Annual Report of the Board of Foreign Missions of the Presbyterian Church in the United States of America [Shantung Mission]|journal=Minutes of the General Assembly of the [[w:Presbyterian Church in the United States of America|Presbyterian Church in the U.S.A.]] [...] Part III: The Reports of the Boards and Permanent Committees to the One Hundred and Thirty-fourth General Assembly, Des Moines, Iowa, May 18–25, 1922|location=Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher=Office of the General Assembly,{{nb...|Witherspoon Building}}|month=July|year=1922|volume=I (Third Series)|page=157|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/reportsofmission1922pres/page/157/mode/1up|oclc=7823959|passage=At the foot of the promontory on which stands Peng Lai Temple is the little Christian Church of Water City, a suburb of Teng-chou. In the church are hung these words: "One volume, Old and New Testaments, circling earth, '''meridianing''' Heaven. One seven-roomed Worship Hall, backing the sea, facing the City."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:E. L. Mayo|E[dward] L[eslie] Mayo]]|chapter=‘Whose Center is Everywhere’|editor=[[w:David Ray (poet)|David Ray]]|title=Collected Poems|series=A New Letters Book|location=Kansas City, Mo.|publisher=w:University of Missouri; Athens, Oh.: Swallow Press, {{w|Ohio University Press}}|year=1954|year_published=1981|page=86|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/collectedpoems00mayo/page/86/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-938652-00-7|passage=[T]reetops stare / Vertiginous and of two minds; and one / Is to let go; // The other, though, / Is to cling on, seeing clear / It is '''meridianed''' and centered by / The pure blue, the apple of its eye.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} Of a celestial body: to reach its meridian.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Percival Lowell|title=On Martian Longitudes|editors=[[w:George Ellery Hale|George E[llery] Hale]]; [[w:James Edward Keeler|James E[dward] Keeler]]|journal=[[w:The Astrophysical Journal|The Astrophysical Journal: An International Review of Spectroscopy and Astronomical Physics]]|location=Chicago, Ill.|publisher=w:University of Chicago Press|month=May|year=1895|volume=I|issue=5|page=397|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/astrophysicaljou01ameruoft/page/397/mode/1up|issn=0004-637X|oclc=5894574618|passage=At the opposition of 1892 [[w:James Edward Keeler|[James Edward] Keeler]] {{...}} found, on comparing his drawings '''meridianed''' by Marth ephemeris with photographs of a globe made by him from [[w:Giovanni Schiaparelli|[Giovanni] Schiaparelli]]'s chart and set to the longitude and latitude of the time of observation: {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=The Salving of the ‘Senator’|editors=[[w:William Chambers (publisher)|William]]; [[w:Robert Chambers (publisher, born 1802)|Robert Chambers]]|magazine=[[w:Chambers's Edinburgh Journal|Chambers’s Journal]]|location=London; Edinburgh|publisher=W. & R. Chambers|date=25 October 1902|volume=V|issue=256 (Sixth Series)|section=chapter IV|page=741|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/chamberss-journal-ser-6-v-5.1901-1902-dec-nov/page/741/mode/1up|column=1|oclc=793924257|passage=By the time the moon '''meridianed''', the weather had decidedly improved and the sea had gone down.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Proceedings of the Grand Chapter of Mississippi {{w|Order of the Eastern Star}}: Twenty-eighth Annual Session|location=Meridian, Miss.|publisher=Dement Bros. Print. Co.|year=1934|page=181|oclc=18992490|passage=Born in Massachusetts, in 1818, neath the shadow of Bunker Hill, and, incidentally of lineage with [[w:Robert Morris (financier)|Robert Morris]] of Revolutionary fame, ere his life '''meridianed''' removing with his family to beautiful "blue grass Kentucky", the home of his heart, where he wrought well and his memory is revered.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Poems 1951: The Prize-winning Entries for the Festival of Britain|location=Harmondsworth, Middlesex [London]|publisher=w:Penguin Books|year=1951|page=231|oclc=471682788|passage=The countable ribs '''meridianed''' over his blood's tides; / The scar-buttoned archipelago of his flexible spine; {{...}}}}

