===Etymology 3===
[[File:Bronze Man acupuncture figurine, front view, Chinese woodcut Wellcome L0037496.jpg|thumb|A [[woodcut]] in a 1537 [[Chinese#Adjective|Chinese]] [[acupuncture#Noun|acupuncture]] [[work#Noun|work]] [[depict]]ing meridians [[associate#Verb|associated]] with the [[heart#Noun|heart]], [[kidney]]s, [[liver#Noun|liver]], [[lung]]s, [[spleen#Noun|spleen]], and [[stomach#Noun|stomach]].]]

Borrowed from {{bor|en|fr|méridien}} or {{bor|en|de|Meridian|t=pathway on the body along which life force is thought to flow}}, from {{der|en|la|merīdiānum|t=midday; position of the sun at noon; the south}} (see further at [[#Etymology 2|etymology 2]]); the French and German words are {{glossary|calque}}s of {{der|en|cmn|經}}, {{m|cmn|经|tr=jīng|t=pathway on the body along which life force is thought to flow; longitude; warp of woven fabric; to go or pass through}}.<ref name="OED"/>

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|[[acupuncture]]|traditional Chinese medicine}} Any of the [[pathway]]s on the body along which {{l|en|chi|id=life force}} or [[qi]] ([[life force]]) is [[think#Verb|thought]] to [[flow#Verb|flow]] and, therefore, the [[acupoint]]s are [[distribute]]d; especially, one of [[twelve]] such pathways [[associate#Verb|associated]] with [[organ]]s of the body. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}

=====Translations=====
