==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*dyew-}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|frm|meridional}}, from {{der|en|la|merīdiōnālis}}, from {{m|la|merīdiēs|t=noon; south}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/mɪˈɹɪdɪənəl/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-meridional.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# located in the south, [[southern]]; later especially, often pertaining to the southern parts of Europe. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Wotton Elements of Architecture|passage=Offices that require heat {{...}} should be '''meridional'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2021|author=Pablo A. Baisotti|title=A New Struggle for Independence in Modern Latin America|publisher=Routledge
|passage=For much of the 19th century what we now know as South America was called '''Meridional''' America}}
# {{lb|en|astronomy|geography|meteorology}} along a [[north]]-[[south]] direction, or relative to a [[meridian]]; or relating to meridians or a meridian {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#: {{ant|en|zonal}}
# of or characteristic of southern areas or people, especially those in the southern parts of Europe {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1982|author=w:Lawrence Durrell|title=Constance|publisher=Faber & Faber|year_published=2004|series=Avignon Quintet|page=740
|passage=This, Constance recognised, may have had nothing to do with the situation – it was probably just a '''meridional''' convention – for in the Mediterranean countries nobody trusts his neighbour [...].}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2002|author={{w|Colin Jones (historian)|Colin Jones}}|title=The Great Nation|page=270|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2003
|passage=As soon as he heard the news of the trial and execution, he summed up the incident as a monument to Catholic intolerance, '''meridional''' superstition and judicial bigotry – and he decided to do something about it.}}

