Page 1445 ness: -------- begin text --------
{{also|Ness|-ness}}
==English==
{{was wotd|2010|August|18}}
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|nesse}} (in placenames), from {{inh|en|ang|ness}}, {{m|ang|næss}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*nasją|t=promontory; ness}}; cognate with {{cog|gml|nes}}, {{cog|is|nes}}, {{cog|sv|näs}}, {{cog|da|næs}}. Related to {{m|en|nose}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|nĕs}}, {{IPA|en|/nɛs/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-ness.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-ness.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛs|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|geography}} A [[promontory]]; a [[cape]] or [[headland]]. ({{non-gloss definition|Frequently used as a suffix in placenames.}})
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1958|author=Eric Rücker Eddison|title=Zimiamvian Trilogy|volume=3|section=The Mezentian Gate|url=http://books.google.co.uk/books?ei=OXMkTOv0Dpeh_AaC5ZjlBA&ct=result&id=Xa1aAAAAMAAJ&dq=%22ness%22&q=%22sickle-shaped+ness%22|page=177|text=<span style="font-variant:small-caps">Velvraz Sebarm</span> stands upon the lake, among orange-trees and pomegranates and almonds and peaches of the south, a mile north-west over the water from Zayana town, and two miles by land: an old castle built of honey-coloured marble at the tip of a long sickle-shaped '''ness''' that sweeps round southwards, with wild gardens running down in the rocks to the water’s edge, and behind the castle a wood of holm-oaks making a wind-break against the north.}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|Girdle Ness}}
* {{l|en|Little Ness}}

