Page 1481 periegesis: -------- begin text --------
==English==
{{was wotd|2016|November|12}}
[[File:Pausanias Description of Greece.jpg|thumb|A page from a 1485 [[manuscript]] of the {{lang|el|Ἑλλάδος περιήγησις}} (''Helládos periḗgēsis''; ''Description of Greece''), a periegesis by the Greek [[traveller]] and [[geographer]] {{w|Pausanias}} (''c.''&nbsp;110 – ''c.''&nbsp;180), from the collection of the {{w|Laurentian Library}} in [[Florence]], [[Italy]]]]

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|grc-koi|περιήγησις||the action of being shown around, as by a [[guide#Noun|guide]] (''chiefly in the titles of works'')}}, from {{der|en|grc|περι-||[[peri-]], ''prefix meaning ‘[[around]], [[surrounding]]’''}} + {{m|grc|ἥγησις||[[command]], [[leading]]}} (from {{m|grc|ἡγέομαι||to [[lead#Verb|lead]]}} + {{m|grc|-σῐς||''suffix forming nouns''}}), from {{m|grc|περιηγεῖσθαι||to lead around}}; compare {{cog|LL.|periegesis}}. Cognate with {{cog|el|περιήγηση||tour, sightseeing}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˌpɛ.ɹɪ.əˈd͡ʒiː.sɪs/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-periegesis.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˌpɛ.ɹi.əˈd͡ʒi.sɪs/}}
* {{hyphenation|en|pe|ri|e|ge|sis}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|periegeses}}

# A [[description]] of an [[area]] or [[territory]].
#: {{ux|en|a '''periegesis''' of Greece}}
#* {{quote-book|en|editor=w:Colin Macfarquhar|chapter=Dionysius|title=[[w:Encyclopædia Britannica|Encyclopædia Britannica; or, A Dictionary of Arts, Sciences, and Miscellaneous Literature; [...] In Eighteen Volumes, Greatly Improved]]|edition=3rd|location=Edinburgh|publisher=Printed for [[w:Andrew Bell (engraver)|A[ndrew] Bell]] and C[olin] Macfarquhar|year=1797|volume=VI (DIA–ETH)|page=33, column 1|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=CXpMAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA33|oclc=222789080|passage=[[w:Dionysius Periegetes|{{smallcaps|Dionysius}}]], a learned geographer, to whom is attributed a '''Periegeſis''', or Survey of the Earth, in Greek verse.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Art. I.—1. ''Griechische Heroen Geschichten.'' Von B. G. Niebuhr an seinen Sohn erzählt. Hamburg, 1842. Grecian Heroic Stories; related by [[w:Barthold Georg Niebuhr|B[arthold] G[eorg] Niebuhr]] to his Son.|journal=[[w:Westminster Review|The Westminster Review]]|edition=American|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Published by Leonard Scott & Co., 112 [[w:Fulton Street (Manhattan)|Fulton Street]]|month=May|year=1843|volume=XXXIX|issue=LXXVII|page=158|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=fPEgAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA158|oclc=507147293|passage=[H]ad we been able to obtain a '''periegesis''' of Greece for the year 776 {{smallcaps|b.c.}}, we should have discovered from one end of the country to the other nothing but legends, preached by the men of genius, received both with earnest emotion and with sincere faith by the hearers.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Otto J. Maenchen-Helfen|J. Otto [Otto John] Maenchen-Helfen]]|chapter=Early Huns in Eastern Europe|editor=Max Knight|title=The World of the Huns: Studies in Their History and Culture|location=Berkeley, Calif.|publisher=w:University of California Press|year=1973|pages=445–446|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=CrUdgzSICxcC&pg=PA446|isbn=978-0-520-01596-8|passage=Where did {{w|Cassiodorus}} read that the [[Hun]]s were the neighbors of the [[Seres]], supposedly the Chinese? There is little doubt that his source was one of the popular compendia which, directly or indirectly, went back to [[w:Dionysius Periegetes|Dionysius [Periegetes]]]' '''''Periegesis'''''. Cassiodorus recommended "the map of Dionysius" to his monks. It is not particularly significant that in both the ''{{w|Getica}}'' and the '''''Periegesis''''' the Caspian Sea is a gulf of the ocean; this was a belief held and combatted since the time of the Ionian geographers.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Christian Jacob|chapter=Mapping in the Mind: The Earth from Ancient Alexandria|editor=[[w:Denis Cosgrove|Denis [E.] Cosgrove]]|title=Mappings|series=Critical Views|location=London|publisher=w:Reaktion Books|year=1999|isbn=978-1-86189-021-4|passage=There is not a single ancient Greek source that depicts someone using maps in a practical situation. {{...}} '[G]eographical knowledge' did not depend on maps, but on other media, such as travel reports, sea journeys and '''periegeses''', descriptions of a particular country. Geography relied on words and discourses, on human memory.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Ian Rutherford|chapter=Tourism and the Sacred: Pausanias and the Traditions of Greek Pilgrimage|editors=[[w:Susan E. Alcock|Susan E[llen] Alcock]]; w:John F. Cherry; w:Jaś Elsner|title=Pausanias: Travel and Memory in Roman Greece|location=Oxford|publisher=w:Oxford University Press|year=2001|page=45|isbn=978-0-19-512816-1|passage=A '''''periegesis''''' is usually understood to be a geographical catalogue, cast in the form of a tour, often of a local area but sometimes more extended in scope. The form is related to local history. Modem scholarship has established that there was a specifically periegetic form of historiography, stretching back at least to the third century {{smallcaps|b.c.}}; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=William Hutton|title=Describing Greece: Landscape and Literature in the ''Periegesis'' of Pausanias|series=Greek Culture in the Roman World|location=Cambridge|publisher=w:Cambridge University Press|year=2005|pages=271–272|isbn=978-0-521-84720-9|passage=This inquiry into Pausanias' generic affinities may seem to have come up with mostly negative results: the '''''Periegesis''''' is not a travel guide; it is not a geography; it is not a work like that of Herakleides Kritikos; it is not a full-blown imitation of [[w:Herodotus|Herodotean]] historiography, nor of [[w:Arrian|Arrianic]] epistolography; it is not a ''periplous'', and it is not, finally, a '''''periegesis'''''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Maria Pretzler|chapter=Approaching Pausanias' ''Periegesis''|title=Pausanias: Travel Writing in Ancient Greece|series=Classical Literature and Society Series|location=London|publisher=[[w:Bloomsbury Publishing|Bloomsbury Academic]]|year=2007|page=1|isbn=978-0-7156-3496-7|passage=Few readers approach [[w:Pausanias (geographer)|Pausanias]]' '''''Periegesis''''' from the beginning, sailing round Cape Sounion and arriving in Athens, before following his convoluted routes through Greece. Not many, in fact, have read the whole work, but almost anyone with an interest in ancient Greece will have 'come across' Pausanias&nbsp;– usually by looking up particular passages: the '''''Periegesis''''' contains useful material for many purposes.}}

