Page 1513 rand: -------- begin text --------
{{also|Rand|RAND|rând}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɹænd/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-rand.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ænd|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|rand}}, from {{inh|en|ang|rand|t=edge, border, margin, rim, shore}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*randaz}}, {{m|gem-pro|*randō|t=edge, rim, crust}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*rem-|t=to rest, prop or support oneself}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|rand|t=edge, border, outskirts, rim}}, {{cog|de|Rand|t=edge, border, margin, rim, outskirts}}, {{cog|sv|rand|t=rand, stripe, edge, verge}}. Related to {{m|en|rind}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete|now|_|dialect}} The [[border]] of an area of land, especially [[marshland]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete|now|_|dialect}} A strip of [[meat]]; a long fleshy piece of beef, cut from the flank or leg; a sort of steak.
#* {{RQ:Beaumont Fletcher Comedies and Tragedies|The Wild-Goose Chase|passage=They came with chopping-knives / To cut me into '''rands''' and sirloins}}
# {{lb|en|dialect}} A [[border]], [[edge]] or [[rim]].
#: {{ux|en|At the wald's '''rand'''.}}
# A strip of leather used to fit the heels of a shoe.
# {{lb|en|basket-making}} A single [[rod]] woven in and out of the [[stake]]s.

====References====
* [http://www.dsl.ac.uk/entry/snd/rand_n_v1 The Dictionary of the Scots Language]

===Etymology 2===
Named after {{w|Witwatersrand}}; the last element is {{der|en|af|rand||ridge}}, from {{der|en|nl|rand}}, from {{der|en|osx|-}} ''rand'', from Germanic ''*randaz.'' Compare Etymology 1, and ''[[Rand]]''.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|s|rand}}

# A rocky slope, especially the area over a river valley; specifically, the [[Rand]]
# The currency of [[South Africa]], divided into 100 [[cent]]s.

