===Etymology 2===
From {{der|en|frm|retrograder|t=to (cause to) go back, return; to (cause to) move backwards; of a celestial object: to show retrograde motion; to date to an earlier period}} (modern {{cog|fr|rétrograder}}), and from its {{glossary|etymon}} {{der|en|la|retrōgradī}}, the {{glossary|present}} {{glossary|active}} {{glossary|infinitive}} of {{m|la|retrōgradior|t=to go or step back or backwards; of a celestial object: to show retrograde motion}} (compare {{cog|LL.|retrogradare}}, {{m|LL.|retrogradari}}, {{m|LL.|retrogredere}}), from {{m|la|retrō|t=back, backwards; behind; before, formerly}} + {{m|la|gradior|t=to step, walk; to advance, go}} (ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*gʰredʰ-|t=to go; to walk}}).<ref>Compare {{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=164520|date=September 2020|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=v}}</ref>

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}}
## {{lb|en|geography}} To [[cause#Verb|cause]] (a [[land#Noun|land]] [[feature#Noun|feature]] such as a [[coastline]] or [[waterfall]]) to [[undergo]] [[retrogradation]], that is, to [[travel#Verb|travel]] in the [[direction]] of the land or [[upstream]] due to [[erosion]].
## {{lb|en|geology}} To [[change#Noun|change]] ([[mineral#Noun|minerals]], [[rock#Noun|rocks]], etc.) [[metamorphically]] through a [[decrease#Noun|decrease]] in [[pressure#Noun|pressure]] or [[temperature]].
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} To cause (someone or something) to [[revert#Verb|revert]] to an [[inferior#Adjective|inferior]] or [[less]] [[developed#Adjective|developed]] [[state#Noun|state]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive}}
## To revert to an inferior or less developed state; to [[decline#Adjective|decline]], to [[regress#Verb|regress]].
##* {{RQ:Eliot Romola|volume=III|page=260|passage={{...}} Monna Brigida, who had '''retrograded''' to false hair in Romola's absence, but now drew it off again and declared she would not mind being gray, if her dear child would stay with her.}}
## {{lb|en|astrology|astronomy}} Of a [[celestial body]], especially a [[planet]]: to [[show#Verb|show]] retrogradation; to [[seem]] to [[move#Verb|move]] across the [[sky#Noun|sky]] in the opposite direction from its [[ordinary]] [[movement]].
## {{lb|en|geography}} Of a land feature: to travel in the direction of the land or upstream due to erosion.
## {{lb|en|military}} To [[retreat#Verb|retreat]] or [[withdraw]] from a [[position#Noun|position]].
## {{lb|en|obsolete}}
### To [[move#Verb|move]] [[backwards]]; to [[recede]].
###* {{quote-book|en|author=Joseph C. Neal|chapter=The Moral of Goslyne Greene, who was Born to a Fortune|title=The Gift: A Christmas, New Year, and Birthday Present|location=Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher=[[w:Carey & Hart|Carey and Hart]]|year=1845|page=68|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=iq8QAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA68|oclc=2914286|passage=A dabble in the stocks does not always turn out profitably; cotton is sometimes heavy on our hands, and real estate will sulkily '''retrograde''', when, by the calculation, it ought to have advanced.}}
### Of the [[telling#Noun|telling]] of an [[incident#Noun|incident]], etc.: to move to an [[early|earlier]] [[time#Noun|time]].

