===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|right}}, {{m|enm|righte}}, from {{inh|en|ang|rihte}}, {{m|ang|rehte|t=right; rightly; due; directly; straight}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*rehta}}, from {{m|gem-pro|*rehtaz|t=right; straight}}.

====Adverb====
{{en-adv|-}}

# On the [[right#Adjective|right]] side.
# Towards the right side.
# [[exactly|Exactly]], [[precisely]].
#: {{ux|en|The arrow landed '''right''' in the middle of the target.}}
#: {{ux|en|Luckily we arrived '''right''' at the start of the film.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1913|author=w:Joseph C. Lincoln
|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL5535161W Mr. Pratt's Patients]|chapter=1
|passage=Then there came a reg'lar terror of a sou'wester same as you don't get one summer in a thousand, and blowed the shanty flat and ripped about half of the weir poles out of the sand. We spent consider'ble money getting 'em reset, and then a swordfish got into the pound and tore the nets all to slathers, '''right''' in the middle of the squiteague season.}}
# [[immediately|Immediately]], [[directly]].
#: {{ux|en|Can't you see it? It's '''right''' beside you!}}
#: {{ux|en|Tom was standing '''right''' in front of the TV, blocking everyone's view.}}
# {{lb|en|British|US|dialect}} [[very|Very]], [[extremely]], [[quite]].
#: {{ux|en|I made a '''right''' stupid mistake there, didn't I?}}
#: {{ux|en|I stubbed my toe a week ago and it still hurts '''right''' much.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=[http://quod.lib.umich.edu/e/eebo/A17689.0001.001?view=toc Of the life or conuersation of a Christen man, a right godly treatise]|author=w:John Calvin|year=1549|passage=a '''right''' godly treatise}}<!-- Good example of very common older usage -->
#* {{quote-book|en|title=w:Valley of the Dolls|page=214|author=w:Jacqueline Susann|year=1966|passage=That's long enough for any small town." Lyon leaned forward. "Do you like Lawrenceville, Mr. Hill?" The driver cocked his head. "Aeah. Why not? Born here. It's a '''right''' nice town|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=FMSUrZYIYF0C&pg=PA214&dq=%22right+kind%7Cnice%22+town%7Cfarm%7Ctree%7Cforest%7Cfield%7Cvillage++-%22the+right+kind%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=EllfT4mBPKHo0QGzwdGLBw&ved=0CM4BEOgBMBs#v=onepage&q=%22right%20kind%7Cnice%22%20town%7Cfarm%7Ctree%7Cforest%7Cfield%7Cvillage%20%20-%22the%20right%20kind%22&f=false}}<!--Aeah => Maine or, at least, New England. Context is Massachusetts)) -->
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Nebraska nightmare|author=Jon Sharpe|year=2004|passage=Well, that would be '''right''' neighborly of you, miss.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Red Mesa|page=101|author=Luke Cypher|year=2008|passage=But it would be '''right''' neighborly and Christian of you to put your own wants aside for a spell.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Ann Hite|title=Ghost on Black Mountain|text=The fog was '''right''' hard to see through so I was on Tom Pritchard before I saw him.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2015|author=Jeff Torrington|title=Swing Hammer Swing!|page=255
|passage=Kids nowadays were a '''right''' thrillproof bunch. The Armoury Section had, unexpectedly, proved to be a real moodclunker.}}
# According to fact or truth; [[actually]]; [[truly]]; [[really]].
# In a [[correct]] [[manner]].
#: {{ux|en|Do it '''right''' or don't do it at all.}}
# {{lb|en|dated|still used in some titles}} To a great extent or degree.
#: {{ux|en|Sir, I am '''right''' glad to meet you &hellip;}}
#: {{ux|en|Members of the Queen's Privy Council are styled The '''Right''' Honourable for life.}}
#: {{ux|en|The '''Right''' Reverend Monsignor Guido Sarducci.}}
#* {{RQ:Jack Straw|IV|79|Sith mercie in a Prince reſembleth '''right''',<br>The gladſome ſunne-ſhine in a winters day, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1913|author=w:Joseph C. Lincoln|chapter=13
|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL5535161W Mr. Pratt's Patients]
|passage=He b'iled '''right''' over, and the tongue-lashing he give that boss Right Liver beat anything I ever listened to. There was heap of Scriptur' language in it, and more brimstone than you'd find in a match factory.}}

=====Usage notes=====
In the US, the word "right" is used as an adverb meaning "very, quite" in most of the major dialect areas, including the Southern US, Appalachia, New England{{,}} and the Midwest, though the usage is not part of standard US English. In the UK also it is not part of the standard language but is regarded as stereotypical of the dialects of northern England, though it occurs in other dialects also.

