===Etymology 3===
From {{inh|en|enm|right}}, {{m|enm|righte}}, {{m|enm|reght}}, {{m|enm|reghte}}, {{m|enm|riȝt}}, {{m|enm|riȝte}}, from {{inh|en|ang|riht}}, {{m|ang|reht}}, {{m|ang|ġeriht|t=that which is right, just, or proper; a right; due; law; canon; rule; direction; justice; equity; standard}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*reht}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*rehtą|t=a right}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*h₃reǵtom}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₃reǵt-|t=to straighten; direct}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|recht|t=a right; privilege}}, {{cog|de|Recht|t=a right}}, {{cog|da|ret|t=a right}}.

====Noun====
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun}}

# That which complies with [[justice]], [[law]] or [[reason]].
#: {{ux|en|We're on the side of '''right''' in this contest.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en
|date=July 22, 1973
|origdate=July 17, 1973
|author=[[w:Chiang Kai-shek|Kai-shek Chiang]]
|title=President Chiang Kai-shek's message to the mass rally supporting Captive Nations Week
|journal=Free China Weekly
|url=https://archive.org/details/sim_free-china-weekly_1973-07-22_14_28/
|volume=XIV
|issue=28
|location=[[Taipei]]
|issn=0016-0318
|oclc=1786626
|page=1
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_free-china-weekly_1973-07-22_14_28/
|text=Throughout our history, whenever evil forces prevailed, the altruistic and upright people have always shown their great wisdom by adhering to the '''right''' against the wrong, renouncing wrongful gain for justice, displaying their great benevolence in national salvation and summoning their great courage to surmount the crisis and turn back the perverse tide.}}
# A legal, just or moral [[entitlement]].
#: {{ux|en|You have no '''right''' to go through my personal diary.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1825|author=w:Samuel Taylor Coleridge|title=w:Table Talk
|passage=There are no '''rights''' whatever, without corresponding duties.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Sketches of Life and Character|passage=
"I do not know that you have any '''right''' to inquire into reasons for my conduct. I am at least sure that I never gave you any such '''right'''," replied Wiley.<br>"I claim no '''right''' but the common '''right''' of humanity," said the old gentleman. "If you do not acknowledge that, my interference in this matter can only be viewed as impertinent."|year=1850|author=T. S. Arthur|page=130|authorlink=Timothy Shay Arthur|pageurl=https://www.google.com/books/edition/Sketches_of_Life_and_Character/mB0tAAAAYAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&pg=PA130|chapter=Seed Time and Harvest|oclc=1087079145|publisher=J. W. Bradley|location=Philadelphia|url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/Sketches_of_Life_and_Character/mB0tAAAAYAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=0}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1922|author=w:Michael Arlen|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL1519647W “Piracy”: A Romantic Chronicle of These Days]|chapter=3/19/2
|passage=Ivor had acquired more than a mile of fishing '''rights''' with the house ; he was not at all a good fisherman, but one must do something ; one generally, however, banged a ball with a squash-racket against a wall.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-08-10|volume=408|issue=8848|magazine=w:The Economist|author=Schumpeter
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/business/21583242-businesspeople-have-become-too-influential-government-cronies-and-capitols Cronies and capitols]
|passage=Policing the relationship between government and business in a free society is difficult. Businesspeople have every '''right''' to lobby governments, and civil servants to take jobs in the private sector.}}
# The right side or direction.
#: {{ux|en|The pharmacy is just on the '''right''' past the bookshop.}}
# The right hand or fist.
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1897|author=w:Bram Stoker|title=Dracula|year_published=1993|page=129|passage="Before he could strike again, however, I got in my '''right''', and he was sprawling on his back on the floor."}}
# {{senseid|en|copyright}}The authority to perform, publish, film, or televise a particular work, event, etc.; a [[copyright]].
# {{lb|en|politics}} The [[ensemble]] of right-wing [[political]] parties; political [[conservatives]] as a group.
#: {{ux|en|The political '''right''' holds too much power.}}
#*{{quote-journal|en|date=2023 May 31|author=Nigel Harris|title=Comment: GBR now! We have no Plan B|journal=RAIL|issue=984|page=3|text=Sunak seems so scared of his party's swivel-eyed right wing that he has been panicked into focusing all new legislation on perceived 'red meat' issues which he hopes the Tory '''right''' will support.}}
# The outward or most finished surface, as of a coin, piece of cloth, a carpet, etc.
#: {{syn|en|right side|q1=of fabric}}
#* {{quote-book|en |year=1890|title=The Woman's World |author=|page=434|ISBN=|passage=Simple cross-stitch, with a space between each stitch, may be worked in two rows, in which case the completed stitch on the wrong sides alternates with that on the '''right'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en |year=1913|title=Woman's Home Companion - Volume 40 |author=|page=40|ISBN=|passage=For the large size, two pieces of silk, eighteen inches  wide and twenty-seven inches long, are sewed together at three sides, '''rights''' together, leaving one end open.}}
#* {{quote-book|en |year=1918 |title=Pacific Rural Press - Volume 95 |author=|page=392|ISBN=|passage=In case there is a right and wrong side to the tops, put two '''rights''' together.}}
# {{lb|en|surfing}} A [[wave]] breaking from right to left (viewed from the shore).
#: {{antonyms|en|left}}

