===Etymology 4===
From {{inh|en|enm|righten}}, {{m|enm|reghten}}, {{m|enm|riȝten}}, from {{inh|en|ang|rihtan}}, {{m|ang|ġerihtan|t=to straighten, judge, set upright, set right}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*rihtijan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*rihtijaną|t=to straighten; rectify; judge}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[correct]].
#: {{ux|en|'''Righting''' all the wrongs of the war immediately will be impossible.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To set [[upright]].
#: {{ux|en|The tow-truck '''righted''' what was left of the automobile.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To return to normal upright position.
#: {{ux|en|When the wind died down, the ship '''righted'''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To do justice to; to [[relieve]] from wrong; to [[restore]] rights to; to [[assert]] or [[regain]] the rights of.
#: {{ux|en|to '''right''' the oppressed}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 3|text=So just is God, to '''right''' the innocent.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1776|author=Thomas Jefferson; John Adams|title=w:Declaration of Independence
|passage=All experience hath shewn that mankind are more disposed to suffer while evils are sufferable, than to '''right''' themselves by abolishing the forms to which they are accustomed.}}

=====Derived terms=====
{{col4|en
