Page 1534 salient: -------- begin text --------
==English==
{{was wotd|2006|April|13}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*sel-}}
The heraldic sense “leaping” and the sense “projecting outward” are borrowed from {{bor|en|la|salientem}}, the accusative form of {{m|la|saliēns|t=springing, leaping}}, present participle of {{m|la|saliō|t=leap, spring|pos=verb}}. The senses “prominent” and “pertinent” are relatively recent, and derive from the phrase {{m|en|salient point}}, which is a calque of the {{calque|en|la|punctum saliēns|notext=1}}, a translation of Aristotle's term for the embryonal heart visible in (opened) eggs, which he thought seemed to move already. Compare also the German calque {{m|de|der springende Punkt}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈseɪ.li.ənt/|/ˈseɪ.ljənt/}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-salient.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{a|AU}} {{IPA|en|/ˈsæɪ.li.ənt/|/ˈsæɪ.ljənt/}}
** {{audio|en|en-au-salient.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{hyphenation|en|sa|lient}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[worthy|Worthy]] of [[note]]; [[pertinent]] or [[relevant]].
#: {{syn|en|pertinent|relevant|Thesaurus:pertinent}}
#: {{ux|en|The article is not exhaustive, but it covers the '''salient''' points pretty well.}}
#* {{RQ:Hardy Return of the Native|chapter=V|passage=With nearer approach these fragmentary sounds became pieced together, and were found to be the '''salient''' points of the tune called "Nancy's Fancy."}}
#* {{RQ:Wells War of the Worlds|chapter=II|passage=The last '''salient''' point in which the systems of these creatures differed from ours was in what one might have thought a very trivial particular.}}
# [[prominent|Prominent]]; [[conspicuous]].
#: {{ant|en|obscure|trivial}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1936|author=w:H.P. Lovecraft|title=s:The Shadow Over Innsmouth|passage=Warning me that many of the street signs were down, the youth drew for my benefit a rough but ample and painstaking sketch map of the town's '''salient''' features.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1834|author=w:George Bancroft|title=History of the United States of America, from the discovery of the American continent
|passage=He [Grenville] had neither '''salient''' traits, nor general comprehensiveness of mind.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2018|author=James Lambert|title=A multitude of ‘lishes’: The nomenclature of hybridity|journal=English World-Wide|page=2|url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/318367113_A_multitude_of_lishes_The_nomenclature_of_hybridity|text=Professionally published dictionaries do not seem to have extended coverage beyond the most frequent and '''salient''' items.}}
# {{lb|en|heraldry|usually of a [[quadruped]]}} Depicted in a [[leaping]] [[posture]]. [[File:Vågå komm.svg|right|thumb|Gules, a reindeer salient or (arms of Vågå).]]
#: {{ux|en|a lion '''salient'''}}
# {{lb|en|often|military}} [[project|Projecting]] [[outwards]], [[point]]ing outwards.
#: {{ux|en|a '''salient''' angle}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Moving by leaps or springs; jumping.
#* {{RQ:Browne Pseudodoxia Epidemica|edition=2nd|passage=frogs and '''salient''' animals}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Shooting or springing out; projecting.
#* {{RQ:Burke Noble Lord|page=50|passage=He had in himſelf a '''ſalient''', living ſpring of generous and manly action.}}
# {{lb|en|geometry}} Denoting any [[angle]] less than two [[right angle]]s.

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|salient point}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|salience}}
* {{l|en|saliency}}

