Page 1547 serac: -------- begin text --------
{{also|sérac}}
==English==
{{was wotd|2018|January|16}}

===Etymology===
{{multiple images
|direction = vertical
|image1    = Zürich und Schabziger 004.jpg
|caption1  = A piece of serac ''(sense 1)'', or [[Schabziger]], [[cheese]] being [[grate#Verb|grated]]
|image2    = Cordée dalpinistes dans le sérac des Bossons, Mont Blanc (5488516448).jpg
|caption2  = An 1859–1910 photograph of [[mountaineers]] passing seracs ''(sense 2)'' of the {{w|Bossons Glacier}} in the {{w|Mont Blanc massif}} in the [[Alps]], from the collection of the Bibliothèque de Toulouse in [[Toulouse]], [[France]]
}}

From {{bor|en|fr-CH|sérac||kind of cheese; sharp tower of ice}}, from {{der|en|frp|sera}}, {{m|frp|seré}}, from {{der|en|la|seraceum}}, from {{m|la|serum||whey}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*ser-||to flow, run}}. Sense 2 is from the resemblance of the towers of ice to the cheese, which tends to cleave into rectangular pieces.<ref>{{cite-book|title=A Hand-book for Travellers in Switzerland, and the Alps of Savoy and Piedmont|series=Murray’s Handbooks for Travellers|edition=15th rev.|location=London|publisher=[[w:John Murray (publisher)|John Murray]], {{w|Albemarle Street}}; Paris: [[w:John Anthony Galignani|Galignani & Co.]]; Boyveau|year=1874|page=lxxiii|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Hw4IAAAAQAAJ&pg=PR73|column=2|oclc=922779628|passage=''Sérac''—Tower of a glacier ice-fall, ice-castle; name derived from a thin cheese which splits into rectangular pieces.}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/sɛˈɹæk/|/ˈsɛɹæk/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-serac.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-serac2.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/sɛˈɹæk/}}
* {{hyphenation|en|se|rac}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# ''Often'' '''{{l|en|sérac}}''': a [[hard]], [[cone]]-[[shaped#Adjective|shaped]], [[pale#Adjective|pale]] [[green#Adjective|green]], [[strongly]] [[flavoured]] [[cheese]] from [[Switzerland]] made from [[skimmed#Adjective|skimmed]] [[cowmilk]] and [[blue fenugreek]] ({{taxlink|Trigonella caerulea|species}}); [[Schabziger]], [[Sapsago]]. It is usually eaten [[grate#Verb|grated]], mixed with [[butter#Noun|butter]], or in a [[fondue]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=The Museum of Foreign Literature and Science|location=[Philadelphia, Pa.]|publisher=Published by [[w:Eliakim Littell|E[liakim] Littell]] & T. Holden; New York, N.Y.: G. & C. & H. Carvill; Boston, Mass.: Kane & Co.|year=1826|volume=IX|column=2|oclc=1013335311|passage=The smoke found its way out of the holes in the roof as it liked best; and nearly over the fire was a black smoky shelf, supporting several masses of that inferior white cheese, called '''Serac'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|magazine=[[w:Alpine Journal|The Alpine Journal: A Record of Mountain Adventure and Scientific Observation. By Members of the Alpine Club]]|location=London|publisher=[[w:Longman|Longman, Green, Longman, Roberts, & Green]]|year=1891|volume=XV|page=214|oclc=53876251|passage=Here and elsewhere the '''sérac''', which is something between curd and cheese, may be advantageously taken instead of butter, which is almost always bad.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Switzerland|series=[[w:Michelin Guides#Green Guides|Michelin Green Guides]]|location=Watford, Hertfordshire|publisher=[[w:Michelin Guides|Michelin Travel Publications]]|year=2001|page=184|isbn=978-2-06-000119-7|passage=Note the Alpine cheese dairy on the Iseltenalp, where fresh dairy produce are made every day (milk, butter, cheese and '''serac''').}}
# {{lb|en|geography|glaciology}} A [[sharp#Adjective|sharp]] [[tower#Noun|tower]] of [[ice#Noun|ice]] formed by [[intersecting#Adjective|intersecting]] [[crevasse]]s of a [[glacier]]. {{defdate|from mid 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=A Hand-book for Travellers in Switzerland and the Alps of Savoy and Piedmont: Including the Protestant Valleys of the Waldenses|series=Murray’s Handbooks for Travellers|location=London|publisher=[[w:John Murray (publisher)|John Murray]]; Leipzig: Black and Armstrong; Paris: [[w:John Anthony Galignani|Galignani]]|year=1838|page=368|column=2|oclc=922779628|passage=Perhaps the finest part of it [the {{w|Lysjoch}}] is the grand '''''sérac''''' scenery of the ''Zwillinge Glacier''. In August, 1876, a party of four, having missed the right track in crossing this pass when the snow was in very bad condition, were carried down in an avalanche of loose snow probably started by their own weight.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Charles Williams|chapter=VI|title=The Alps, Switzerland, and the North of Italy: With Numerous Engravings|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Printed by Alexander Montgomery, 17, [[w:Spruce Street (Manhattan)|Spruce-Street]]|year=1854|page=82|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=CHgSAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA82|oclc=886359906|passage=Numerous blocks of ice, to which the name "'''serac'''" is given, covered the plain. These are large fragments of frozen snow, almost rectangular. They take their name from the resemblance they bear to a compact cheese called "serac," which is made from skimmed milk, and pressed into rectangular cans.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=W. Wigram|title=[Notes and Queries.] Intersection of the Lines of Bedding of the Névé by Blue Veins of Pure Ice|editor=H. B. George|magazine=[[w:Alpine Journal|The Alpine Journal: A Record of Mountain Adventure and Scientific Observation. By Members of the Alpine Club]]|location=London|publisher=[[w:Longman|Longman, Green, Longman, Roberts, & Green]]|month=March|year=1863|year_published=1864|volume=I|issue=I|page=46|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=J4c5AAAAcAAJ&pg=PA46|oclc=53876251|passage=As you mount the Aletschhorn from the sleeping place, towards the lowest col from which you first overlook the Lotsch Glacier there descends, from a higher plateau, and on your right hand, a large mass of '''sérac'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Jamie Ambrose; Robert Dinwiddie; John Farndon; Tim Harris; David Summers|chapter=Glaciers and Ice-sheets|editor=Peter Frances|title=Natural Wonders of the World|location=London|publisher=w:Dorling Kindersley|month=October|year=2017|page=233|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=8ck1DwAAQBAJ&pg=PA233|isbn=978-0-2412-7629-7|passage=The Boltoro Glacier {{...}} The surface of the glacier is rough and uneven, broken by crevasses and covered by '''seracs''' and rock debris.}}

