===Etymology 4===
From {{inh|en|enm|sounden}}, from {{der|en|fro|sonder}}, from {{m|fro|sonde||sounding line}} of {{der|en|gem|-}} origin, compare {{cog|ang|sundgyrd||a sounding rod}}, {{m|ang|sundline||a sounding line}}, {{cog|ang|sund||water, sea}}. More at [[#Etymology 3|Etymology 3 above]].

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} Of a [[whale]], to [[dive]] downwards.
#: {{ux|en|The whale '''sounded''' and eight hundred feet of heavy line streaked out of the line tub before he ended his dive.}}
# To [[ascertain]], or to try to ascertain, the thoughts, motives, and purposes of (a person); to examine; to try; to test; to probe.
#: {{ux|en|When I '''sounded''' him, he appeared to favor the proposed deal.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 2|I|i|23|1|Tell me moreouer, haſt thou '''ſounded''' him,<br>If he appeale the Duke on ancient malice,<br>Or worthily as a good ſubiect ſhould<br>On ſome knowne ground of treacherie in him.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Indian Emperour|40|IV|i|passage=I was in Jeſt: <br> And by that offer meant to '''ſound''' your breaſt.}}
#* {{RQ:Addison Cato|1|1|passage=I've '''sounded''' my Numidians man by man.}}
# To [[fathom]] or [[test]]; to ascertain the depth of water with a sounding line or other device.
#: {{ux|en|Mariners on sailing ships would '''sound''' the depth of the water with a weighted rope.}}
#* {{RQ:Marlowe Tamburlaine|part=1|scene=ii|page=46|passage=As when the Sea-man {{...}}<br>All fearefull foldes his ſailes, and '''ſounds''' the maine,<br>Lifting his prayers to the heauens for aid,<br>Againſt the terrour of the winds and waues.}}
# {{lb|en|medicine}} To [[examine]] with the instrument called a sound or sonde, or by [[auscultation]] or [[percussion]].
#: {{ux|en|to '''sound''' a patient, or the bladder or urethra}}

