Page 1562 steppe: -------- begin text --------
{{also|Steppe}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|de|Steppe}} or {{der|en|fr|steppe}}, in turn from {{der|en|ru|степь||flat grassy plain}} or {{cog|uk|степ}}. There is no generally accepted earlier etymology, but there is a speculative {{cog|orv|-}} reconstruction *{{m|orv||сътепь|trampled place, flat, bare}}, related to {{m|orv|топот}}, {{m|orv|топтать}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK|US}} {{IPA|en|/stɛp/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vininn126-steppe.wav|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛp}}
* {{homophones|en|step}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The [[grassland]]s of [[Eastern Europe]] and [[Asia]]. Similar to (North American) [[prairie]] and (African) [[savanna]]. {{defdate|from 1671}}
#*{{RQ:Carlyle Sartor Resartus|chapter=Preliminary|page=3|passage=Nevertheless be it remarked, that even a Russian '''steppe''' has tumuli and gold ornaments {{…}}}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1981|author=w:William Irwin Thompson|title=The Time Falling Bodies Take to Light: Mythology, Sexuality and the Origins of Culture|publisher=Rider/Hutchinson & Co.|location=London|page=187|passage=Enkidu was the hairy man of the wild '''steppes''', and Gilgamesh was the hero of civilization; but now the contrast is between Gilgamesh, the king, the man of political power, the heroic man of action, the extrovert, and Utnapishtim, the man of religious authority, the introvert, the sage.}}
# A vast, cold, dry grass-plain.
#* {{quote-web|en|year=2000|author=Mary Elizabeth v. N.|title=Steppe|work=Blue Planet Biomes|publisher=West Tisbury Elementary School|url=http://www.blueplanetbiomes.org/steppe.htm|passage=Grasslands: The '''Steppe''' biome is a dry, cold, grassland that is found in all of the continents except Australia and Antarctica. It is mostly found in the USA, Mongolia, Siberia, Tibet and China. There isn't much humidity in the air because '''Steppe''' is located away from the ocean and close to mountain barriers.}}

====Usage notes====
Although it may be the steppe [[biome]], one would not normally speak of the steppes of Canada, whereas one would speak of the steppes of Asia or the steppes of Russia.

