Page 1564 strait: -------- begin text --------
{{also|Sträit}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|streit||obsolete}}
* {{alter|en|streight||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|streit}}, from {{der|en|fro|estreit}} (modern form {{m|fr|étroit}}), from {{der|en|la|strictus}}, perfect passive participle of {{m|la|stringō||compress, tighten}}. {{doublet|en|stretto|strict}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/stɹeɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-strait.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪt|s=1}}
* {{homophones|en|straight}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er}}

# {{lb|en|archaic}} [[narrow|Narrow]]; [[restricted]] as to [[space]] or [[room]]; [[close]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1866|author=w:Algernon Swinburne|chapter=Aholibah|title=Poems and Ballads|location=London|publisher=John Camden Hotten|page=311|url=https://archive.org/details/b29012685
|passage=Sweet oil was poured out on thy head<br>And ran down like cool rain between<br>The '''strait''' close locks it melted in.}}
#* {{RQ:Emerson May-Day|pages=14–15|pageref=15|passage=Where shall we keep the holiday, / And duly greet the entering May? / Too '''strait''' and low our cottage doors, / And all unmeet our carpet floors; {{...}}}}
#* '''1894''', {{w|Ernest Dowson}}, “To One in Bedlam” in ''The Second Book of The Rhymers’ Club'', London: Elkin Mathews & John Lane, p. 35,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/secondbookofrhym00rhym]</sup>
#*: Those scentless wisps of straw, that miserably line
#*: His '''strait''', caged universe, whereat the dull world stares,
#*: [[pedant|Pedant]] and pitiful.
# {{lb|en|archaic}} [[righteous|Righteous]], [[strict]].
#: {{ux|en|to follow the '''strait''' and narrow}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-1|act=IV|scene=iii|passage=[he] takes on him to reform / Some certain edicts and some '''strait''' decrees / That lie too heavy on the commonwealth,}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Acts|26|5|passage={{...}} after the most '''straitest''' sect of our religion I lived a Pharisee.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[tight|Tight]]; [[close]]; [[tight-fitting]].
#* {{RQ:Beaumont Fletcher Comedies and Tragedies|title=Two Noble Kinsmen|act=III|scene=vii|passage=''Palamon''. {{...}} Stay a little, / Is not this peece too '''streight'''? / ''Arcite''. No, no, tis well.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[close|Close]]; [[intimate]]; [[near]]; [[familiar]].
#* {{RQ:Sidney Arcadia|book=2|chapter=22|passage=After the noble Prince ''Leonatus'' had by his fathers death succeeded in the kingdome of ''Galatia'', he (forgetting all former iniuries) had receiued that naughtie ''Plexirtus'' into a '''streight''' degree of fauour {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Difficult; distressful.
#* '''18th c.''', {{w|Thomas Secker}}, ''Sermons on Several Subjects'', 2nd edition, 1771, Volume III, Sermon XI, p. 253,<sup>[https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/007692365]</sup>
#*: But to make your '''strait''' Circumstances yet '''straiter''', for the Sake of idle Gratifications, and distress yourselves in Necessaries, only to indulge in Trifles and Vanities, delicate Food, shewish Dress, ensnaring Diversions, is every Way wrong.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[parsimonious|Parsimonious]]; [[stingy]]; [[mean]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King John|act=V|scene=vii|passage={{...}} I do not ask you much, / I beg cold comfort; and you are so '''strait''' / And so ingrateful, you deny me that.}}
# {{obs sp|en|straight}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Record Commission|title={{w|Statutes of the Realm|The Statutes of the Realm. Printed by Command of His Majesty King George the Third in pursuance of an Address of the House of Commons of Great Britain. From Original Records and Manuscripts.}}|year=1810|volume=1|page=lxiii|text=''A '''strait''' Line over a Vowel denotes the Omission of the Letter'' m ''or'' n ''following:''<br>quā{{spaces|5}}-{{spaces|5}}-{{spaces|5}}quam{{spaces|5}}‖{{spaces|5}}nō{{spaces|5}}-{{spaces|5}}-{{spaces|5}}non{{spaces|5}}‖{{spaces|4}}{{...}}<br>{{...}}<br>''The '''strait''' Line over'' m ''in the Middle of a Word denotes the Omission of the Letter'' n ''following:''<br>om̄es{{spaces|5}}-{{spaces|5}}-{{spaces|5}}omnes{{spaces|5}}‖{{spaces|5}}om̄ia{{spaces|5}}-{{spaces|5}}-{{spaces|5}}omnia}}

====Usage notes====
The adjective is often confused with {{m|en|straight}}.

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|strait-handed}}
* {{l|en|straitjacket}}
* {{l|en|strait-laced}}

====Translations====
{{trans-top|tight, close, confined}}
* Bulgarian: {{t+|bg|тесен}}, {{t+|bg|ограничен}}
* Chinese:
*: Mandarin: {{t+|cmn|紧}}
* Gothic: {{t|got|𐌰𐌲𐌲𐍅𐌿𐍃}}
* Macedonian: {{t|mk|те́сен}}
* Maori: {{t|mi|kikī}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}
[[Image:Strait.svg|thumb|right|150 px|Sketch of a strait connecting two larger bodies of water.]]

# {{lb|en|geography}} A narrow [[channel]] of water connecting two larger [[body of water|bodies of water]].
#: {{ux|en|the '''Strait''' of Gibraltar}}
#* {{RQ:Defoe Captain Singleton|pages=232–233|pageref=233|passage={{...}} we steered directly through a large Out-let, which they call a '''Streight''', tho’ it be fifteen Miles broad {{...}}}}
# A narrow pass, passage or street.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=2|canto=7|page=282|passage=He brought him through a darksom narrow  '''strayt''', / To a broad gate all built of beaten gold:}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Troilus and Cressida|act=III|scene=iii|passage=For honour travels in a '''strait''' so narrow, / Where one but goes abreast:}}
# A [[neck]] of land; an [[isthmus]].
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Poems 1842|title=Morte d’Arthur|volume=II|page=4|passage=The bold Sir Bedivere uplifted him, / Sir Bedivere, the last of all his knights, / And bore him to a chapel nigh the field, / A broken chancel with a broken cross, / That stood on a dark '''strait''' of barren land.}}
# {{lb|en|often|in the plural}} A difficult position.
#: {{ux|en|to be in [[dire straits|dire '''straits''']]}}
#* '''1684''', {{w|Robert South}}, “A Sermon Preached at Westminster-Abbey” in ''Twelve Sermons Preached upon Several Occasions'', London: Thomas Bennett, 1692, p. 420,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A60954.0001.001]</sup>
#*: {{...}} let no man, who owns the Belief of a Providence, grow desperate or forlorn, under any Calamity or '''Strait''' whatsoever {{...}}
#* {{RQ:Homer Pope et al Odyssey|volume=III|page=19|passage=Plutarch is of opinion that this sleep of Ulysses was feigned; and that he made use of the pretence of natural infirmity to conceal the '''streights''' he was in at that time in his thoughts {{...}}}}

====Usage notes====
The geographical term ''straits'' used in the name of a location (as a [[proper noun]]) does not necessarily imply the existence of multiple straits; see for instance the [[Bering Straits]], the [[Straits of Gibraltar]], the [[Straits of Hormuz]], the [[Straits of Juan de Fuca]], the [[Straits of Magellan]], the [[Straits of Malacca]], the [[Sunda Straits]], the [[Taiwan Straits]], etc.

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|cross-strait}}
* {{l|en|dire straits}}
* {{l|en|Foveaux Strait}}
* {{l|en|Menai Strait}}

