===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete|transitive}} To [[confine]]; put to difficulties.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1577|author=w:Raphael Holinshed|title={{w|Holinshed's Chronicles|Holinshed’s Chronicles}}|location=London|volume=1|publisher=The Historie of Englande|page=3|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A03448.0001.001
|passage=After Bardus, the Celtes {{...}} were in short tyme, and with small labour broughte vnder the subiection of the Giaunt Albion, the sonne of Neptune, who altering the state of things here in this yland, '''straited''' the name of Celtica and the Celtes within the boundes of Gallia {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Winter's Tale|act=IV|scene=iv|passage={{...}} If your lass / Interpretation should abuse and call this / Your lack of love or bounty, you were '''straited''' / For a reply {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1658|author=William Sanderson|title=A Compleat History of the Life and Raigne of {{w|Charles I of England|King Charles}}|location=London|publisher=Humphrey Moseley, et al|page=885|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A62144.0001.001
|passage=The King, Duke of ''York'', Prince ''Rupert'' and ''Maurice'' are still at ''Oxford'' closely surrounded by the Parliaments Forces, and the other not well resolving what course to take, all their Horse being about ''Faringdon'', in expectation of the Lord ''Ashley'' with his Foot to joyn in a Body, if they be not prevented by Colonel ''Fleetwood'' and ''Rainsborough'', '''straiting''' and allarming Oxford very often {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|transitive}} To [[tighten]].

