Page 1587 Tornado Alley: -------- begin text --------
==English==
{{wikipedia}}
[[File:Tornado Alley Diagram.svg|thumb|upright=1.5|Tornado Alley is featured by the pink-colored area.]]
{{wikidata|Q923442}}
{{commonscat}}
{{rfc|en}}

===Etymology===
{{m|en|tornado}}+{{m|en|alley}}. Coined in 1952 by U.S. Air Force meteorologists Ernest J. Fawbush and Robert C. Miller. From originally being defined as a long north-to-south strip (akin to an alleyway) of the Great Plains with a large amount of tornadic activity.

===Pronunciation===
{{IPA|en|/ˌtɔː(ɹ)ˈneɪdəʊ ˈælɪ/}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun|head=[[tornado|Tornado]] [[alley|Alley]]}}

# {{senseid|en|923442}}{{lb|en|US|meteorology}} {{place|en|region|c/USA}}. Area located in the middle of the USA, where tornadoes are particularly frequent and intense.

====Usage notes====
Depending on the authority, Tornado Alley may extend into Canada, into the U.S. Midwest, and reach the Gulf of Mexico coast. For those versions that reach the Gulf coast, {{m|en|Dixie Alley}} is a subregion of Tornado Alley, instead of a separate region of tornadic activity. Similarly for those that reach the Midwest and Great Lakes, the {{m|en|Hoosier Alley}} is a subregion, instead of a separate region.

====Derived terms====
{{col3|en|
|Dixie Alley
|South Alley
|Carolina Alley
|Hoosier Alley
}}

===See also===
{{col3|en|
|Hailstorm Alley
|Hurricane Alley
|Typhoon Alley
}}

