Page 1599 twilight zone: -------- begin text --------
==English==
{{wp|terminator (solar)|mul=Twilight Zone}}

===Etymology 1===
Because such a zone (in the literal sense of the term) is {{l|en|twilit}}; see {{m|en|twilight#Etymology|twilight § Etymology}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|astronomy|astrophysics}} A moving {{l|en|line#Noun}} that divides the {{l|en|daylit}} side and the dark {{l|en|night}} side of a {{l|en|planetary body}}.
#: {{syn|en|terminator}}
#: {{cot|en|greyline}}
# {{lb|en|by extenstion}} A [[region]] or [[context]] located [[in between]] others and therefore not [[subject]] to their [[norm]]s.
#: {{syn|en|shadowland}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1911|title=Case and Comment
|passage=As to this right, therefore, the people have expressly created a '''twilight zone''', in which neither nation nor state can act.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1978|author=Cornelius Cole Smith|title=Fort Huachuca: The Story of a Frontier Post|passage=They were interesting because they represented a sort of '''twilight zone''' separating the "old days" from modern times.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|author=Grant Gilmore|title=Security Interests in Personal Property|passage=...the classification scheme related to goods which lay in a "'''twilight zone'''" between consumer goods and equipment or between inventory and farm products.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=Charles C Marshall|title=The Roman Catholic Church in the Modern State|passage=But is there not a '''twilight zone''' over which both Church and State put forth claims?}}
# {{lb|en|geography}} A [[deteriorating]] area surrounding a [[central business district]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2012|author=George Sternlieb; James W. Hughes|title=Shopping Centers: U.S.A.|page=52
|passage=Geographically speaking, the '''twilight zone''' encompasses those areas that lie between the central business district and the first ring of suburban shopping centers, many of which are comparatively obsolete.}}

=====Translations=====
{{trans-top|region or context located in between others}}
* German: {{t+|de|Grauzone|f}}
{{trans-bottom}}

=====See also=====
* {{sense|physical}} {{l|en|crepuscular}}
* {{sense|figurative}} {{l|en|grey area}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To put or place into an [[indeterminate]] position; to be in an [[ambiguous]], undetermined, or improper context.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1989|author=Jack Kendall|title=Playing for Keeps|passage=Sometimes I think she's really a gym teacher '''twilight-zoned''' into the corridors of government. I'm always surprised to see that it's a gold, clipless Cross pen she's carrying and not a gym whistle.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause to [[daydream]] or [[zone out]]; to cause to lose attention to one's surroundings.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1997|author=James Patterson|title=Cat & Mouse|passage="I've been distant and into myself all night," I said. "The kids say I get '''twilight zoned'''."}}

===Etymology 2===
From the anthology television series ''[[w:The Twilight Zone|The Twilight Zone]]'', first aired in 1959, whose name was inspired by [[#Etymology_1|the astrophysical sense]].

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|figuratively}} A region in which [[surreal]], [[supernatural]], or [[fantastic]] events occur.
#* '''1996''' Christopher Lloyd, "Mixed Doubles," ''Frasier,'' Season 4, Episode 6 (originally aired 19 November 1996), spoken by Martin Crane (John Mahoney)
#*: Oh, I can't talk right now, Duke. I'm in the '''twilight zone'''.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998|author=Eric Chaisson|title=The Hubble wars: astrophysics meets astropolitics in the two-billion-dollar struggle over the Hubble Space Telescope|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=1zn3HK9REioC&pg=PA337&dq=%22entered+the+twilight+zone%22&hl=en&ei=MMIzTZyjJMiecNnfgckH&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=4&ved=0CDcQ6AEwAzgU#v=onepage&q=%22entered%20the%20twilight%20zone%22&f=false|page=337|publisher=Harvard University Press
|passage=Ground controllers were uncertain for some agonizing hours what had happened to the spacecraft [...] "We have entered the '''twilight zone'''," dead-panned one jaded engineer.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Steve Sisgold|title=What's Your Body Telling You?: Listening To Your Body's Signals to Stop Anxiety, Erase Self-Doubt and Achieve True Wellness|publisher=McGraw-Hill Professional|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=ij9Z9xgghjAC&pg=PA103&dq=%22entered+the+twilight+zone%22&hl=en&ei=O78zTevPD86ecf-okZ4H&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=3&ved=0CDUQ6AEwAg#v=onepage&q=%22entered%20the%20twilight%20zone%22&f=false|page=103
|passage=Suddenly, to her astonishment and mine, it turned black and blue! I wondered if we'd just entered the '''twilight zone'''.}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To experience or perceive something bizarre or fantastic.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2016|author=Bill Roddey|title=[http://blog.pennlive.com/humor-comedy/2016/02/making_id_tvs_too_pretty_to_li.html Making ID TV's 'Too Pretty to Live' Crime Show Starring Me]
|passage=Then a gnome like white haired lady, bent like a human question mark, lurched toward me and asked if I was the mailman. [...] I was '''Twilight Zoning'''.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To behave or occur in a confusing or unexpected manner.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2011|author=David H.; [https://www.yelp.com/biz/beddington-registry-services-calgary?hrid=T1oXvXil8cNelwkjJ7Lj7g Beddington Registry Service review]|title=Yelp
|passage=The service is likely from a bad twilight zone episode. [...] I did get what I required but I had to ask lots of questions as the communication was '''Twilight Zoning''' often.}}
-------- end text --------
Page 1600 ubac: -------- begin text --------
==English==

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{der|en|oc|ubac}}, from {{der|en|la|opācus}}. {{doublet|en|opaque}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈuː.bæk/|/ˈjuː.bæk/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈu.bæk/|/ˈju.bæk/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|geography}} The [[shady]] side of a [[mountain]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Japanese Journal of Geology and Geography: Transactions, Titles and Abstracts|location=Tokyo|publisher=Nihon Gakujutsu Kaigi &#91;{{w|Science Council of Japan}}&#93;|year=1942|page=34|issn=0021-5058|oclc=748645064|passage=The distribution of barley is generally on the adret slope, the ubac seeming to be disregarded. A sunny slope is the most important natural factor in the cultivation of barley and wheat. Very rarely they are cultivated in '''ubac''' fields, but close investigation showed them to be of secondary adret nature. In this case seeds are sown a month earlier and mown ten days later than on the adret slope.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=John J. Baxevanis|chapter=The Wines of Savoie|title=The Wines of Champagne, Burgundy, Eastern and Southern France|location=Totowa, N.J.|publisher=w:Rowman & Littlefield|year=1987|pages=70–71|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=EczgQ7PLGVkC&pg=PA71|isbn=978-0-8476-7534-0|passage=Viticulure in an alpine (Savoie) or sub-alpine environment (like the Jura) is a precarious activity {{...}} Exposure, by contrast, exaggerates or modifies the quantity of the altitudinal elements in terms of '''''ubac''''' (shady) versus ''adret'' (sunny) positions, wet and dry positions, or windy and protected positions, respectively. The advantages of ''adret'' slopes, which are usually drier and more protected, are evident and of paramount importance. ''Adret'' aspects receive much higher levels of direct sunlight, which produces higher soil and plant temperatures.}}

====Antonyms====
* {{l|en|adret}}

