Page 1613 watershed: -------- begin text --------
{{also|water-shed}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{com|en|water|shed}}, a {{calque|en|de|Wasserscheide|nocap=1}}, a compound of {{m|de|Wasser|t=water}} + {{m|de|scheiden|t=to divide}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈwɔːtəʃɛd/}}
* {{a|US}} {{enPR|wôʹtərshĕd}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈwɔtɚʃɛd/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-watershed.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{hyphenation|en|wa|ter|shed}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|hydrology|UK|Ireland|Australia|New Zealand}} The [[topographical]] [[boundary]] dividing two adjacent [[catchment basin]]s, such as a [[ridge]] or a [[crest]].
#: {{syn|en|water parting|drainage divide|water divide|divide}}
# {{lb|en|hydrology|US|Canada}} A region of land within which water flows down into a specified body, such as a river, lake, sea, or ocean; a [[drainage basin]].
#: {{syn|en|catchment basin|catchment|catchment area|drainage area|river basin|water basin}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} A [[critical]] [[point]] marking a change in course or development.
#: {{syn|en|turning point|crossroads}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|origyear=2007|author=w:David A. Weintraub|title=Is Pluto a Planet?: A Historical Journey through the Solar System|location=Princeton, NJ|publisher=w:Princeton University Press|isbn=9780691123486|page={{gbooks|dW1_AwAAQBAJ|115|watershed}}|passage=In the minds of many, 1847 was a '''watershed''' year during which Ceres, Pallas, Juno, Vesta, and Astraea were reclassified as asteroids.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2021 November 17|author=Anthony Lambert|title=How do we grow the leisure market?|journal=RAIL|issue=944|page=34|text=Coronavirus has been a '''watershed''' for the railways. It has accelerated the decline of season tickets and reduced business travel after years of steadily rising passenger numbers.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Cade Metz; Karen Weise|title=Microsoft Throws a Coming-Out Party for A.I.|work=The New York Times|date=2023-02-07|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/02/07/technology/microsoft-ai-chatgpt-bing.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=In a 2,000-word blog post published ahead of the press event, Brad Smith, Microsoft’s president, called this a “'''watershed''' year” and acknowledged the potential downsides, calling for “wide-ranging and deep conversations” on the issues.}}
# {{lb|en|Canada|British|television|radio}} The time after which material of more adult nature ([[violence]], [[swear word]]s, [[sex]]) may be [[broadcast]] on television or radio, either one laid down by law or one contrived from convention (e.g. when children are not watching)
#: {{syn|en|safe harbor|q1=US}}

====Derived terms====
