Page 1622 zone: -------- begin text --------
{{also|Zone|zoné|zône|zonë|zonę|żonę}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|la|zōna}}, from {{der|en|grc|ζώνη||[[girdle]], [[belt]]}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GenAm}} {{enPR|zōn}}, {{IPA|en|/zoʊn/}}
* {{a|RP}}, {{IPA|en|/zəʊn/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-zone.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|əʊn|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|geography|now|_|rare}} Each of the five [[regions]] of the earth's surface into which it was divided by [[climatic]] differences, namely the [[torrid zone]] (between the tropics), two [[temperate zone]]s (between the tropics and the polar circles), and two [[frigid zone]]s (within the polar circles).
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1567|translator=w:Arthur Golding|author=Ovid|title=Metamorphoses|section=I
|passage=And as two '''Zones''' doe cut the Heaven upon the righter side, / And other twaine upon the left likewise the same devide, / The middle in outragious heat exceeding all the rest: / Even so likewise through great foresight to God it seemed best, / The earth encluded in the same should so devided bee […].}}
#* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy|edition=2nd|part=I|section=2|member=4|subsection=vi|passage=To avoid which, we will take any pains&nbsp;{{...}}; we will dive to the bottom of the sea, to the bowels of the earth, five, six, seven, eight, nine hundred fathom deep, through all five '''zones''', and both extremes of heat and cold&nbsp;[…].}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1841|author=w:George Bancroft|title=History of the United States, from the Discovery of the American Continent|volume=2|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=OLUTAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA270&dq=%22defies+every+wind,+outrides+every+tempest,+and+invades+every+zone%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=1QGkU9KEHMnJkQXe34GwCg&ved=0CDYQ6AEwBA#v=onepage&q=%22defies%20every%20wind%2C%20outrides%20every%20tempest%2C%20and%20invades%20every%20zone%22&f=false|page=270
|passage=And while idle curiosity may take its walk in shady avenues by the ocean side, commerce{{...}}defies every wind, outrides every tempest, and invades every '''zone'''.}}
# Any given [[region]] or [[area]] of the world.
# A given [[area]] distinguished on the basis of a particular characteristic, use, restriction, etc.
#: ''There is a no-smoking '''zone''' that extends 25 feet outside of each entrance.''
#: ''The white '''zone''' is for loading and unloading only.''
#: ''Files in the Internet '''zone''' are blocked by default, as a security measure.''
# A band or area of growth encircling anything.
#: ''a '''zone''' of evergreens on a mountain; the '''zone''' of animal or vegetable life in the ocean around an island or a continent''
#* {{RQ:Melville Omoo
|passage=Some of the lagoons, said to have subterranean outlets, have no visible ones; the inclosing island, in such cases, being a complete '''zone''' of emerald.}}
# A [[band]] or [[stripe]] extending around a body.
# {{lb|en|crystallography}} A series of [[plane]]s having [[mutually]] [[parallel]] [[intersection]]s.
# {{lb|en|baseball|informal}} The [[strike zone]].
#: ''That pitch was low and away, just outside of the '''zone'''.''
# {{lb|en|ice hockey}} Every of the three parts of an [[ice rink]], divided by two [[blue line]]s.
#: {{ux|en|Players are off side, if they enter the attacking '''zone''' before the puck.}}
# {{lb|en|handball}} A [[semicircular]] area in front of each [[goal]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1974|author=Franko Blazic|coauthors=Zorko Soric|title=Team Handball|page=31|publisher=|location=|url=https://books.google.cz/books?hl=cs&id=k3NYAAAAYAAJ&dq=%22the+zone%22|isbn=|passage=The defender playing at the top of the zone is nine to fourteen metres out from the goal line.}}
# {{lb|en|chiefly|sports}} A high-performance [[phase]] or [[period]].
#: ''I just got in the '''zone''' late in the game: everything was going in.''
# {{lb|en|basketball|American football}} A [[defensive]] scheme where defenders guard a particular area of the court or field, as opposed to a particular opposing player.
# {{lb|en|networking}} That collection of a [[domain]]'s [[DNS]] [[resource records]], the [[domain]] and its [[subdomain|subdomains]], that are not [[delegate|delegated]] to another authority.
# {{lb|en|networking|dated}} A logical group of network [[device]]s on {{w|AppleTalk}} (an obsolete networking protocol).
# {{lb|en|now|_|literary}} A [[belt]] or [[girdle]].
#* '''17th c''', [[w:John Dryden|John Dryden]], ''2005'', ''Pygmalion and the Statue'', Paul Hammond, David Hopkins (editors), ''The Poems of John Dryden: Volume Five: 1697-1700'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=6wFSAwAAQBAJ&pg=PA263&dq=%22An+embroidered+zone%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=_t-jU_PYKc_68QXh4YCYBw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22An%20embroidered%20zone%22&f=false page 263],
#*: Her tapered fingers too with rings are graced, / And an embroidered '''zone''' surrounds her slender waist.
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|II|lines=211-220|text={{...}} Or should she, confident, / As sitting queen adored on beauty's throne, / Descend with all her winning charms begirt / To enamour, as the '''zone''' of Venus once / Wrought that effect on Jove, so fables tell : / How would one look from his majestic brow, / Seated as on the top of virtue's hill, / Discountenance her despised, and put to rout / All her array; her female pride deject, / Or turn to reverent awe ? {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1779|author=[[w:Thomas Forrest (navigator)|Thomas Forrest]]|title=A Voyage to New Guinea and the Moluccas from Balambangan|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=Kzpzc_5DkAIC&pg=PA21&dq=%22An+embroidered+zone%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=_t-jU_PYKc_68QXh4YCYBw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22An%20embroidered%20zone%22&f=false|page=21
|passage=From the waiſt downwards, they wore a looſe robe, girt with an embroidered '''zone''' or belt about the middle, with a large claſp of gold, and a precious ſtone.}}
#* '''18th c''', [[w:William Collins (poet)|William Collins]], ''The Passions: An Ode for Music'', '''1810''', Alexander Chalmers, Samuel Johnson (editors), ''The Works of the English Poets, from Chaucer to Cowper'', Volume 13, [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=vnEJAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA204&dq=%22Loose+were+her+tresses+seen,+her+zone%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=kfijU7n0Boy48gWHzIDIBA&ved=0CGMQ6AEwDA#v=onepage&q=%22Loose%20were%20her%20tresses%20seen%2C%20her%20zone%22&f=false page 204],
#*: Love fram'd with Mirth a gay fantastic round, / Loose were her tresses seen, her '''zone''' unbound,
#* '''1819''', Lord Byron, ''Don Juan'', Canto I, LV, '''1827''', ''The Works of Lord Byron, including The Suppressed Poems'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=fD30P3gsPboC&pg=PA565&dq=%22Her+zone+to+Venus,+or+his+bow+to+Cupid%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=VwCkU-6VO8Pp8AX4_YDIBg&ved=0CCgQ6AEwAg#v=onepage&q=%22Her%20zone%20to%20Venus%2C%20or%20his%20bow%20to%20Cupid%22&f=false page 565],
#*: There was the Donna Julia, whom to call / Pretty were but to give a feeble notion / Of many charms in her as natural / As sweetness to the flower, or salt to ocean, / Her '''zone''' to Venus, or his bow to Cupid / (But this last simile is trite and stupid).
#* '''1844''', {{w|Charles Dickens}}, ''[[w:Martin Chuzzlewit|The life and Adventures of Martin Chuzzlewit]]'', '''1865''', ''Works of Charles Dickens'', Volume VI: ''Martin Chuzzlewit''—Volume II, [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=cetLAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA421&dq=%22it+was+the+prettiest+thing+to+see+her+girding+on+the+precious+little+zone%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=Yv2jU4XOONTd8AWztYHgCw&ved=0CB0Q6AEwAA#v=onepage&q=%22it%20was%20the%20prettiest%20thing%20to%20see%20her%20girding%20on%20the%20precious%20little%20zone%22&f=false page 421],
#*: {{...}}it was the prettiest thing to see her girding on the precious little '''zone''', and yet obliged to have assistance because her fingers were in such terrible perplexity;&nbsp;[…].
#* {{RQ:Haggard She|passage=`Look now on me, Kallikrates!' and with a sudden motion she shook her gauzy covering from her, and stood forth in her low kirtle and her snaky '''zone''', in her glorious radiant beauty and her imperial grace, rising from her wrappings, as it were, like Venus from the wave, or Galatea from her marble, or a beatified spirit from the tomb.}}
# {{lb|en|geometry}} The curved surface of a [[frustum]] of a [[sphere]], the portion of surface of a sphere delimited by parallel planes.
#* '''1835''', Charles Davies, David Brewster (editors and translators), [[w:Adrien-Marie Legendre|Adrien-Marie Legendre]], ''Elements of Geometry and Trigonometry'', [1794, ''Eléments de géométrie''], [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=w5c-AAAAYAAJ&pg=PA292&dq=%22frustum%22%7C%22frustums%22%7C%22frusta%22+sphere+%22zone%22%7C%22zones%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=4sOiU4G0K4u7lQWWioHYBg&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22frustum%22%7C%22frustums%22%7C%22frusta%22%20sphere%20%22zone%22%7C%22zones%22&f=false page 293],
#*: To find the surface of a spherical '''zone'''.
#*: Rule.—''Multiply the altitude of the '''zone''' by the circumference of a great circle of the sphere, and the product will be the surface'' (Book VIII. Prop. X. Sch. 1).
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|author=John Bird|title=Engineering Mathematics|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=_BhgAwAAQBAJ&pg=PA183&dq=%22frustum%22%7C%22frustums%22%7C%22frusta%22+sphere+%22zone%22%7C%22zones%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=4sOiU4G0K4u7lQWWioHYBg&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22frustum%22%7C%22frustums%22%7C%22frusta%22%20sphere%20%22zone%22%7C%22zones%22&f=false|page=183
|passage=A '''zone of a sphere''' is the curved surface of a frustum.{{...}}Determine, correct to 3 significant figures (a) the volume of the frustum of the sphere, (b) the radius of the sphere and (c) the area of the '''zone''' formed.}}
# {{lb|en|geometry|loosely|perhaps by [[meronymy]]}} A [[frustum]] of a [[sphere]].
# A [[circuit]]; a [[circumference]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1667|author=John Milton|title=Paradise Lost|section=Book V, lines 558 to 560
|passage=And we have yet large day; for scarce the sun / Hath finish'd half his journey, and scarce begins / His other half in the great '''zone''' of heaven.}}

