Page 1716 target: -------- begin text --------
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|frm|targette}}, {{m|frm|targuete}}, diminutive of {{m|frm|targe||light shield}}, from {{der|en|fro|-}}, from {{der|en|frk|*targa||buckler}}, akin to {{cog|non|targa||small round shield}} (whence also {{cog|ang|targe}}, {{m|ang|targa||shield}}) from {{der|en|gem-pro|*targǭ||edge}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*derǵʰ-||fenced lot}}. Akin to {{cog|goh|zarga||side wall, rim}} (German {{m|de|Zarge||frame}}), {{cog|es|tarjeta||card}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈtɑɹɡɪt/|[ˈtʰɑɹɡɪt̚]}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-target.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈtɑːɡɪt/}}
* {{a|AU}} {{IPA|en|/ˈtaːɡɪt/}}
** {{audio|en|en-au-target.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɑː(ɹ)ɡɪt|s=2}}
[[Image:WA archery target with arrows.jpg|thumb|right|A target used for [[archery]].]]

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[butt]] or [[mark]] to [[shoot]] at, as for [[practice]], or to test the [[accuracy]] of a [[firearm]], or the [[force]] of a [[projectile]].
#: {{ux|en|Take careful aim at the '''target'''.}}
# A [[goal]] or [[objective]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-22|volume=407|issue=8841|page=70|magazine=w:The Economist
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/finance-and-economics/21579879-buy-out-firm-really-does-focus-operational-improvements-engineers Engineers of a different kind]
|passage=Private-equity nabobs bristle at being dubbed mere financiers.{{...}}Much of their pleading is public-relations bluster. Clever financial ploys are what have made billionaires of the industry’s veterans. “Operational improvement” in a portfolio company has often meant little more than promising colossal bonuses to sitting chief executives if they meet ambitious growth '''targets'''. That model is still prevalent today.}}
#: {{ux|en|They have a '''target''' to finish the project by November.}}
# An object of criticism or ridicule.
# A person, place, or thing that is frequently attacked, criticized, or ridiculed.
# A kind of shield:
## A kind of [[small]] [[shield]] or [[buckler]], used as a [[defensive]] [[weapon]] in [[war]].
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-1|II|IV|line=200|text=These four came all afront, and mainly thrust at me. I made me no more ado but took all their seven points in my '''target''', thus.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} A shield resembling the [[Roman]] [[scutum]], larger than the modern buckler.
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1786|author=Francis Grose|title=A Treatise on Ancient Armour and Weapons|page=22
|passage=The '''target''' or buckler was carried by the heavy armed foot, it answered to the scutum of the Romans; its form was sometimes that of a rectangular parallelogram, but more commonly had its bottom rounded off; it was generally convex, being curved in its breadth.}}
## {{lb|en|heraldry}} A [[bearing]] representing a buckler.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1762|author=Anton Friedrich Büsching|title=A New System of Geography: In which is Given, a General Account of the Situation and Limits, the Manners, History, and Constitution, of the Several Kingdoms and States of the Known World|page=12
|text=The fourth field is also party per pale, and for the dutchy of Genevois, contains chequered Or and azure: The sinister for the dutchy of Montserat, a '''target''', gules. The point Or is a black eagle of the county of Maurienne.}}
# {{lb|en|sports}} The [[pattern]] or [[arrangement]] of a series of [[hit]]s made by a [[marksman]] on a butt or mark.
#: {{ux|en|He made a good '''target'''.}}
# {{lb|en|surveying}} The sliding [[crosspiece]], or [[vane]], on a leveling [[staff]].
# {{lb|en|rail transport}} A [[conspicuous]] disk attached to a [[switch]] [[lever]] to show its [[position]], or for use as a [[signal]].
# {{lb|en|cricket}} the number of [[run]]s that the side [[batting]] last needs to [[score]] in the final [[inning]]s in order to [[win]]
# {{lb|en|linguistics}} The [[tenor]] of a [[metaphor]].
# {{lb|en|translation studies}} The translated version of a document, or the language into which translation occurs.
#: {{ux|en|Do you charge by source or '''target'''?}}
# A [[person]] (or group of people) that a person or organization is trying to [[employ]] or to have as a [[customer]], [[audience]] etc.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=September 2, 2011|author=Phil McNulty|work=BBC
|title=[http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/14770875.stm Bulgaria 0-3 England]
|passage=Gary Cahill, a '''target''' for Arsenal and Tottenham before the transfer window closed, put England ahead early on and Rooney was on target twice before the interval as the early hostility of the Bulgarian supporters was swiftly subdued.}}
# {{lb|en|UK|dated}} A thin cut; a [[slice]]; specifically, of [[lamb]], a piece consisting of the [[neck]] and [[breast]] [[joint]]s.
# {{lb|en|Scotland|obsolete}} A [[tassel]] or [[pendant]].
#: {{syn|en|targe}}
# {{lb|en|Scotland|obsolete}} A [[shred]]; a [[tatter]].

