[[Image:Japan satellite.jpg|thumb|240px|a satellite image showing '''Japan''']]

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|Giapan||obsolete}}
* {{alt|en|Japonia||obsolete}}
* {{alt|en|Japon||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
First attested in English as {{m|en|Giapan}} in Richard Willes's 1577 ''The History of Travayle in the West and East Indies'' (cited in {{w|Peter C. Mancall}}'s ''Travel Narratives from the Age of Discovery'', pp. 156–57), translating a 19 February 1565 letter of the Portuguese Jesuit missionary {{w|Luís Fróis}} as "Of the Ilande of Giapan".

{{bor+|en|pt|Japão}} with possible influence from {{bor|en|nl|Japan}}, both from either or both:
* {{der|en|ms|Jepun}}, from {{der|en|nan-hok|日本|tr=Ji̍t-pún}}, from {{der|en|ltc|日本||sun origin}}.
* {{cog|id|-}} {{der|en|ms|Jepang}}, from {{der|en|zhx-teo|日本|tr=Ji̍k-púng}}, from {{der|en|ltc|日本||sun origin}}.
With {{IPAchar|/j/}} readings, such as {{m|en|Iaponia}} / {{m|en|Japonia}} or {{m|en|Japon}} / {{m|en|Iapon}} from {{bor|en|yue|日本}}, also from {{der|en|ltc|日本||sun origin}}. 

Compare also modern {{cog|cmn|日本}}, {{cog|ja|-}} {{ja-r|日本|^にっぽ​ん}} / {{ja-r|日本|^にほ​ん}}, {{cog|ko|^일본}} ({{m|ko|日本}}), {{cog|vi|Nhật Bản}} ({{m|vi|日本}}).

The earliest form of ''Japan'' in Europe was {{w|Marco Polo}}'s {{m|it|Cipangu}}, from some form of synonymous {{der|en|zhx|-}} {{m|zh|日本國||nation of Japan}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/d͡ʒəˈpæn/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-Japan.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|æn|s=2}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{senseid|en|Q17}}{{place|en|country/and/archipelago|r/East Asia}}
#: {{syn|en|Jap.|Jpn.|Land of the Rising Sun|Japonia|Nihon|Nippon|Yamato|State of Japan}}
#* '''1889 Jan.''', {{w|Oscar Wilde}}, [http://www.ricorso.net/rx/library/authors/classic/Wilde_O/prose/Decay_L.htm The Decay of Lying: An Observation]", ''The Nineteenth Century'':
#*: ''Vivian:'' If you set a picture by {{w|Hokusai}}, or {{w|Hokkei}}, or any of the great native painters, beside a real Japanese gentleman or lady, you will see that there is not the slightest resemblance between them. The actual people who live in '''Japan''' are not unlike the general run of [[English]] people; that is to say, they are extremely commonplace, and have nothing curious or extraordinary about them. In fact the whole of '''Japan''' is a pure invention. There is no such country, there are no such people... if you desire to see a Japanese effect, you will not behave like a tourist and go to [[Tokyo|Tokio]]. On the contrary, you will stay at home and steep yourself in the work of certain {{w|Japanese art}}ists, and then, when you have absorbed the spirit of their style, and caught their imaginative manner of vision, you will go some afternoon and sit in the [[Hyde Park|Park]] or stroll down [[Piccadilly]], and if you cannot see an absolutely Japanese effect there, you will not see it anywhere.
#* '''1985 February''', {{w|Steve Jobs}}, [https://allaboutstevejobs.com/verbatim/interviews/playboy_1985 interview with David Sheff], ''{{w|Playboy (magazine)|Playboy}}'':
#*: '''Japan'''’s very interesting. Some people think it copies things. I don’t think that anymore. I think what they do is reinvent things. They will get something that’s already been invented and study it until they thoroughly understand it. In some cases, they understand it better than the original inventor... That strategy works only when what they’re working with isn’t changing very much—the [[stereo]] industry and the [[automobile]] industry are two examples. When the target is moving quickly, they find it very difficult...
#* '''2008 November 21''', Graham Linehan, ''{{w|The IT Crowd}}'', Season 3, Episode 1:
#*: ''Nolan:'' You do know '''Japan''' have expressed concern?<br>''Douglas:'' What, the whole country?<br>''Nolan:'' No, not the whole... Mr [[Yamamoto]].<br>''Douglas:'' He's important, isn't he?<br>''Nolan:'' He's the major [[shareholder]].

