{{wikipedia|Kuru (disease)}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|for|kúru|lit=trembling, shivering}}.<ref>{{cite-book |last=Scott |first=Graham |title=The Fore Language of Papua New Guinea |date=1978 |publisher=Pacific Linguistics |pages=2, 6 |hdl=1885/146489 |doi=10.15144/PL-B47 |isbn=978-0-85883-173-5 }}</ref><ref>{{cite-journal|last1=Zigas |first1=V. |last2=Gajdusek |first2=D. C. |title=Kuru: Clinical study of a new syndrome resembling ''paralysis agitans'' in natives of the Eastern Highlands of Australian New Guinea |journal=Medical Journal of Australia |date=23 November 1957 |volume=2 |issue=21 |pages=745-754 |doi=10.5694/j.1326-5377.1957.tb60287.x }}</ref> Perhaps eventually from Proto-[[wikipedia:Kainantu–Goroka languages|Gorokan]] *''kút''(V) (“dangling, shaking”) if cognate with the reduplicated element of {{cog|ygr|gúli gúli hu-||be loose, rattle}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|AU}} {{IPA|en|/ˈkʉː.ɹʉː/}}
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈkʊ.ɹuː/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʊɹu|q1=UK|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# A [[chronic]], [[progressive]], [[fatal]] central nervous system disease found mainly among the [[Fore]] and neighboring peoples of [[New Guinea]], caused by a [[prion]] that probably resembles the [[scrapie]] agent of sheep, transmissible to nonhuman primates, and believed to be transmitted by ritual [[cannibalism]].
#: {{syn|en|laughing death|laughing sickness}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|author=w:Matt Ridley|title=Genome|publisher=Harper Perennial|year_published=2004|page=273
|passage=By the late 1950s, '''kuru''' was the leading cause of death among Fore women, and it had killed so many that men outnumbered women by three to one.}}

====Translations====
{{trans-top|central nervous system disease}}
* Chinese:
*: Mandarin: {{t|cmn|庫魯病|tr=kùlǔbìng}}
* Esperanto: {{t|eo|kuruo}}
* Finnish: {{t+|fi|kuru}}
* French: {{t+|fr|kuru|m}}
* German: {{t|de|Kuru|f}}
* Irish: {{t|ga|kuru|m}}
* Portuguese: {{t+|pt|kuru|m}}
* Russian: {{t+|ru|ку́ру|f}}
{{trans-bottom}}

