==German==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh|aber}}, {{m|gmh|aver}}, from {{inh|de|goh|aber}}, {{m|goh|abur}}, {{m|goh|aver}}, {{m|goh|avur}}, {{m|goh|afur}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*aferą||behind}}. Compare {{cog|bar|owa||but}}, {{cog|lb|awer||but}}, {{cog|stq|oaber||but}}, {{cog|gml|āver}}, {{cog|nds-de|aver|t=but}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˈaːbɐ/}} {{q|standard}}
* {{IPA|de|/ˈabɐ/}} {{q|colloquial; when unstressed by regular shortening, but also used when stressed}}
* {{audio|de|De-aber.ogg|Audio}}
* {{audio|de|De-at-aber.ogg|Audio (Austria)}}

===Conjunction===
{{head|de|conjunction|coordinating}}

# [[but]]; [[however]]; [[though]]
#: {{usex|de|inline=1|Ich mag keine Orangen, '''aber''' ich mag Äpfel.|I don't like oranges, '''but''' I like apples.}}

====Usage notes====
* Unlike most other conjunctions, ''aber'' doesn't need to be the first word of a clause and can thereby emphasize the preceding word(s): ''Ich bin dafür, er '''aber''' lehnt es ab.'' — “I’m in favour, '''but''' ''he'' has  rejected it.” In such a construction, ''aber'' can be considered an adverb, though the usual interpretation is that it is still a conjunction.
* After a negative, {{m|de|sondern}} is used to express a contrast, while ''aber'' expresses a gradation or nuance. Compare:
:: {{uxi|de|Wir haben keine Pizza gekauft, '''sondern''' Zutaten, um eine selbst zu machen.|We didn't buy a pizza '''but''' ingredients to make one ourselves.}}
:: {{uxi|de|Wir haben die Pizza nicht selbst gemacht, '''aber''' haben auch Zutaten gekauft, um sie zu verbessern.|We didn't make the pizza ourselves '''but''' also bought ingredients to improve it.}}
* It's usually better or less dated English to not translate ''sondern'' with ''but'':
:: {{uxi|de|Er ist nicht genial, '''sondern''' dumm.|He isn’t brilliant; '''in fact''' he's stupid. / He isn’t brilliant, he's stupid.}}
:: {{uxi|de|Er ist nicht genial, '''aber''' ziemlich klug.|He isn’t brilliant '''but''' quite intelligent.}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|de|aber hallo}}
* {{l|de|aber, aber}}

===Adverb===
{{de-adv}}

# {{lb|de|obsolete|except in compounds}} [[again]]
# {{lb|de|qualifier}} [[rather]]; [[quite]]; [[unusually]]; {{ngd|used with adjectives to express a surprising degree, whether this surprise be real or for effect}}
#: {{usex|de|inline=1|Das ist '''aber''' teuer.|That's '''rather''' expensive. ≈ That's more expensive than I would’ve thought.}}
