===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|coumbe}}, {{m|enm|cumbe}}, from {{inh|en|ang|cumb}}, from {{der|en|cel-bry-pro|-}} (compare {{cog|cy|cwm}}), from {{der|en|cel-pro|*kumbā}}. {{doublet|en|cwm}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{enPR|ko͞om}}, {{IPA|en|/kuːm/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-cwm.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{homophones|en|coom|cwm}}
* {{rhymes|en|uːm}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[valley]], often [[wooded]] and often with no [[river]]
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1914|author=w:Saki; ‘The Cobweb’|title=Beasts and Superbeasts
|passage=its long, latticed window [...] looked out on a wild spreading view of hill and heather and wooded '''combe'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Southey Madoc
|passage=gradual rise the shelving '''combe''' displayed.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1950|month=April|author=Timothy H. Cobb|title=The Kenya-Uganda Railway|journal=Railway Magazine|pages=264-265|text=You wake up next morning on what looks like Salisbury Plain, only here you climb up the side of every '''combe''', round the end and out the other side.}}
# A [[cirque]].

====Usage notes====
Used, especially in South West England, in many [[placename]]s, e.g. {{m|en|Compton}}, {{m|en|Wycombe}}.

