===Etymology 1===

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsəʊfi/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-Sophie.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{rhymes|en|əʊfi|s=2}}

====Proper noun====
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{given name|en|female|from=fr:Sophie|eq=Sophia}} of Ancient Greek origin. {{cln|en|female given names from Ancient Greek}}
#* '''1832''' [[w:Bryan Procter|Barry Cornwall]], ''English Songs'', 1851, LXXXV ("To Sophie"):
#*: Wilt thou be a nun, '''Sophie'''? / Nothing but a nun? / Is it not a better thing / With thy friends to laugh and sing?
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1991|author=w:Julian Barnes|title=Talking It Over|pages=241, 252|isbn=0-224-03157-0
|passage=No, like ''a'' small child, my daughter, '''Sophie''' Anne Louise. We gave her three names, all of which exist in English as well as in French, so she can change her name just by changing her accent. - - -<br>'''Sophie''' Anne Louise. It is a bit pretentious, do you not find? Maybe it is better in English. '''Sophie''' Anne Louise. No, it still sounds like one of Queen Victoria's grandchildren.}}
# {{given name|en|female|diminutive=Sophia}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|author=Marilyn Seguin|title=The Bell Keeper: The story of Sophia and the Massacre of the Indians at Gnadenhutten, Ohio, in 1782|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=Ztax4WEsnT4C&pg=PT9&dq=sophie+sophia+-intitle:%22%22&hl=en&ei=NNSbTsKqHoOpiAfgz_WUBw&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=7&ved=0CEQQ6AEwBjgU#v=onepage&q=sophie%20sophia%20-intitle%3A%22%22&f=false|page=8
|passage=Sophia landed on her behind on the soft moss that lined the river bank. "Besides, you don't win yet, '''Sophie'''," he said. "I have one more stone still."}}
# {{place|en|village|department/Ouest|c/Haiti}}.

=====Derived terms=====
* [[Sophie's choice]]

===Etymology 2===
See {{l|en|Sophy|id=etym-shah}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|shah}} {{archaic spelling of|en|Safavi|id=shah|nodot=1}}<ref name="OEDonline_Sophy1"/>
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|II|i|page=167|columns=1–2|text={{smallcaps|The Prince of Morocco}}:<br>{{...}}By this Symitare,<br>That ſlew the '''Sophie''', and a Perſian Prince{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1980|year_published=2007|author=w:Roger Savory|title=Iran under the Safavids|location=Cambridge [u.a.]|publisher=Cambridge University Press|isbn=9780521224833|page=259|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=v4Yr4foWFFgC&pg=PA259|passage=The Safavid shahs were commonly termed by Western writers "'''Sophie'''", "Sophy", "Sophi" or "Soffi". All these terms were probably corruptions of Ṣafī, the name of the founder of the Safavid Order, rather than of Ṣūfī, as the Safavid supporters called themselves.}}
#* {{seemoreCites|en}}

