==English==
{{wikipedia|lang=en|polo (disambiguation)|polo}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GenAm}} {{IPA|en|/ˈpoʊloʊ/}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈpəʊ.ləʊ/|/ˈpəʊl.əʊ/|[ˈpəʊləʊ]|[ˈpɒʊɫəʊ]}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-polo.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{rhymes|en|əʊləʊ|s=2}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{der|en|bft|پولو|tr=polo||ball}}. Cognate with {{cog|bo|པོ་ལོ}}, {{m|bo|ཕོ་ལོང}}, {{m|bo|སྤོ་ལོ||ball}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} A ball game where two teams of players on [[horseback]] use long-handled [[mallet]]s to propel the ball along the ground and into their opponent's goal.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2019|author=w:Namwali Serpell|title=w:The Old Drift|publisher=Hogarth|page=227|passage=There were '''polo''' fields – sometimes green, sometimes brown – where in the old days, people had actually played that strange game that seems like a drunken bet about golf and horse riding.}}
# The game of [[ice polo]], one of the ancestors of [[ice hockey]]; a similar game played on the [[ice]], or on a prepared floor, by players wearing [[skate]]s.<!--Webster 1913-->
# {{lb|en|countable}} A [[polo shirt]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2007 February 22|author=Mike Albo|title=Outfitters to Presidents, Preppies, Me|journal=New York Times|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2007/02/22/fashion/22CRITIC.html
|passage=Then on the second floor there is the creepy boy’s section, which had little headless mannequins in premium '''polos''' ($39.50), rugby shirts ($49.50) and a precocious leather pilot jacket for $148.}}

