{{head|es|verb form}}

# {{apocopic form of|es|vale}}: is [[worth]]
#: {{ux|es|mi casa y mi hogar cien doblas '''val'''. (''val'' rhymes with ''hogar'', [[Rhymes:Spanish|assonant rhyme]])|[[there's no place like home]].}}

=====Usage notes=====
* In [[Old Spanish]], after the consonants /d/, /n/, /l/, /ʎ/, /ɾ/ and /θ/, a final /e/ was regularly elided, as in {{m|es|pid}}, {{m|es|vien}}, {{m|es|val}}, {{m|es|quier}}, {{m|es|faz}}, versus the modern forms of {{m|es|pide}}, {{m|es|viene}}, {{m|es|vale}}, {{m|es|quiere}}, and {{m|es|hace}}, with ''-e'' restored by analogy (compare modern Portuguese, which still has apocope in words such as {{m|pt|vem||he/she comes}}, {{m|pt|quer||he/she wants}}, {{m|pt|faz||he/she does}}). In modern Spanish, a few [[apocope]]s following [[coronal]] consonants are still preserved: {{m|es|buen}}, {{m|es|gran}}, {{m|es|san}}, derived from {{m|es|bueno}}, {{m|es|grande}}, and {{m|es|santo}}.

