Page 2099 Nehrung: -------- begin text --------
==German==

===Etymology===
From [[East Prussian]] {{inh|de|gmh|nerge|t=spit}}, related with the adjective {{der|de|gem-pro|*narwaz}}, whence {{cog|en|narrow}}. If {{m|gmh||nerge}} is an early contraction, the word may have been derived with the Germanic suffix {{m|gem-pro|*-ingaz}} commonly used in placenames (compare {{m+|de|-ingen}}). If, however, the form {{m|de||Nehrung}} (16th c.) is a later alteration of {{m|gmh||nerge}}, it could be an old [[ablaut]] form or derived with the Germanic suffix {{m|gem-pro|*-į̄}} (then orginally meaning “narrowness”).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˈneːʁʊŋ/}}
* {{audio|de|De-Nehrung.ogg|Audio}}
* {{audio|de|De-at-Nehrung.ogg|Audio (Austria)}}
* {{hyph|de|Neh|rung}}

===Noun===
{{de-noun|f}}

# {{lb|de|geography}} [[spit]] {{gloss|long, very narrow peninsula, especially in the Baltic Sea}}
#: {{uxi|de|Frische '''Nehrung'''|Vistula spit}}
#: {{uxi|de|Kurische '''Nehrung'''|Curonian spit}}

====Declension====
{{de-ndecl|f}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|de|Narbe}}

===See also===
* {{l|de|Haff}}

===Further reading===
* {{pedia|lang=de}}
