Page 2176 φλοιός: -------- begin text --------
==Ancient Greek==

===Etymology===
Formally, it belongs to {{m|grc|φλέω||to abound}} together with {{m|grc|φλόος||skin, slough}}. Semantically, the connection with {{m|grc|φλέω}} is clear in {{m|grc|ὑπέρφλοιος||luxuriant, succulent}}. Frisk thinks that the rind may have received its name from its being rich in flesh and juice, as opposed to the wood underneath. However, it could also be from the same {{der|grc|qsb-grc|-}} of {{m|grc|φλέως||Ravenna grass}}, which is suggested for {{m|grc|φλέω}} too.

===Pronunciation===
{{grc-IPA}}

===Noun===
{{grc-noun|φλοιοῦ|m|second}}

# {{lb|grc|botany}} [[bark]] of [[tree]]s, especially [[smooth]] [[bark]]
# [[husk]] or [[skin]] of certain [[fruit]]s
# [[membrane]] enclosing the [[egg]]s of certain [[animal]]s
# [[tissue]] from which [[spider]]s spin their [[web]]s
# {{lb|grc|figuratively}} of [[superficial]] or [[useless]] [[covering]]s, [[redundancy]]

====Declension====
{{grc-decl|φλοιός|οῦ}}

====Derived terms====
{{der4|grc|τανύφλοιος|φλοΐζομαι|φλόϊνος|φλοιοβαρής|φλοιορραγής|φλοιόρριζος|φλοιόω|φλοιώδης}}

===Further reading===
* {{R:LSJ}}
* {{R:Middle Liddell}}
* {{R:Bailly}}
* {{R:Slater}}
* {{R:Woodhouse}}
* {{R:grc:Beekes}}

