Page 2394 cathair: -------- begin text --------
{{also|cathaír}}
==English==

===Etymology 1===
{{compound|en|cat|hair}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|cathair|+|pl2qual=when referring to individual strands of hair}}

# The [[hair]] of a [[cat]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Benedict Kiely|title=Dogs Enjoy the Morning|publisher=w:Penguin Books|year=1968|year_published=1971|page=18|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/dogsenjoymorning0000kiel_v0w7/page/18/mode/1up|passage=A pimpled chin, dark with '''cathairs''', a mouthful of irregular teeth, were visible below helmet and goggles.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1993 |title=Effective Telemarketing: How to Sell Over the Telephone |author=Allen Warfield; Al Brooks |page=111 |ISBN=|passage=How can you tell a cat owner? all the little claw marks on their back. . .Or by the '''cathair''' that sticks to their suit. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1993|title=The Cat Who Wasn't There|author=w:Lilian Jackson Braun|page= |ISBN=|passage=The conscientious Mrs. Fulgrove was driving away as he pulled into the barnyard, and he waved to her; the woman's scowl indicated that she had worked overtime because of the vast amount of '''cathair''' everywhere.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Lilian Jackson Braun|title=[[w:The Cat Who...|The Cat Who]] Blew the Whistle|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:G. P. Putnam's Sons|G. P. Putnam’s Sons]]|year=1994|page=81|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/catwhoblewwhist000brau/page/81/mode/1up|isbn=0-399-13981-8|passage=The closed-door policy, he liked to explain, kept the cats out of his hair and the '''cathairs''' out of his typewriter.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2000|title=Tamaqua: Volume Seven Issue One |author=|page=75 |ISBN=|passage=Meditate on the steady drone and the rocking of the back and forth vacuum dance you do as you suck up the '''cathair''', the ashes, the seeds, the stray leaves.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2000|title=Nimrod International Journal - Volume 44 |author=|page=128 |ISBN=|passage=Cat likes to brush against it and sun on the deck chair, the cushion is a mat of gray '''cathair'''. }}

===Etymology 2===
From {{bor|en|ga|cathair}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# An ancient [[Irish]] fortification of [[stone]] or [[earthwork]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:William Frederick Wakeman|William F[rederick] Wakeman]]|chapter=Raths or Duns|title=Archæologia Hibernica. A Hand-book of Irish Antiquities, Pagan and Christian: Especially of Such as Are Easy of Access from the Irish Metropolis.|location=Dublin|publisher=James M{{sup|c}}Glashan,{{nb...|21 D’Olier-Street}}. William S. Orr & Co.{{...|147 Strand,}}London|year=1848|section=part I (Pagan Antiquities)|page=47|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/archologiahiber00wakegoog/page/n72/mode/1up|passage=Several '''cathairs''' which we have examined are not circular in plan, but appear to have been formed to suit the contour of the eminence upon which they stand; and others are of an oval form.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|chapter=The Dúns of Aran|title=A World of Stone: Life, Folklore and Legends of the Aran Islands|publisher=O’Brien Educational|year=1977|year_published=1980|page=28|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/worldofstone0000osul/page/28/mode/1up|isbn=0 905140 12 5|passage=There were probably other '''cathairs''' and duns on the Aran Islands which were not as sturdily built or not as well preserved as those that survived.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Theresa McDonald|title=Achill: 5000 B.C. to 1900 A.D.: Archaeology, History, Folklore|year=1992|page=128|isbn=0 9519974 0 8|passage=‘On this island there are three cyclopean '''cathairs''' but their stones have been nearly all removed to build the modern little houses which are nearly in as rude a style as the Cahirs ever were.{{nb...}}’}}

===Anagrams===
* {{anagrams|en|a=aachirt|Cathari|Chaitra|chirata|cithara}}

[[Category:en:Hair]]

