===Etymology 2===
Inherited from the {{inh|en|ang|port}}, from the {{der|en|la|porta||passage, gate}}, reinforced by the {{der|en|fro|porte}}. {{doublet|en|porta}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|now|_|Scotland|historical}} An [[entryway]] or [[gate]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1485|author=w:Thomas Malory|title=Le Morte Darthur|volume_plain=book X|passage=And whan he cam to the '''porte''' of the pavelon, Sir Palomydes seyde an hyghe, ‘Where art thou, Sir Trystram de Lyones?’}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1590|author=w:Edmund Spenser|title=The Faerie Queene|section=III.1|passage=Long were it to describe the goodly frame, / And stately '''port''' of Castle Joyeous {{...}}.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=Him I accuse / The city '''ports''' by this hath enter'd|author=[[w:William Shakespeare|Shakespeare]]|title=[[w:Coriolanus (play)|Coriolanus]]|year=1623|section=V.vi}}
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=And from their ivory '''port''' the Cherubim, / Forth issuing at the accustomed hour|author=[[w:John Milton|Milton]]|title=w:Paradise Lost|year=1667|volume_plain=book IV}}
# An opening or doorway in the side of a [[ship]], especially for boarding or loading; an [[embrasure]] through which a cannon may be discharged; a [[porthole]].
#* {{quote-book|en|passage={{...}} her '''ports''' being within sixteen inches of the water {{...}}|author=[[w:Walter Raleigh|Sir W. Raleigh]]|year=c. 1615|title=A Discourse of the Invention of Ships, Anchors, Compass{{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|medicine}} A small medical appliance installed beneath the skin, connected to a vein by a catheter, and used to inject drugs or to draw blood samples.
# {{lb|en|curling|bowls}} A space between two [[stone]]s wide enough for a delivered stone or bowl to pass through.
# An [[opening]] where a [[connection]] (such as a pipe) is made.
# {{lb|en|computing}} A logical or physical construct in and from which data are transferred. {{pedia|Computer port (hardware)}}
# {{lb|en|computing}} A female connector of an electronic device, into which a cable's male connector can be inserted.

