Page 2472 tor: -------- begin text --------
{{also|Appendix:Variations of "tor"}}
==English==
{{was wotd|2013|February|19}}
{{wikipedia}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/tɔɹ/}}
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/tɔː(ɹ)/}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-tor.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɔɹ|ɔː(ɹ)|s=1}}
* {{homophones|en|tore|q1=in accents with the [[horse-hoarse merger]]|torr|q2=all accents|tour|q3=in accents with the [[w:pour-poor merger|pour-poor merger]]|taw|q4=in [[non-rhotic]] accents}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|tor}}, {{m|enm|torr-}}, from {{inh|en|ang|torr}}, {{m|ang|tor|t=a high rock, lofty hill, tower}}, possibly from {{der|en|cel-pro|-}}, compare {{der|en|owl|*tor|t=hill}}; ultimately from {{der|en|la|turris|t=tower}}, from {{der|en|grc|τύρρις}}, {{m|grc|τύρσις|t=tower}}, of non-Indo-European origin.

Cognate with {{cog|kw|tor}}, {{cog|gd|tòrr}}, {{cog|cy|tŵr}}, {{cog|ga|tor}}, {{cog|fr|tor}}, and {{cog|rm|tor}}/{{m|rm|tur}}/{{m|rm|tuor}}; the first four are from Proto-Celtic (from {{cog|la|turris}}), the last two directly from {{cog|la|turris}} (from {{cog|grc|τύρρις}} and {{m|grc|τύρσις}}). It is not clear whether the Celtic forms were borrowed from Old English or vice versa. {{doublet|en|tower}}.

[[File:Tor on Knocknagun, Wicklow, Ireland.jpg|thumb|right|150 px|'''Tor''' near the summit of Knocknagun, in Wicklow, Ireland]]

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|geology}} A [[craggy]] [[outcrop]] of [[rock]] on the [[summit]] of a [[hill]], created by the erosion and weathering of rock.
#* {{RQ:Kingsley Westward Ho|volume=I|page=183|text=Bursdon and Welsford were then, as now, a rolling range of dreary moors, unbroken by '''tor''' or tree, {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Doyle Hound of the Baskervilles|9|text=The moon was low upon the right, and the jagged pinnacle of a granite '''tor''' stood up against the lower curve of its silver disc.}}
# {{lb|en|South-West England}} A [[hill]] with such rock formation.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Lydia Joyce|title=Shadows of the Night|publisher=Signet Eclipse|isbn=9780451223425|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=BBqu0HYQRr4C&pg=PA242&dq=%22tors%22|page=242|text=She had slipped the letters into her pocket next to the packet of antique documents and had taken an umbrella—as the sky was ominous out over the distant '''tors'''—and strolled around the manor house and down the road toward the village.}}

=====Translations=====
{{trans-top|outcrop of rock}}
