Page 2509 fascia: -------- begin text --------
{{also|fàscia}}
==English==
{{wikipedia|Fascia (disambiguation)}}
[[File:BentoXVI-58-12052007.jpg|right|thumb|Pope Benedict XVI wearing a white '''fascia''', while a cardinal is seated near him (partially obscured from view) wearing a red '''fascia''' (sense 7).]]

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|fascia||a band, bandage, swathe}}. Related to {{m|la|fascēs||bundle of rods containing an axe with the blade projecting}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰask-||bundle, band}}. {{doublet|en|fajita|fess|fascism}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈfæʃə/|/ˈfæʃjə/|/ˈfæʃi.ə/}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈfeɪʃə/|/ˈfeɪʃjə/|/ˈfeɪʃi.ə/}} ''(especially sense 1)''
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-fascia.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-fascia2.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-fascia3.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{rhymes|en|æʃə}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|fasciae}}

# {{senseid|en|Q5436625}} {{lb|en|architectural element}} A wide [[band]] of material covering the ends of [[roof]] [[rafter|rafters]], sometimes supporting a [[gutter]] in [[steep]]-[[slope]] roofing, but typically it is a [[border]] or [[trim]] in low-[[slope]] roofing.
#: {{syn|en|frieze}}
# A [[face]] or front [[cover]] of an [[appliance]], especially of a [[mobile phone]].
#: {{syn|en|case}}
# {{senseid|en|Q1417134}} {{lb|en|UK}} A [[dashboard]].
#: {{syn|en|dashboard}}
# {{lb|en|architectural element}} A flat band or broad [[fillet]]; especially, one of the three bands that make up the [[architrave]], in the Ionic order.
# A broad well-defined band of [[color]].
# A band, [[sash]], or [[fillet]]; especially, in [[surgery]], a [[bandage]] or [[roller]].
# {{senseid|en|Q1078244}} {{lb|en|ecclesiastical|fashion}} A [[sash]] worn by certain members of the Catholic and Anglican churches.
#: {{syn|en|sash}}
# {{lb|en|anatomy}} The layer of loose [[tissue]], often containing [[fat]], immediately beneath the [[skin]]; the stronger layer of [[connective tissue]] covering and [[invest]]ing [[muscle]]s and [[organ]]s; an [[aponeurosis]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=Dennis E. McDonnell; Steven J. Harrison|chapter=10: Retropharyngeal Approach to the Occipitocervical Junction|editors=Richard G. Fessler; Laligam N. Sekhar|title=Atlas of Neurosurgical Techniques: Spine and Peripheral Nerves|publisher=w:Thieme Medical Publishers|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=t-a1qEhhEIYC&pg=PA92&dq=%22fascia%22%7C%22fascias%22%7C%22fasciae%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwilgPC4jab9AhXBm2oFHasQBdAQ6AF6BAg3EAI#v=onepage&q=%22fascia%22%7C%22fascias%22%7C%22fasciae%22&f=false|page=89
|passage=The deepest layer of cervical '''fascia''' consists of two main subdivisions: the alar and prevertebral '''fasciae'''.}}
#* '''2017''', David Lesondak, '''''Fascia''': What it is and Why it Matters'', Handspring Publishing, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=86JnEAAAQBAJ&pg=PA3&dq=%22fascia%22%7C%22fascias%22%7C%22fasciae%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwilgPC4jab9AhXBm2oFHasQBdAQ6AF6BAhCEAI#v=onepage&q=%22fascia%22%7C%22fascias%22%7C%22fasciae%22&f=false page 3],
#*: '''Figure 1.1'''<br>Close-up of the '''fascia''' surrounding a muscle in an unembalmed cadaver.
#* '''2017''', Andrea Pasini, Antonio Stecco, Carla Stecco, ''19: Fascial Anatomy of the Viscera'', Torsten Liem, Paolo Tozzi, Anthony Chila (editors), '''''Fascia''' in the Osteopathic Field'', Handspring Publishing, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=M6NnEAAAQBAJ&pg=PA173&dq=%22fascia%22%7C%22fascias%22%7C%22fasciae%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwilgPC4jab9AhXBm2oFHasQBdAQ6AF6BAhVEAI#v=onepage&q=%22fascia%22%7C%22fascias%22%7C%22fasciae%22&f=false page 173],
#*: This is evidence that the insertional '''fasciae''' are the ones that provide the connections between internal '''fasciae''' and muscular '''fascia''', and between the different organs. The same pattern can be applied to the '''fasciae''' that surround the glands.
# The signboard above a shop or other location open to the public.

====Usage notes====
The plural {{m|en|fascias}} is used for the first five definitions while {{m|en|fasciae}} is used for the sixth.

