==Japanese==

===Kanji===
{{ja-kanji|grade=c|rs=山08}}

====Readings====
{{ja-readings
|goon=げ-
|kanon=がい-
|kun=がけ-, まま-, ほき-
|nanori=きし-
}}

===Etymology 1===
{{ja-kanjitab|がい|yomi=kanon|alt=崕}}

From {{der|ja|ltc|sort=かい'|-}} {{ltc-l|崖|[[cliff]], [[precipice]]}}. Not attested in isolation. First attested in compound {{m|ja|崖岸|tr=gaigan||cliff shore, cliff bank}} in 900.<ref name="KDJ2-gaigan">{{R:Nihon Kokugo Daijiten 2|崖岸}}</ref>

====Pronunciation====
{{ja-pron|がい}}

====Affix====
{{ja-noun|がい}}

# {{defdate|from 900}} [[cliff]]

===Etymology 2===
{{ja-kanjitab|がけ|yomi=k|alt=厓}}

Shift from older pronunciation ''kake''. First attested in 1280.<ref name="KDJ2">{{R:Nihon Kokugo Daijiten 2}}</ref>

May be a shift in usage from compounding element ''kake'' as in the synonymous terms {{m|ja|懸路|tr=kakeji}}, {{m|ja|懸道|tr=kakemichi||cliff road, steep mountain road}},<ref name="KDJ2"/> where the ''kake'' comes from verb {{m|ja|かける|懸ける|tr=kakeru|to set into; to set across}}, from the way the road would be set into the cliff or mountain.

The change from ''kake'' to ''gake'' apparently happened some time during the {{w|Edo period}} (1603–1868). This term is recorded as ''Caqe'' (then-current Portuguese orthography for modern romanization ''kake'') in the 1603 ''{{w|Nippo Jisho}}'' Japanese-Portuguese dictionary.<ref name="N">{{R:Nippo Jisho original}}, available via Google Books [https://books.google.com/books?id=TFJAAQAAMAAJ&vq=caqa&pg=PA37#v=onepage&q=caqe&f=false here], right-hand column, 12th entry</ref>

====Pronunciation====
{{ja-pron|がけ|acc=0|acc_ref=DJR,SMK5,NHK}}

====Noun====
{{ja-noun|がけ}}

# {{defdate|from 1280}} [[steep]] [[bank]], [[cliff]]
#: {{syn|ja|切岸|tr=kirigishi|lit=cut bank}}

===Etymology 3===
{{ja-kanjitab|まま|yomi=k}}

From {{inh|ja|ojp|sort=まま|-}}. Further derivation unclear.

First attested in the ''{{w|Man'yōshū}}'' of 759.<ref name="KDJ2"/>

Modern references either omit this reading entirely or provide no pitch accent information for this reading, suggesting that it might be archaic or even obsolete in modern usage.<ref name="DJR"/><ref name="SMK5"/><ref name="NHK"/>

====Pronunciation====
{{ja-pron|まま}}

====Noun====
{{ja-noun|まま}}

# {{defdate|759–???}} {{lb|ja|sort=まま|archaic|possibly|_|obsolete}} [[steep]] [[slope]] or [[incline]]; [[collapsed]] part of a [[levee]] or [[embankment]]

===Etymology 4===
{{ja-kanjitab|yomi=k|ほき}}

Derivation unclear. First attested in the late 1100s.<ref name="KDJ2"/>

Modern references either omit this reading entirely or provide no pitch accent information for this reading, suggesting that it might be archaic or even obsolete in modern usage.<ref name="DJR"/><ref name="SMK5"/><ref name="NHK"/>

====Pronunciation====
{{ja-pron|ほき}}

====Noun====
{{ja-noun|ほき}}

# {{defdate|late 1100s–???}} {{lb|ja|sort=ほき|archaic|possibly|_|obsolete}} a [[steep]] area on a [[mountainside]], a [[cliff]]

===Etymology 5===
{{ja-kanjitab|きし|yomi=nanori}}

Probably from {{m|ja|岸|tr=kishi||[[shore]], [[bank]] (of a body of water)}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{ja-pron|きし}}

