==English==
{{wikipedia}}
{{wikipedia|48 Doris}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdɒɹɪs/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-Doris.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Back ache-Doris.wav|Audio (UK)}}

===Etymology 1===
The feminine form of {{m|en|Doric}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{sense|surname}} {{alter|en|Dorris}}

====Proper noun====
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{lb|en|Greek mythology}} The daughter of [[Oceanus]], who married [[Nereus]] and bore fifty sea-[[nymph]]s or [[nereid]]s.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=IV|canto=XI|stanza=49|passage=And snowy neckd '''Doris''', and milkewhite Galathæa.}}
# {{sid|en|Q1233764}}{{place|en|ancient region|r/Asia Minor|modern|c/Turkey|inhabited by the ancient [[Dorian]]s}}.
# {{sid|en|Q130987}}{{place|en|ancient mountainous region|r/Greece|the traditional homeland of the [[Dorian]]s}}.
# {{lb|en|astronomy}} {{w|48 Doris}}, a main belt [[asteroid]].
# {{given name|en|female|from=Ancient Greek}}, taken to regular use at the end of the 19th century.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1866|author=Mary A. Prescott|chapter=Doris Daylesford, A Story|title=Beadle's Monthly Magazine of To-day|volume=2|page=149
|passage="My '''Doris'''—may I call you that, dearest?"<br>"Call me Sappho, call me Chloris, call me Lalage, or '''Doris'''—only call me thine," I should have answered, if it had not been a little too sentimental.… I am afraid I omitted to state, in the proper place, that '''Doris''' is a name which has descended through a dozen generations of our family, that it belongs to myself as well as to my niece {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1989|author=Judy Carter|title=Stand-up Comedy: A Book|isbn=0440502438|page=35|passage=I've never met an old person named Judy. Now that's true. Maybe something happens to girls with young names like Debby, Judy, and Susie. At a certain age they make you change it to '''Doris''', Edna, or Myrtle.}}
# {{surname|en}}.

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|astronomy}} {{w|48 Doris}}, [[㊽]]

