Page 2845 Perusia: -------- begin text --------
{{also|Perúsia}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|Perusia}}. {{doublet|en|Perugia}}.

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{sid|en|Q1130478}}{{lb|en|historical}} {{place|en|An <<ancient>> [[Etruscan]] and later [[Roman]] <<city>> which eventually became present-day [[Perugia]], <<c/Italy>>}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|title=A Commentary on Livy, Books VI-X: Volume III, Book IX|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=dMISDAAAQBAJ&pg=PA455&dq=%22Perusia%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwiY4aH-zZb9AhWDdcAKHYEZBQwQ6AF6BAhcEAI#v=onepage&q=%22Perusia%22&f=false|page=455|publisher=w:Oxford University Press
|passage=That is, Fabius marched to relieve Sutrium, defeated the Etruscans there, crossed the Ciminian wood, defeated another Etruscan army near '''Perusia''', and then made ''[[indutiae]]'' with Arretium, Cortona, and '''Perusia'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Edward Bispham|title=From Asculum to Actium|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=aUYTDAAAQBAJ&pg=PA358&dq=%22Perusia%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwiY4aH-zZb9AhWDdcAKHYEZBQwQ6AF6BAhUEAI#v=onepage&q=%22Perusia%22&f=false|page=358|publisher=w:Oxford University Press
|passage='''Perusia''' has the epithet ‘Augusta’ in two inscriptions, and another refers to ‘'''Perusia''' Restituta’, which should most naturally restoration after the destruction of 40.<sup>126</sup> This implies that the city enjoyed Augustus' favour, as Saddington notes;<sup>127</sup> but that is no reason for largely rejecting the accounts of Imperator Caesar's punishment of '''Perusia''' in 40, excepting the fantasy of a massacre of the curial class.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Basil Dufallo|title=The Ghosts of the Past|publisher=w:Ohio State University Press|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=7a1ElTHqjBAC&pg=PT106&dq=%22Perusia%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwiY4aH-zZb9AhWDdcAKHYEZBQwQ6AF6BAhhEAI#v=onepage&q=%22Perusia%22&f=false|page=95
|passage=The siege of '''Perusia''' in 41 seems, additionally, to have cost him<sup>[Propertius]</sup> a relative, a figure mentioned in elegy 1.22 and often identified with the Gallus who speaks in 1.21.}}

