==Latin==
{{wikipedia|lang=la}}
[[Image:Monty python foot.png|thumb|pēs hūmānus (human foot)]]
[[Image:Shoeing in progress.jpg|thumb|pēs equī (foot of a horse)]]

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*ped-}}
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*pets}}, from {{inh|la|ine-pro|*pṓds}} (compare {{cog|sa|पद्|tr=pád}}, {{cog|grc|πούς}} and {{cog|ang|fōt}}, whence {{cog|en|foot}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|pēs}}
* {{audio|la|la-cls-pes.ogg|Audio (Classical)}}

===Noun===
{{la-noun|pēs/ped<3>|g=m}}

# a [[foot]], ''in its senses as''
## {{lb|la|anatomy}} a [[human]] [[foot]]
##: {{ux|la|… ne manus, nec '''pedes''', nec alia membra …|… not the hands, not the '''feet''', and not the other limbs …}}
##* {{Q|la|Ovid|Fasti|6|395-397|quote=Forte revertēbar fēstīs Vestālibus illa [...].<br/>hūc '''pede''' mātrōnam vīdī dēscendere nūdō.|trans=''It so happened that I was returning from the festival of Vesta [...]. Here I saw a matron coming down bare'''foot'''.''<br/>(Literally, in the ablative singular: “pede nūdō” or “with bare foot.” Roman matrons walked barefoot to honor {{w|Vesta (mythology)}} during the {{w|Vestalia}}.)}}
## {{lb|la|zoology}} [[any]] [[equivalent]] [[body part]] of an [[animal]], [[including]] [[hooves]], [[paws]], etc.
## {{lb|la|units of measure}} [[any]] of [[various]] [[unit]]s of [[length]] [[notionally]] [[based]] on the [[adult]] [[human]] [[foot]], [[especially]] {{lb|la|historical}} the [[Roman foot]].
## {{lb|la|poetry}} a [[metrical foot]]: the [[basic]] [[unit]] of [[meter]]ed [[poetry]]
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Tristia|1|15-16|quote=vāde, liber, verbīsque meīs loca grāta salūtā:<br/>contingam certē quō licet illā '''pede'''!|trans=''Go, [my] book, and greet with my words [those] beloved places: at least I shall reach [them] with the ‘'''foot'''’ that is allowed!''<br/>(The exiled poet puns that the metrical “feet” of his poem shall go where his own “feet” cannot.)}}
## {{lb|la|geography}} the [[base]] of a [[mountain]]
## {{lb|la|furniture}} the [[bottom]] of a [[leg]] of a [[table]], [[chair]], [[stool]], etc.
# {{lb|la|figuratively}} a [[place]] to [[tread]] one's [[foot]]: [[territory]], [[ground]], [[soil]]
# {{lb|la|nautical}} a [[rope]] [[attach]]ed to a [[sail]] [[in order to]] [[set]]
# {{lb|la|music}} [[tempo]], [[pace]], [[time]]
# {{lb|la|botany}} the [[pedicel]] or [[stalk]] of a [[fruit]]

====Declension====
{{la-ndecl|pēs/ped<3>}}

====Hyponyms====
* {{sense|metrical foot}} {{l|la|trochaeus}}; pes dissyllbus or disyllbus, pes bibrevis, {{l|la|choreus}}, {{l|la|jambus}}, {{l|la|spondeus}}, {{l|la|spondius}}, {{l|la|spondeos}} {{qualifier|2-syllable feet}}; pes trisyllabus, {{l|la|amphibrachus}}, {{l|la|amphibrachys}}, {{l|la|amphimacrus}}, {{l|la|dactylus}}, {{l|la|extensipes}}, {{l|la|molossus}}, pes anapaestus, pes antanapaestus, pes antibacchius, pes bacchius, pes creticus, pes hippius {{qualifier|3-syllable feet}}; pes tetrasyllbus, {{l|la|antispastus}}, {{l|la|chorjambus}}, {{l|la|dichoreus}}, {{l|la|dijambus}}, {{l|la|dispondeus}}, {{l|la|epitritus}}, {{l|la|paeon}}, {{l|la|proceleumaticus}}, {{l|la|proceleusmaticus}} {{qualifier|4-syllable feet}}; pes pentasyllbus, {{l|la|dochmius}}, {{l|la|mesobrachys}}, {{l|la|mesomacros}}, {{l|la|pariambodes}}, probrachys, pes amoebaeus, pes antamoebaeus, pes orthius {{qualifier|5-syllable feet}}

====Meronyms====
