==English==
{{wikipedia|Tempe, Arizona}}

===Etymology===
Named for the [[Vale of Tempe]] in northern [[Thessaly]], [[Greece]]

===Pronunciation===
{{rfp|en}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{place|en|city|s/Arizona}}.
# The [[Vale of Tempe]]; {{lb|en|by extension}} a [[beautiful]] [[valley]]; any [[delightful]] [[rural]] spot.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Thomas Nashe|chapter=The Terrors of the Night. Or A Discourse of Apparitions.|editor=[[w:Alexander Balloch Grosart|Alexander B[alloch] Grosart]]|title=The Complete Works of Thomas Nashe.{{nb...|In Four Volumes. For the First Time Collected and Edited with Memorial-Introduction, Notes and Illustrations, etc.}}|series=The Huth Library{{nb...|or Elizabethan-Jacobean Unique or Very Rare Books in Verse and Prose Largely from the Library of Henry Huth Esqʳ.}}|volume=III ({{nb...|Haue with You to Saffron-Walden. Terrors of the Night. 1594—96.|nospace=1}})|publisher={{nb...|Printed for private circulation only|nospace=1}}|year=1594|year_published=1883–84|pages=263–264|passage=Farre vnworhie am I to ſpend the leaſt breath of commendation in the extolling ſo delightfull and pleaſant a '''''Tempe''''', or once to conſecrate my inke with the excellent mention of the thrice noble and illuſtrious Chieftaine vnder whom it is flouriſhingly gouerned.}}
#* {{RQ:Estienne Liebault Maison Rustique|chapter=Reader|page=18|passage=And finally, ſeeing that the whole earth was once a '''Tempe''', an Eden (that is, a place of all pleaſures and delights) and the aſsigned poſſeſsion and naturall inheritance of man and woman to labour and liue in with exceeding great ioy and felicity; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Drayton Poly-Olbion|chapter=To the Generall Reader|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=_VliAAAAcAAJ&pg=PP19|passage={{...}} refuſing to walke forth into the '''''Tempe''''' and Feelds of the Muſes, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Horace Walpole|chapter=Letter CCLVIII|title=Letters from the Hon. Horace Walpole, to George Montagu, Esq. from the Year 1736, to the Year 1770.{{nb...|Now First Published from the Originals, in the Possession of the Editor.}}|series=The Works of Horatio Walpole, Earl of Orford|seriesvolume=VI|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} Rodwell and Martin,{{nb...|New Bond Street}}, and {{w|Henry Colburn}},{{nb...|Conduit Street}}|date=7 July 1770|year_published=1818|page=442|passage=I am rejoiced to be once more in the gay solitude of my own little '''Tempe'''.}}

====Derived terms====
