[[File:Sabile abava 2006 06.jpg|thumb|250px|Upe]]

===Etymology===
In principle, from {{cog|ine-bsl-pro|*apē-}}, from {{der|lv|ine-pro|*h₂ep-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*h₂op-||(flowing) water, river}}. Cognates include {{cog|lt|ùpė}}, dialectal {{m|lt|upė|ũpė}}, {{m|lt|upìs}}, {{m|lt|ùpis}}, {{cog|prg|ape||river}}, {{m|prg|apus||(water) well}}, {{cog|xsv|upa||river}}, {{cog|hit|-}} {{m|lv|ẖap(a)||river}}, {{cog|sa|अप्|आपः|water (plural)}}, {{cog|xto|-}}, {{cog|txb|-}} {{m|xto|āp||water}}. The ''u'' in Latvian {{m|lv|upe}} (and Lithuanian {{m|lt|upė}}), instead of the expected ''a'', as in the {{cog|prg|-}} cognate, is difficult to explain. Some suggest that {{m|lv|upe}} was influenced by other words (e.g., {{cog|prg|wupyan||cloud}}), others that it is not cognate with {{cog|prg|-}} {{m|lv|ape}}, but rather a reflex of Proto-Indo-European {{m|ine-pro|*eup-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*ūp-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*up-||to call, to cry, to yell}}, or then a specific Eastern Baltic word without a Proto-Indo-European etymology.<ref>{{R:lv:LEV}}</ref>

===Noun===
{{lv-noun|f|5th}}

# {{lb|lv|geography}} [[river]] {{gloss|[[permanent]] [[watercourse]]}}
#: {{ux|lv|strauja, lēna '''upe'''|fast, slow '''river'''|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|lv|kalnu, līdzenumu '''upe'''|mountain, lowland '''river'''|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|lv|'''upes''' gultne|'''river'''bed|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|lv|'''upes''' ieleja|'''river''' valley|inline=1}}
