Page 2993 aah: -------- begin text --------
{{also/aah|åh}}
==Translingual==

===Symbol===
{{mul-symbol}}

# {{ISO 639|3|Abuʼ Arapesh language|Abuʼ Arapesh}}

==English==
{{was wotd|2006|May|7}}

===Pronunciation===
As an interjection the word is pronounced basically the same way as the interjection [[ah]] but the double ''a'' stresses prolongation. In the noun and the verb there is no extra prolongation.
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-aah.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Interjection===
{{en-interj}}

# Indication of [[amazement]] or [[surprise]] or [[enthusiasm]].
#: '''''Aah!''' That's amazing!''
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Joan Morrison|title=Share House Blues|publisher=Boolarong Publications|year=1985|page=62|chapter=5|passage=''''Aaah'''!' they sigh, as the silvery Space Shuttle races heavenwards on top of a towering pillar of flame.}}
# Indication of [[joy]]ful [[pleasure]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1834|author=w:Edgar Allan Poe|title=s:The Assignation
|passage=Yet I remember—'''aah!''' how should I forget?}}
# Indication of sympathy.
#: '''''Aah''', I feel so bad for you...''
# Indication of mouth being opened wide.
#: ''Dentists would always instruct, say '''aah!'''''
# To express understanding.
#: '''''Aah'''. Now I understand.''
# The sound of one screaming (with as many a's or h's as needed for emphasis).
#: '''''AAAHHH'''! A bug! A bug! Get it off me! Get it off me!''
#: '''''Aah'''! A rat!''

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|say aah}}

====Translations====
{{trans-top|expressing amazement or surprise}}
* Armenian: {{t+|hy|ուխ|sc=Armn}}, {{t+|hy|ահ|sc=Armn}}
* Chinese:
*: Mandarin: {{t+|cmn|啊|tr=a}}, {{t+|cmn|哇|tr=wa}}, {{t+|cmn|呀|tr=ya}}
* Danish: {{t|da|ah}}, {{t|da|aha}}
* Dutch: {{t+|nl|och}}
* Estonian: {{t+|et|ah}}
* Georgian: {{t|ka|აჰ|sc=Geor}}
* Hungarian: {{t+|hu|á}}
* Kazakh: {{t|kk|аһ}}
* Latin: {{t|la|hui}}
* Lithuanian: {{t+|lt|ach}}
* Polish: {{t+|pl|ach}}
* Portuguese: {{t+|pt|nossa}}
* Russian: {{t+|ru|ах}}
* Serbo-Croatian:
*: Cyrillic: {{t|sh|о|sc=Cyrl}}
*: Roman: {{t+|sh|o|sc=Latn}}
* Shan: {{t+|shn|ဢႃ}}
* Spanish: {{t+|es|ay|alt=¡ay!}}, {{t+|es|ah|alt=¡ah!}}
* Swedish: {{t+|sv|åh}}, {{t+|sv|oh}}, {{t+|sv|ah}}
* Tuvan: {{t|tyv|аа}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Expression of [[amazement]], [[surprise]], [[enthusiasm]], or [[fear]].
# Expression of joy and/or pleasure.
# The exclamation aah.

====Translations====
{{trans-top|expression of amazement or surprise or enthusiasm}}
* Estonian: {{t+|et|ahhetus}}
* Polish: {{t+|pl|ach}}
{{trans-bottom}}

{{checktrans-top}}
* Telugu: {{t+check|te|ఆహా|sc=Telu}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive|informal}} To say or exclaim '''aah'''.
## To express amazement or surprise or enthusiasm, especially by the interjection '''aah'''.
##: {{ux|en|Everyone who came by oohed and '''aahed''' over her new appearance.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Kieran Kramer|chapter=7|title=When Harry Met Molly|publisher=[[w:St. Martin's Press|St. Martin’s]] Paperbacks|year=2010|page=66|isbn=978-0-312-61164-4|passage=In the next few minutes, Molly tossed dress after dress aside, oohing and '''aahing''' at the varied fabrics, the elaborate detailing of each one, until she found a dress that was— / Breathtaking.}}
## To express joy or pleasure, especially by the interjection '''aah'''.

====Usage notes====
* The object of feelings usually is indicated by the prepositions [[over]] or [[at]].
* Very often the word is used together with some other verb derived from an interjection. The most common combination is ''to [[ooh]] and aah''.

====Translations====
{{trans-top|to express amazement using "aah!"}}
* Estonian: {{t+|et|ahhetama}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===Anagrams===
* {{anagrams|en|a=aah|AHA|a-ha|aha}}

