#: {{alti|en|A.B.C.}}
# {{lb|en|aviation}} {{initialism of|en|{{w|Advance Booking Charter}}}}
# {{lb|en|historical|basketball}} {{initialism of|en|{{w|Asian Basketball Confederation}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=August 10, 2013 |work=Rappler |title=Looking back: When PH ruled Asian basketball |author=Gilas Pilipinas|volume=|number=|passage=The first was the Asian Games, held every 4 years, and the Asian Basketball Confederation ('''ABC'''), held every 2 years.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=January 15, 2017|work=Business Mirror |title=Hero of '67 ABC  |author=Ray Roquero |volume=|number=|passage=The most memorable championship of his life, the 1967 '''ABC''' (now Fiba Asia), was also the most dramatic for the Philippines in the entire 1960s.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=August 16, 2017|work=Asia Pacific Report |title=Australia, NZ slug it out for FIBA Asia Cup crown |author=|volume=|number=|passage=The now FIBA Asia Cup was previously called as the FIBA Asia Championships and the older Asian Basketball Confederation ('''ABC''') Championships. }}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=April 5, 2018|work=Sports Interactive Network Philippines |title=  |author=Gerry Ramos |volume=|number=|passage=Former PBA players pay final respects to Danny Florencio in his final journey home But Florencio would cement his place in local basketball history as the hero in the Philippines’ dramatic 83-80 win over South Korea for the 1967 Asian Basketball Confederation ('''ABC''') Championship (now known as the FIBA-Asia). }}
# {{lb|en|historical|bowling}} {{initialism of|en|{{w|American Bowling Congress}}}}
# {{lb|en|often|attributively}} {{initialism of|en|[[Argentina]], [[Brazil]] and [[Chile]]|dot=:}} the three most [[powerful]] and [[wealthy|wealthiest]] [[country|countries]] in [[South America]].
#: {{co|en|'''ABC''' countries}}
# {{initialism of|en|[[Santo André]], [[São Bernardo do Campo]] and [[São Caetano do Sul]]|dot=:}} [[satellite city|satellite cities]] around the city of [[São Paulo]] that form the most important [[industrial]] area in [[Brazil]].
# {{lb|en|US|rail}} {{initialism of|en|{{w|Atlanta, Birmingham and Coast Railroad}}}}
# {{initialism of|en||American Book Center|dot=:}} an English-language bookstore in [[Amsterdam]] founded in 1972.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=June 8 2009 |work=Irish Independent |title=10 best bookshops in the world |author=Derbhile Dromey|volume=|number=|passage=The American Book Center in Amsterdam is home to one of the largest collections of English literature in continental Europe. Its rickety shelves, spread over four floors, are crammed with American masterpieces from the likes of Hemmingway and Flannery O’Connor. If non-fiction is more your buzz, there are dozens of lavishly illustrated books on subjects such as art and design. '''ABC''' also runs The Treehouse, a series of events designed to nourish literary souls from the roots upwards. }}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=March 7, 2011|work=Boston Globe |title=Marblehead storyteller Judith Black goes international |author=Caitlin Rung|volume=|number=|passage=In addition to sharing American-style storytelling with audiences throughout Finland and France, including a featured presentation at the ELSA conference in Paris this month, Marblehead storyteller Judith Black will be helping to organize and host a story slam at '''ABC''' Treehouse, an institution that promotes and supports intercultural exploration in Amsterdam. }}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=January 26, 2014|work=Dutch News |title=From tea to e-readers: English bookshops in the Netherlands |author=Ana McGinley|volume=|number=|passage=The American Book Center – a part of Amsterdam's book scene for 41 years – has embraced the latest in book technology. Lynn Kaplanian Buller has been involved with the '''ABC''' almost since its launch in 1972, adding a second store in The Hague in 1976.}}
# {{lb|en|computer language}} An imperative general-purpose [[programming language]], intended for teaching or prototyping.
# {{lb|en|Christianity}} {{initialism of|en||[[Anglican]] [[book|Book]] [[centre|Centre]]|dot=:}} the [[publishing house]] and [[bookshop]] of the {{w|Anglican Church of Canada}} until 2013.

