Page 3188 دربند: -------- begin text --------
==Ottoman Turkish==

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|ota|fa|دربند|tr=darband|t=mountain or road pass, narrows}}, which is from {{compound|fa|در|tr1=dar|t1=door|بند|tr2=band|t2=band, knot, tie}} (''i.e. literally'': "barred gate").

===Noun===
{{head|ota|noun|tr=derbent, dervent}}

# {{lb|ota|geography}} [[narrows]], [[pass]]; a narrow place where one may cross when travelling
#: {{syn|ota|گچید|tr1=[[geçit]]}}
# [[derbend]], a small [[fort]] or [[outpost]] at a remote, usually mountainous location

====Usage notes====
The pronunciation ''derbent'' is more formal, whereas ''dervent'' was used in colloquial speech and thence passed to most Balkan languages, according to Georgiev mostly through {{m+|el|δερβένι}} to account for the loss of the final ''-t'' in {{m+|bg|дерве́н}}, {{m+|mk|дервен}}, {{m+|sh|дѐрвен}}.

====Derived terms====
* {{l|ota|دربندجی|tr=derbentçi, derventçi|t=[[derbendci]], irregular soldier or custom-house officer stationed at a pass}}

====Descendants====
* {{desc|tr|derbent|pos=n}} {{q|obsolete}}
** {{desc|tr|derbentçi|der=1|pos=n}} {{q|historical}}
* {{desc|el|δερβένι|bor=1|g=n}}
** {{desc|el|δερβενάκι|der=1|g=n}} {{q|rare}}, {{l|el|δερβέναγας|g=m}}
** {{desc|bor=1|bg|дерве́н|g=m|дерве́нт|g2=m}} {{q|obsolete}}
*** {{desc|bg|дервентджия|der=1|g=m}} {{q|historical}}
** {{desc|mk|дервен|bor=1|g=m}}
