=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|octochamp}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|champen}}, {{m|enm|chammen|t=to bite; gnash the teeth}}, perhaps originally {{onomatopoeic|en|title=imitative}}.

[[File:Champ (food).JPG|right|thumb|200px|champ (etymology 2, noun)]]

====Pronunciation====
* {{a|US|UK|GenAm|RP}} {{IPA|en|/t͡ʃæmp/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-champ.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{rhymes|en|æmp|s=1}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# {{lb|en|Ireland}} A meal of [[mashed potatoes]] and [[scallion]]s.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[bite]] or [[chew]], especially noisily or impatiently.
#* {{RQ:Hooker Laws
|passage=They began {{...}} irefully to '''champ''' upon the bit.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Fables|chapter=Palamon and Arcite|passage=Foamed and '''champed''' the golden bit.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1938|author=w:Xavier Herbert|title=[[w:Capricornia (novel)|Capricornia]]|location=New York|publisher=D. Appleton-Century|year_published=1943|chapter=XII|page=200|url=https://archive.org/details/capricornianovel00herb
|passage=He was mad, reeling about and gesticulating at the rushing train, and '''champing''' and gurgling like a lunatic.}}
#* '''1951''', {{w|Isaac Asimov}}, ''[[w:Foundation (novel)|Foundation]]'' (1974 [[w:Panther Books|Panther Books Ltd]] publication), part V: “The Merchant Princes”, chapter 13, page 166, ¶ 18
#*: The man beside him placed a cigar between Mallow’s teeth and lit it. He '''champed''' on one of his own and said, “You must be overworked. Maybe you need a long rest.”

=====Derived terms=====
* [[champ up]]

=====Translations=====
{{trans-top|to bite or chew}}
