From {{m|en|champagne}} by shortening.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/ʃæmp/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-champ2.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{lb|en|informal}} {{1|champagne}}.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Ann Heller|title=Prom Nights Often Offer Students Primer On Fine Dining|journal=Dayton Daily News|date=6 April 1990
|passage="They're dressed up very elegantly and it's nice they have a glass of '''champ''', even if it's non-alcoholic," Reif says.}}
#* '''2009''', [[w:The Lonely Island|The Lonely Island]] (featuring [[w:T-Pain|T-Pain]]), "[[w:I'm on a Boat|I'm on a Boat]]", ''[[w:Incredibad|Incredibad]]'':
#*: We're drinkin' Santana '''champ''', 'cause it's so crisp
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Tara Palmer-Tomkinson|title=Inheritance|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=GnP5Yvrmu-YC&pg=PT175|publisher=Pan Books|year_published=2010|isbn=9780330513265
|passage='Glass of '''champ'''?' she called, skipping into the kitchen.}}

===Etymology 4===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|fr|champ||field}}. {{doublet|en|campus|camp}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|champe}} {{qualifier|obsolete?}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|architecture|obsolete or rare}} The [[field]] or [[ground]] on which [[carving]] appears in [[relief]].
# {{lb|en|heraldry|obsolete or rare}} The field of a shield.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1914|author=John Horne Stevenson|title=Heraldry in Scotland|page=30
|text=If a man, he adds, have taken for his arms 'a low of gules in a '''champ''' of silver,'<sup>1</sup> {{...}}<br/><sup>1</sup>A flame (pile wavy) gules in a silver field. Thus the arms of the family of Bataille de Mandelot are, Argent three flames, per piles wavy gules, issuant from the base. Woodward, ''Heraldry'', i. 158. Otherwise one might almost suppose that the word 'low' of the MS. was a misprint or a misunderstanding of the scribe for 'cow'; for the instance in one MS. of the original French is that of a man who took 'une vache de geules et trois estoiles par dessus.'}}

===Etymology 5===
{{blend|en|church|camp}} or back-formation from ''[[champing]]''.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To camp overnight in a historic church as a novelty or part of a holiday.

=====Related terms=====
* [[glamp]]

