Page 3463 snoot: -------- begin text --------
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|sco|snoot}}, {{m|sco|snout|t=snout}}, from {{der|en|enm|snowte}}, from {{der|en|dum|snute}}; ultimately from {{der|en|gmw-pro|*snūt}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*snūtaz}}. {{doublet|en|snout}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/snuːt/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-snoot.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{rhymes|en|uːt|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|informal}} An [[elitist]] individual; one who looks down upon lower social classes.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1943|author=Lucius Morris Beebe|title=Snoot if You Must|publisher=D. Appleton-Century Company, Incorporated |url=https://books.google.co.nz/books?id=Ks8EAQAAIAAJ|page=44|text=The sidecars— sneer if you will, you purists and gastronomic '''snoots'''— at Perino's in Wilshire in Los Angeles.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=29 October 2013|author=Moosewood Collective |title=Sundays at Moosewood Restaurant: Ethnic and Regional Recipes from the Cooks at the Legendary Restaurant|publisher=w:Simon & Schuster  |url= https://books.google.co.nz/books?id=jZKjAQAAQBAJ |page=96|isbn=9781439147955 |text= In defense of low-grade teas, I must say they are very cheap, and I have a large box in my cupboard right next to the higher quality, more '''snoot'''-worthy varieties.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=31 January 2014|author=Robert B Parker|title=Perish Twice|publisher= Oldcastle Books |url= https://books.google.co.nz/books?id=RXEgBzA43d8C |page=73|isbn=9781842434932 |text=“Do you know any of Larry's other partners?” “Some.” “Do you know Mary Lou Goddard?” “Oh, that '''snoot'''.” “'''Snoot'''?” “Yes, Larry told me about her. He went out with her a couple of times and then she got possessive.”}}
# A language [[pedant]] or [[snob]]; one who practices [[linguistic]] [[elitism]]. {{rfex|en}}
# {{lb|en|dialectal|or|slang}} A [[nose]] or [[snout]], especially in [[derogatory]] use.
#* {{quote-book|en|date=31 February 2013|origyear=1966|author= Friedrich Reck|translator=Paul Rubens|title=Diary of a Man in Despair|publisher=w:New York Review of Books |url= https://books.google.co.nz/books?id=EUrXGJVdZEcC |page=173|isbn=9781842434932 |text= And then he did something which must be unprecedented in jurisprudence. He leaped from his chair, ran over to the old man, and shaking his fist under his nose, roared: 'Listen, you! If you keep on with this stuff, I'll punch you one in the '''snoot'''!}}
# {{lb|en|internet slang|childish|jocular}} [[snout|Snout]]; especially of a [[dog]] ("[[doggo]]"), [[cat]] ("[[catto]]"), or [[snake]] ("[[snek]]").
# {{lb|en|theater|photography}} A cylindrical or conical attachment used on a [[spotlight]] to restrict spill light.
#* {{quote-book|en|date=26 December 2014|author= Alyn Stafford |title=Flash Techniques for Location Portraiture: Single and Multiple-Flash Lighting Techniques|publisher= Amherst Media |url= https://books.google.co.nz/books?id=RKIsBgAAQBAJ |page=36|isbn=9781608954681 |text= '''Snoots''' have traditionally been round in shape when attached to studio strobes, but with flash photography, they have taken on a more rectangular shape because the flash heads are rectangular.}}

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|nose}} See [[Thesaurus:nose]]

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|droop snoot}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|snootful}}
* {{l|en|snooty}}

====Translations====
{{trans-see|slang: human nose|schnozzle}}

{{trans-top|theater: cylindrical or conical attachment on a spotlight}}
