==English==
[[File:Magaliesberg08.jpg|thumb|Magaliesberg Range, Transvaal, South Africa]]

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|es|cuesta|t=slope}}. {{doublet|en|coast}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkwɛstə/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛstə|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|geomorphology}} A [[hill]] or [[ridge]] with a gentle [[slope]] on one side, and a steep slope on the other.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1965|author=Lawrence Martin|title=The Physical Geography of Wisconsin|publisher=Univ of Wisconsin Press|isbn=9780299034757|page={{gbooks|QB7qpOSeh6sC|217}}
|passage=At that point the '''cuesta''' is 10 miles wide but the hilltops have an eastward descent of only about 62 feet. In its general eastward slope the surface of the '''cuesta''' is exactly that of one made by weathering and stream erosion, acting upon a gently-dipping limestone bed in a region never glaciated.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1986|author=Gwen Schultz|title=Wisconsin's Foundations: A Review of the State's Geology and Its Influence on Geography and Human Activity|publisher=Univ of Wisconsin Press|isbn=9780299198749|page={{gbooks|NUVd8gSdy8AC|127}}
|passage=In eastern Wisconsin the '''cuesta''' is drift-covered and less noticeable; permanent settlement came later; lead and zinc mining was absent; and industrial development took a different course.}}

===Further reading===
* {{pedia}}

===Anagrams===
* {{anagrams|en|a=acestu|Escaut|acutes|causet|scutae}}

