Page 3642 look: -------- begin text --------
{{also|löök|Look}}
==English==
{{wikipedia|look|mul=looking}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|loken}}, {{m|enm|lokien}}, from {{inh|en|ang|lōcian}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*lōkōn}}. Further origin {{unk|en|title=unknown}}, no certain cognates outside Germanic.<ref>{{R:Philippa EWN 2009|look}}</ref> The English word, however, is cognate with {{cog|sco|luke}}, {{m|sco|luik}}, {{m|sco|leuk|t=to look, see}}, {{cog|fy|lôkje}}, {{m|fy|loaitsje|t=to look}}, {{cog|nl|loeken|t=to look}}, {{cog|nds-de|löken}}, {{cog|gsw|luege|t=to look}}, {{cog|de|lugen|t=to look}}, {{cog|yi|לוגן}}. Possibly related to {{m+|sa|लोक्|t=to see, behold}} (from {{m+|ine-pro|*lewk-|t=light}} in the sense of "[[illuminating]]" (cf. related word {{m|sa|रुच्}} "to shine, illuminate")).<ref> {{R:sa:MW|906}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|lo͝ok}}, {{IPA|en|/lʊk/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-look.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
** {{rhymes|en|ʊk|s=1}}
** {{homophones|en|luck}} {{a|most of Northern England}}
* {{a|some Northern Enɡlish dialects, esp. Bolton|Scotland|Northern Ireland}} {{IPA|en|/luːk/}}
** {{rhymes|en|uːk}}
** {{homophones|en|Luke}}
* {{a|Liverpool usually}} {{IPA|en|/luːx/}}
** {{rhymes|en|uːx}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[try]] to [[see]], to [[pay attention]] to with one’s [[eyes]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:look}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} {{non-gloss definition|As an intransitive verb, often with "at".}}
##: {{troponyms|en|glance|Thesaurus:stare}}
##: {{ux|en|They kept '''looking''' at me.}}
##: {{ux|en|Don’t '''look''' in the closet.}}
##* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=5|passage=Then came a maid with hand-bag and shawls, and after her a tall young lady.{{...}}She '''looked''' around expectantly, and recognizing Mrs. Cooke's maid{{...}}Miss Thorn greeted her with a smile which greatly prepossessed us in her favor.}}
##* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=10|passage=He '''looked''' round the poor room, at the distempered walls, and the bad engravings in meretricious frames, the crinkly paper and wax flowers on the chiffonier; and he thought of a room like Father Bryan's, with panelling, with cut glass, with tulips in silver pots, such a room as he had hoped to have for his own.}}
##* {{quote-song|en|year=1968|author=Ray Thomas|title=Legend of a Mind|artist=The Moody Blues|album=In Search of the Lost Chord|passage=Timothy Leary's dead. <br> No, no no no, he's outside, '''looking''' in.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|colloquial}} {{non-gloss definition|As a transitive verb, often in the {{glossary|imperative mood|imperative}}; chiefly takes {{glossary|relative clause}} as direct object.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1972|title=w:The Godfather|passage='''Look''' how they massacred my boy.}}
##: {{ux|en|'''Look''' what you did to him!}}
##: {{ux|en|'''Look''' who's back!}}
# {{senseid|en|appear}} To [[appear]], to [[seem]].
#: {{ux|en|It '''looks''' as if it’s going to rain soon.}}
#: {{ux|en|Our new boss '''looks''' to be a lot more friendly.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=c. 1701-03|author=w:Joseph Addison|url=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=HoUTAAAAQAAJ|title=Remarks on Several Parts of Italy, &amp;c.|publisher=Dedication
|passage=THERE is a pleaſure in owning obligations which it is a pleaſure to have received; but ſhould I publiſh any favours done me by your Lordſhip, I am afraid it would '''look''' more like vanity, than gratitude.}}
#* {{RQ:Ferguson Zollenstein|IV
|passage=So this was my future home, I thought!{{...}}Backed by towering hills, the but faintly discernible purple line of the French boundary off to the southwest, a sky of palest Gobelin flecked with fat, fleecy little clouds, it in truth '''looked''' a dear little city; the city of one's dreams.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1963|author=w:Margery Allingham|title=w:The China Governess
|chapter=2|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL2004261W
|passage=Now that she had rested and had fed from the luncheon tray Mrs. Broome had just removed, she had reverted to her normal gaiety.&nbsp; She '''looked''' cool in a grey tailored cotton dress with a terracotta scarf and shoes and her hair a black silk helmet.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2012|title=Chelsea 6-0 Wolves|url=http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/19632463
|passage=Chelsea's youngsters, who '''looked''' lively throughout, then combined for the second goal in the seventh minute. Romeu's shot was saved by Wolves goalkeeper Dorus De Vries but Piazon kept the ball alive and turned it back for an unmarked Bertrand to blast home.}}
# {{lb|en|copulative}} To give an appearance of being.
#: {{ux|en|That painting '''looks''' nice.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1843|author=w:Thomas Carlyle|title=[[:w:Past and Present (book)|Past and Present]]|section=book 2, chapter 6, ''Monk Samson''|passage=Once, slipping the money clandestinely, just in the act of taking leave, he slipt it not into her hand but on the floor, and another had it; whereupon the poor Monk, coming to know it, '''looked''' mere despair for some days{{nb...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|often|with "for"}} To [[search]] for, to [[try]] to [[find]].
# To face or present a view.
#: {{ux|en|The hotel '''looks''' over the valleys of the HinduKush.}}
#* '''1769''', {{w|Benjamin Blayney}} (editor), ''{{w|King James Version|King James Bible}}'', Oxford standard text, ''[[s:Bible (King James)/Ezekiel|Ezekiel]]'', xi, 1,
#*: Moreover the spirit lifted me up, and brought me unto the east gate of the LORD's house, which '''looketh''' eastward:
# To expect or anticipate.
#: {{ux|en|I '''look''' to each hour for my lover’s arrival.}}
#* '''1596''', {{w|Edmund Spenser}}, ''{{w|The Fairie Queene}}'', Book VI, Canto XI, '''1750''', ''The Works of Edmund Spenser'', Volume 4, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=PyxMAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA139&dq=%22looking+each+hour+into+death%27s+mouth+to+fall%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwjTjumR2vLZAhVSOZAKHTDnBAMQ6AEIJzAA#v=onepage&q=%22looking%20each%20hour%20into%20death's%20mouth%20to%20fall%22&f=false page 139],
#* {{RQ:Noire Thug-A-Licious|page=108|passage="Ain't gone be no Rikers Island for you next time," I warned him. "You get tapped on another gun charge and you '''looking''' at some upstate time."<br>'''Looking''' each Hour into Death's Mouth to fall,}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-1|1|1|passage=Gloster, what ere we like,thou art Protector,<br>And '''lookest''' to command the Prince and Realme.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[express]] or [[manifest]] by a look.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=c. 1815|author=w:Lord Byron|title=Waterloo
|passage=Soft eyes '''looked''' love to eyes which spake again,}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|often|with "to"}} To make sure of, to see to.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1898|translator=w:Samuel Butler|author=w:Homer|title=[[s:The Odyssey (Butler)/Book XXIII|The Odyssey]]
|passage="'''Look''' to it yourself, father," answered Telemachus, "for they say you are the wisest counsellor in the world, and that there is no other mortal man who can compare with you.{{nb...}}}}
# {{lb|en|dated|sometimes|figurative}} To show oneself in looking.
#: {{ux|en|'''Look''' out of the window [i.e. lean out] while I speak to you.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Taming of the Shrew|Induction|2|text=I have{{...}}more feet than shoes, or such shoes as my toes '''look''' through the overleather.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic|_|or|_|dialectal}} To [[check#Verb|check]], to [[make sure#Verb|make sure]] (of something).
#* {{RQ:Swift Gulliver|part=II|chapter=I|page=151|passage=Finding it was like to overblow, we took in our Sprit-ſail, and ſtood by to hand the Fore-ſail; but making foul Weather, we '''look'd''' the Guns were all faſt, and handed the Miſſen.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To look at; to turn the eyes toward.
#* {{RQ:Hough Purchase Price|chapter=I|text=Serene, smiling, enigmatic, she faced him with no fear whatever showing in her dark eyes.{{...}}She put back a truant curl from her forehead where it had sought egress to the world, and '''looked''' him full in the face now,{{nb...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[seek]]; to search for.
#* {{circa|1552–1599}}, {{w|Edmund Spenser}}, unidentified sonnet,
#*: '''Looking''' my love, I go from place to place, <br> Like a young fawn that late hath lost the hind; <br> And seek each where, where last I saw her face, <br> Whose image yet I carry fresh in mind.
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To influence, overawe, or subdue by looks or presence.
#: {{ux|en|to '''look''' down opposition}}
#* '''1692''', {{w|John Dryden}}, ''Cleomenes the Spartan Hero, a Tragedy'', Act 3, Scene 1, '''1701''', ''The Comedies, Tragedies, and Operas Written by John Dryden, Esq'', Volume 2, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=zehWAAAAcAAJ&pg=RA1-PA464&dq=%22A+spirit+fit+to+start+into+an+empire,+And+look+the+world+to+law.%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiwjsH0nvPZAhWHqJQKHacPACsQ6AEITTAH#v=onepage&q=%22A%20spirit%20fit%20to%20start%20into%20an%20empire%2C%20And%20look%20the%20world%20to%20law.%22&f=false page 464],
#*: A Spirit fit to start into an Empire, <br> And '''look''' the World to Law.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1882|author=w:Wilkie Collins|title=Heart and Science
|passage=Ovid might have evaded her entreaties by means of an excuse. But her eyes were irresistible: they '''looked''' him into submission in an instant.}}
# {{senseid|en|baseball}}{{lb|en|baseball}} To [[look at]] a pitch as a batter without swinging at it.
#: {{ux|en|The fastball caught him '''looking.'''}}
#: {{ux|en|Clem Labine struck Mays out '''looking''' at his last at bat.}}
#: {{ux|en|It's unusual for Mays to strike out '''looking'''. He usually takes a cut at it.}}

====Usage notes====
Though the use of the pronunciation {{IPAchar|/luːk/}} is now restricted to northern English dialects, it was formerly more widespread. For example, it is mentioned without comment in {{w|John Walker (lexicographer)|Walker's}} ''Critical Pronouncing Dictionary''.<ref>{{R:Critical Pronouncing Dictionary|Look|350|2|ppage=1}}</ref>

